1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==John Woodville==
2 CONT '''Sir John Woodville (c.1445 – 12 August 1469) was the second son, and fourth child''', of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta of Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1465, Woodville's sister, Queen Elizabeth, procured '''his marriage to Catherine Neville, Dowager Duchess of Norfolk''' (born c. 1400 – died after 1483), who was aunt to the powerful Richard Neville, Earl of Warwick.[1]
2 CONC  '''As the Duchess was about 65 years of age at the time and Woodville was only 19, the marriage was seen by all, particularly Warwick, as an indecent grasp for money and power by the Woodville family. One chronicler described it 
2 CONC as a "maritagium diabolicum" (the diabolical marriage).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1465, John was created a Knight of the Bath by his sister's husband King Edward IV.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1469, Woodville and his father accompanied King Edward on a march north, to put down what was thought to be a minor rebellion supporting Edward's brother the Duke of Clarence as the legitimate king. Before they met the rebel
2 CONC s both Clarence and Warwick had announced their support for the rebellion; by the time the King met the rebels, the rebel force was far stronger than his. In a parley, the rebels told the King that they had no fight with him but a
2 CONC dvised him to distance himself from the Woodvilles. In no position to argue, the King sent the Woodville party away.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Woodville and Rivers went first to the Rivers' house at Grafton and from there made their way westwards towards Wales. They were captured by Warwick's men on the western bank of the Severn and taken to Coventry in Warwickshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Before leaving Calais to support the uprising, Warwick had published a manifesto citing the Woodvilles in general, and the Earl and John Woodville specifically, as his reason for supporting Clarence against the King. The public
2 CONC ation of this manifesto was deemed, by Warwick, to justify the execution of Rivers and his son. They were beheaded on 12 August and their heads placed on spikes above the gates of Coventry. Woodville died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Woodville
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://1henry6.wikidot.com/woodville Woodville - the 1st part of Henry Vl]
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Hence in 1469 Earl Rivers was among the royal favourites whom Warwick, Archbishop Neville, and Clarence determined to destroy as the essential preliminary to their recovery of power. They supported a manifesto denouncing evil go
2 CONC vernment and the king's upstart favourites supposedly issued by the Yorkshire rebel Robin of Redesdale, whose defeat of Pembroke's army at Edgcote (27 July) led to Warwick's capture of the king and hence of the government. Rivers 
2 CONC was not at Edgcote, but he and his son '''Sir John Woodville''' were seized at Chepstow, taken to Kenilworth, and executed there on 12 August 1469. Charges of sorcery against the Duchess Jacquetta were withdrawn after Edward IV's 
2 CONC recovery of power."
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir John Wydeville, Master of the Queen's Horse1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8
2 CONT *'''M, #44680, b. circa 1445, d. 12 August 1469
2 CONT *Father	Sir Richard Wydeville, 1st Earl Rivers, Constable of England, Lord High Treasurer1,2,3,5,6,9 b. b 1408, d. 12 Aug 1469
2 CONT *Mother	Jacquetta (Jacqueline) of Luxembourg1,2,3,5,6,9 b. 1416, d. 30 May 1472
2 CONT *'''     Sir John Wydeville, Master of the Queen's Horse was born circa 1445 at of Grafton Regis, Northampshire, England; Age 20 in 1465.8 He married Katharine Neville''', daughter of Sir Ralph Neville, 1st Earl Westmoreland, 4th 
2 CONC Baron Neville and Joan Beaufort, '''in January 1465; No issue.1,10,11,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 Sir John Wydeville, Master of the Queen's Horse died on 12 August 1469 at Coventry, Warwickshire, England; Beheaded immediately after the Battle o
2 CONC f Edgecote, without trial.1,10,2,3,5,6,8
2 CONT *'''Family     Katharine Neville d. bt Jul 1483 - Mar 1484
2 CONT *Citations
