1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Until there is more definite information this profile is for ELIZABETH GREY who married GERALD FITZGERALD, 9th EARL OF KILDARE. 
2 CONT
2 CONT THERE IS A ISSUE OF WHETHER IT IS ELIZABETH GREY THAT MARRIED BOTH, Gerald FitzGerald, 9th Earl of Kildare AND Sir John Arundell of Lanherne OR IF ELEANOR GREY WAS THE WIFE OF Sir John Arundell of Lanherne. EACH HAS SOURCES LISTED
2 CONC , THE SOURCES LISTED AT PEERAGE, I HAVE NOT BEEN ABLE TO VIEW TO VERIFY.
2 CONT
2 CONT *<nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT From the '''Dictionary of national biography (1885)''', which supports her marriage to Gerald Fitzgerald:
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati19stepuoft#page/120/mode/2up/search/FitzGerald
2 CONT *Pg. 119-120
2 CONT *'''In 1520, (Gerald Fitzgerald, ninth earl of) Kildare married (secondly, after first wife Elizabeth, daughter of John Zouche of Codnor in Derbyshire, died at Lucan in October 1517, and was buried at the Monastery of the Friars O
2 CONC bservant at Kilcullen, County Kildare) the Lady Elizabeth Grey, fourth daughter of Thomas, marquis of Dorset, Granddaughter of Elizabeth Woodville, queen of Edward IV and first cousin of Henry VIII. 
2 CONT *___________
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth Grey, Countess of Kildare (c.1497- after 1548), was an English noblewoman, and the second wife of Irish peer Gerald FitzGerald, 9th Earl of Kildare. Her father was Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset. .... etc.
2 CONT *'''Lady Elizabeth Grey was born in about 1497, a daughter of Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset, and Cecily Bonville, Baroness Harington and Bonville, one of the wealthiest heiresses in England in the latter half of the 15th-cen
2 CONC tury. Elizabeth's paternal grandmother was Elizabeth Woodville, Queen consort of King Edward IV of England.
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth had 13 siblings, including her eldest brother Thomas Grey, 2nd Marquess of Dorset, who succeeded their father when he died in September 1501, when she was about four years old. Two years later, their mother, Cecily m
2 CONC arried Henry Stafford, 1st Earl of Wiltshire, which caused many quarrels over their inheritance. On one occasion, Cardinal Thomas Wolsey was forced to intervene on behalf of King Henry VIII of England, and he ordered both Cecily a
2 CONC nd Thomas to contribute to the dowries of Elizabeth and her three surviving sisters.[1]
2 CONT *'''She was appointed one of the Maids of Honour to Princess Mary Tudor in 1514, and accompanied her to France when the princess set out to marry King Louis XII. She remained at the French court when Queen Mary's other English lad
2 CONC ies were sent home, and stayed on to serve Mary's successor, Queen Claude, consort of the new King Francis I, in the same capacity. Elizabeth's fellow English Maids of Honour, who also were allowed to remain behind in Queen Claude
2 CONC 's household, were Anne Boleyn, and Mary Boleyn.[2]
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth was one of Queen Catherine of Aragon's attendants at the Field of the Cloth of Gold in 1520.
2 CONT *'''She married Gerald "Gearóid Og" FitzGerald, 9th Earl of Kildare in London in about 1522. His first wife, Elizabeth Zouche had died, leaving him a son, Thomas, and three daughters. By his marriage to Elizabeth, who was Henry VI
2 CONC II's cousin, Gerald gained much influence at court.[3] Elizabeth was styled as the Countess of Kildare. The match, while advantageous to Gerald, was also partially based on the physical attraction the couple had for one another. H
2 CONC istorian Mary Anne Everett Green described Gerald as having been quite handsome in appearance, and he in turn was pleased by Elizabeth.[4] He had been a kindly husband to his first wife, and his second marriage was also happy.[5] 
2 CONC According to historian Barbara Jean Harris, Elizabeth married Gerald against her father's will; however in 1527 her mother forgave her by granting Elizabeth a dowry of £1000. She added the following as means of explanation for the
2 CONC  money: "forasmuch as the said marriage is honourable and I and all her friends have cause to be content with the same".[6]
2 CONT *'''In 1523, Elizabeth returned with her husband to Ireland, where he served as Lord Deputy of Ireland (1524–1525, 1532–1534), and as Deputy to the King's Lieutenant of Ireland (1533).[7] Extant letters she wrote home to England, 
2 CONC show that Elizabeth had taken a keen interest in the Irish political situation.[8]
2 CONT *'''Together Gerald and Elizabeth had at least six children:
