2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2892.htm#i86885 ___________________
2 CONT *'''John Sutton, 3rd Baron Dudley (1494–1553), commonly known as Lord Quondam, was the eldest son and heir of Sir Edward Sutton, 2nd Baron Dudley and his wife Lady Cicely (Willoughby) Sutton, a descendant of Edward III of England.
2 CONT *'''He was born about 1494, at Dudley Castle, Worcestershire. He was betrothed to Lady Cecily Grey (a daughter of Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset, by Cecily, his wife, suo jure Baroness Harington and Baroness Bonville) by 30 O
2 CONC ctober 1501, whom he subsequently married. He was knighted 13 October 1513. He succeeded his father Edward Sutton, 2nd Baron Dudley as Baron Dudley in 1532 and immediately began to sell his patrimony, including half of Powis Castl
2 CONC e. He was never summoned to Parliament. ....
2 CONT *''' 'Lord Quondam' had several brothers: Thomas, William, Arthur, Geoffery and George. Lord Dudley decided upon a city residence at Tothill Street in Westminster. He died in Middlesex and was buried 18 September 1553 in St Margar
2 CONC et's, Westminster, London; his wife was buried there on 28 April 1554.[2]
2 CONT *'''His children included Edward, Captain Henry (Knt.), George, Margaret, Thomas, Dorothy, Elizabeth, and Robert.
2 CONT *It is often stated that Thomas Dudley was a grandson of Captain Sir Henry Dudley. He sailed on the Arbella in 1630 to become governor of the Massachusetts Bay Colony several times. However the descent from the Barons Dudley is no
2 CONC t proven. According to Cotton Mather's history,[3] Gov. Dudley used a seal with the arms of the Sutton family on official documents and his will. Whatever the ambiguities associated with his paternal ancestry, however, both Dudley
2 CONC 's mother Susanna Thorne and his second wife Katherine Deighton were demonstrably of noble descent.[4][5]
2 CONT *Indeed, according to one theory,[6] the difficulty of proving Gov. Dudley's descent from Lord Quondam is compounded by reluctance during his lifetime to acknowledge any connection to that pauper and national laughing-stock. This 
2 CONC long-standing genealogical Gordian knot may soon be cut through DNA testing.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Sutton,_3rd_Baron_Dudley _____________________
2 CONT *'''Cecily GREY (B. Sutton of Dudley)
2 CONT *'''Born: ABT 1497, Denbigh, Denbighshire, Wales
2 CONT *'''Died: 28 Apr 1554
2 CONT *'''Buried: 28 Apr 1554, Westminster, Middlesex, England
2 CONT *'''Notes: In Feb 1537, she wrote to Thomas Cromwell, complaining of the poverty in which she and her husband were forced to live.
2 CONT *Father: Thomas GREY (1° M. Dorset)
2 CONT *Mother: Cecily BONVILLE (M. Dorset)
