2 CONT REMARKS:
2 CONT
2 CONT 278 Knight of the Garter - 1521
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT
2 CONT Born about 1498, son of William Courtenay, 9th Earl of Devon, and Catherine of York, daughter of Edward IV, King of England. In December 1512 he obtained a reversal of his father's attainder whereby he succeeded to the Earldom of 
2 CONC Devon. After June 1515, he married Elizabeth Grey, suo jure Baroness L'Isle, but she died not long afterwards; and, on 25 October 1519, he married Gertrude Blount. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1520 he attended King Henry VIII at the Field of Cloth of Gold. On 24 April 1521 he was nominted Knight of The Garter and was installed on 9 June. In April 1522 he was appointed Keeper of Burling Park; in May 1523 High Steward 
2 CONC of the Duchy of Cornwall and Warden of the Stannaries and, in 1525, Constable of Windsor Castle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 June 1525, he was created Marquess of Exeter and, on 19 February 1528, had livery of his mother's lands. On 17 May 1528 he was appointed Lieutenant of the Order of the Garter and, in June 1528, Seneschal of the Duchy of Corn
2 CONC wall. He supported King Henry VIII in his attempt to obtain a divorce, signing the letter to Pope Clement VII to that effect, being a Commissioner in 1533 for Queen Catherine's deposition, and was rewarded with the Stewardship of 
2 CONC numerous Abbeys and Priories in the West of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was on the trial of Queen Anne Boleyn; assisted in suppressing the rebellion called the Pilgrimage of Grace; and, on 15 May 1537, acted as High Steward at the trial of the Lrds Darcy and Hussey for their share therein. The meas
2 CONC ures of Cromwell, the Vicar General, became so obnoxious to him that he drifted into a treasonable conspiracy with the Pole family, endeavouring to raise the men of Devon and Cornwall, and being taken prisoner was, on 3 December 1
2 CONC 538, tried by his Peers in Westminster Hall. Found guilty, on 9 January 1539, he was beheaded on Tower Hill with Lord Montagu and Sir Edward Nevill. Having been attainted, the Earldom became forfeited.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources 	1. [S00058]   The Complete Peerage, 1936 , Doubleday, H.A. & Lord Howard de Walden, Reference: IV 330
2 CONT
