1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Elizabeth Neville1
2 CONT *'''F, #32962, b. circa 1491, d. circa 1533
2 CONT *Father	Sir George Neville, Lord Abergavenny, Constable of Dover Castle, Warden of the Cinque Ports1,2 b. c 1469
2 CONT *Mother	Joan FitzAlan1,2 b. c 1470, d. 14 Nov 1508
2 CONT *'''     Elizabeth Neville was born circa 1491. She married Sir Henry Daubeney, Earl Bridgwater''', son of Sir Giles Daubeney, Lord Daubeney and Elizabeth de Arundel, '''before 16 July 1517.1,2 Elizabeth Neville died circa 1533.1
2 CONT *'''Family     Sir Henry Daubeney, Earl Bridgwater b. Dec 1493, d. 12 Apr 1548
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. II, p. 311.
2 CONT *[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 95.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1098.htm#i32962 _____________
2 CONT *'''Lady Elizabeth Neville1
2 CONT *'''F, #1052, b. before 1495
2 CONT *Last Edited=18 Jan 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=1.01%
2 CONT *'''     Lady Elizabeth Neville was born before 1495.1 She was the daughter''' of Sir George Neville, 3rd Lord Abergavenny and Lady Joan FitzAlan.1 '''She married Henry Daubeney, 1st and last Earl of Bridgwater''', son of Giles Da
2 CONC ubeney, 1st Baron Daubeney and Elizabeth Arundell, '''before 16 July 1517.1
2 CONT *'''      From before 16 July 1517, her married name became Daubeney.1
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S8] BP1999 volume 1, page 17. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S8]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p106.htm#i1052 _____________
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth NEVILLE
2 CONT *'''Born: ABT 1494, Arundel, Sussex, England
2 CONT *Father: George NEVILLE (3° B. Abergavenny)
2 CONT *Mother: Joan FITZALAN (B. Abergavenny)
2 CONT *'''Married: Henry DAUBENEY (1° E. Bridgewater) BEF 16 Jul 1517, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, England
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/NEVILLE4.htm#Elizabeth NEVILLE5 ____________
2 CONT *George Neville or Nevill, 5th and de jure 3rd Baron Bergavenny KG, PC (c.1469 – 1535/6) was an English courtier. He held the office of Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports.
2 CONT *George Neville was born in Abergavenny, Monmouthshire,[citation needed] the son of George Neville, 4th Baron Bergavenny, by his first wife, Margaret Fenne (d. 28 September 1485), daughter of Hugh Fenne, sub-treasurer of England.[
2 CONC 1] Margaret was born about 1444 at Scoulton Burdeleys in Norfolk.[citation needed]
2 CONT *His younger brother Sir Thomas Nevill was a trusted councillor of King Henry VIII and Speaker of the House of Commons. His youngest brother, the courtier Sir Edward Neville, was executed by Henry in 1538 for treason.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *Neville married firstly Joan FitzAlan (d. 14 November 1508), the daughter of Thomas FitzAlan, 17th Earl of Arundel, and Margaret Woodville (d. before 4 August 1492), second daughter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers. She was 
2 CONC a younger sister of Elizabeth Woodville, wife of Edward IV. According to Hawkyard, the marriage was childless; however according to Cokayne and Richardson, there were two daughters of the marriage:[6][5][7][8]
2 CONT **'''Elizabeth Neville, who married Henry Daubeney, 1st Earl of Bridgewater.[7]
2 CONT **Jane Neville, who married Henry Pole, 1st Baron Montagu, elder brother of Cardinal Reginald Pole, executed in 1539.[9]
2 CONT *He married secondly, before 5 September 1513, Margaret Brent, the daughter of John Brent of Charing, Kent,[10] and Anne Rosmoderes,[citation needed] by whom he had no issue.[11]
2 CONT *He married thirdly, about June 1519, Lady Mary Stafford, youngest daughter of Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham, by Lady Eleanor Percy, by whom he had three sons and five daughters:[12]
2 CONT **Henry Neville, 6th Baron Bergavenny.
2 CONT **John Neville.
2 CONT **Thomas Neville.
2 CONT **Mary Neville, who married firstly Thomas Fiennes, 9th Baron Dacre; secondly John Wotton; and thirdly Francis Thursby, esquire.
2 CONT **Katherine Neville, who married Sir John St. Leger.
2 CONT **Margaret Neville, who married firstly John Cheney, and secondly Henry Poole, esquire.
2 CONT **Dorothy Neville (d.1559), who married, as his first wife, William Brooke, 10th Baron Cobham, by whom she had a daughter, Frances Brooke, who married firstly Thomas Coppinger (1546–1580), and secondly Edward Becher.[13]
2 CONT **Ursula Neville, who married Sir Warham St Leger.
2 CONT *He married fourthly his former servant, Mary Brooke alias Cobham, by whom he had a daughter whose name is unknown.[10]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Nevill,_5th_Baron_Bergavenny _____________
2 CONT *'''Henry Daubeney, 1st Earl of Bridgewater, 2nd Baron Daubeney''' (December 1493 – 8 April 1548) was an English peer and member of the House of Lords.
2 CONT *He was the son and heir of Giles Daubeney, 1st Baron Daubeney (1451-1508), KG by his wife Elizabeth Arundell, the daughter of Sir John Arundell of Lanherne, Cornwall.
2 CONT *His father had intended Henry before his sixteenth birthday to marry one of the daughters of Sir John IV Basset (1462–1528), of Tehidy in Cornwall, and Whitechapel in Devon, and at some time before December 1504 for that eventual
2 CONC  purpose had taken into his household two of Basset's daughters, Anne Basset and Thomasine Basset, to give the 11-year-old Henry a choice of future bride.[1] However no such marriage took place, possibly due to his father's early 
2 CONC death four years later in 1508[2] and Henry's subsequent entry into the wardship of his mother Elizabeth, who at the same time obtained his marriage "without disparagement", apparently an escape clause from the contract.[3] In 151
2 CONC 1 Anne Basset married James Courtenay, so it appears the contract had been abandoned by that time.[4] The proposed Daubeney-Basset marriage was the result of Henry's father having invested heavily, in excess of 3,000 marks, to ena
2 CONC ble John Basset to redeem his substantial inheritance from the Beaumont family, comprising amongst others the Devonshire manors of Shirwell, Umberleigh and Heanton Punchardon. The redemption of these lands by Daubeney snr. was par
2 CONC t of the "great indenture" of 11 December 1504[5] made with Basset, which would require ownership of the lands to descend to the male issue of the marriage between Henry Daubeney and one of the Basset daughters. Even though he had
2 CONC  failed to meet his part of the bargain of marrying one of the Basset daughters, Henry spent considerable effort in later life trying to prevent the Basset family obtaining the reversion of these properties, as the indenture provi
2 CONC ded for. The dispute figures prominently in the Lisle Letters.[6] Indeed Henry tried to alienate the Beaumont lands to Edward Seymour,[7] the queen's brother, then trying to build up a Devon estate,[8] who was also a key influence
2 CONC  in obtaining Henry's earldom.[9]
2 CONT *In 1513 he served in the French war. In 1520 he was present at the Field of the Cloth of Gold, together with his first wife, and at the Calais interview of 1532.[10] He was created Earl of Bridgewater on 19 July 1538.
2 CONT *'''Henry married twice but left no progeny:
2 CONT *'''Firstly to Elizabeth Neville''', the daughter of George Neville, 5th Baron Bergavenny, by his first wife, Joan FitzAlan (d.1508), daughter of Thomas FitzAlan, 17th Earl of Arundel, and Margaret Woodville (d.1492), second daugh
2 CONC ter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers, and a younger sister of Elizabeth Woodville, wife of King Edward IV.[11][12][13]
2 CONT *Secondly he married Katherine Howard (d. 1554), daughter of Thomas Howard, 2nd Duke of Norfolk by his second wife Agnes Tilney (d. 1545). He had quarrelled with her by 1535 and sought a divorce.[14]
2 CONT *'''There were no issue of either marriage''', and at his death the barony of Daubeney and the earldom of Bridgewater became extinct.[15]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Daubeney,_1st_Earl_of_Bridgewater ___________________
