1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/144292758/anne_de-la_tour_d&#39;auvergne
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne, Countess of Auvergne
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse John Stewart, Duke of Albany 
2 CONT
2 CONT Noble family La Tour 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father John III, Count of Auvergne 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Jeanne de Bourbon-Vendome 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 1496 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 1524 (aged 28) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne de la Tour d'Auvergne was sovereign Countess of Auvergne from 1501 until 1524, and Duchess of Albany by marriage to John Stewart, Duke of Albany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the elder of the two daughters born to Jean III of la Tour d'Auvergne and Jeanne of Bourbon-Vendôme. Her younger sister was Madeleine of la Tour d'Auvergne, who would marry Lorenzo II de' Medici and become the mother of Ca
2 CONC therine de' Medici. As the elder daughter, Anne was her father's heiress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1505, she married her first cousin John Stewart, Duke of Albany, the intermittent heir presumptive to the Kingdom of Scotland and its sometime-regent, who lived in France as a sort of exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne died in 1524, leaving her inheritance (the feudal county of Auvergne) to her infant niece, Caterina de' Medici of Urbino (born 1519), daughter of her late younger sister Madeleine and Lorenzo II, Duke of Urbino. Catherine, th
2 CONC us countess of Auvergne, afterward married Henri, Duke of Orleans, and by his accession to the French throne as Henry II, became Queen Catherine of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT A manuscript detailing Anne's inheritance, with pictures of her castles, and her descent from the legendary Belle Moree survives in the Royal Library of the Hague.[1]
2 CONT
