1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Voir [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louise_de_Montpensier '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise de Bourbon, Duchess of Montpensier
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise de Bourbon, (1482- 5 July 1561), was the Duchess of Montpensier, suo jure from February 1538 to 1561.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Gilbert, Count of Montpensier, and Clara Gonzaga. Her paternal grandparents were Louis I, Count of Montpensier and Gabriele de La Tour d'Auvergne.[1] Her maternal grandprents were Federico I Gonzaga,
2 CONC  Marquess of Mantua and Margaret of Bavaria. Her five younger siblings included Charles III, Duke of Bourbon. He was killed in battle in May 1527 when he led the Imperial troops sent by Emperor Charles V against Pope Clement VII i
2 CONC n what became the Sack of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT By dint of her three brothers having died childless, Louise became the heiress to the county of Montpensier and the dauphinate of Auvergne. The estates, however, had been sequestered by the French King Francis I (at the instigatio
2 CONC n of his mother, Louise of Savoy) when her brother Charles, Duke of Bourbon and Constable of France formed an alliance with Charles V. After the Duke's death at the siege of Rome, his fiefs were confiscated by the King. On 17 May 
2 CONC 1530, she became the suo jure Duchess of Chatellerault, Countess of Forez, Baroness de Beaujeu, which had formed part of her brother Charles' inheritance, however the titles were revoked by the King in January 1532. Eventually som
2 CONC e of her late brother's inheritance was restored to her. In February 1538, Louise was invested as Duchess of Montpensier, Dauphine of Auvergne, Baroness de La Tour and de la Bussière by King Francis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1499, Louise married her first husband, Andre III de Chauvigny, prince of Deols and vicomte de Brosse. The marriage was childless. She married secondly on 21 March 1504, her cousin Louis de Bourbon, Prince of La Roche-sur-Yon, 
2 CONC by whom she had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Suzanne de Bourbon-Montpensier (1508- 1570), married Claude of Rieux, Count of Harcoute and Aumale, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis III de Bourbon, Duke of Montpensier, (10 June 1513- 23 September 1582) married firstly in 1538, Jacqueline de Longwy, Countess of Bar-sur-Seine (died 28 August 1561), by whom he had a son and five daughters, including Charlo
2 CONC tte of Bourbon. His second wife was Catherine of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles, prince of La Roche sur Yon (died 1565), married Philippa of Montespédon, Dame de Beaupreau (died 1575), by whom he had a son, Henri, and a daughter, Jeanne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise died on 5 July 1561. She was buried at Saint-Chapelle, Chateau de Champigny in Touraine. Her eldest son Louis succeeded her as Duke of Montpensier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her daughter Suzanne, Louise was an ancestress of King Louis XV of France. Through her granddaughter Charlotte of Bourbon, Louise was an ancestress of the House of Hanover, which reigned in Great Britain from 1714 to 1901,
2 CONC  and from which descends the current British Royal Family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ [http://www.genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00002102&tree=LEO "Louis I 'le Bon' de Bourbon Comte de Montpensier"] on Genealogics, citing as authorites: Europäische Stammtafeln, Band II, Frank Baron Freytag von Loringhove
2 CONC n, 1975, Isenburg, W. K. Prinz von, Reference: Page 30; Gens Nostra , Reference: yr 1966; Cahiers de Saint Louis , Dupont, Jacques and Saillot, Jacques, Reference: page 515.
