1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Rest of the Story:'''
2 CONT '''Is it Elder and Younger or Aunt and Niece?'''
2 CONT
2 CONT To be absolutely precise: biologically, they are Aunt and Niece. In historical and legal documentation, they are referred to as Elder and Younger to keep their active court files separated.
2 CONT Here is why both terms apply and how to use them to keep your documentation bulletproof.
2 CONT * 1. The Biological Fact: Aunt and Niece
2 CONT This is their permanent position on the family tree.
2 CONT Anne (Dame de Genlis) is the daughter of the 1st Duke.
2 CONT Anne (Duchesse d'Halluin) is the daughter of Florimond (the 2nd Duke).
2 CONT Because Florimond is the younger brother of the Dame de Genlis, the two women are strictly Aunt and Niece.
2 CONT * 2. The Archival Label: Elder and Younger (L'Aînée and La Jeune)
2 CONT This is the practical sorting label used by 16th- and 17th-century French royal notaries.
2 CONT Because the Aunt lived a long life (dying in 1607) and her Niece was born in the late 1500s, both women were alive, unmarried, and operating at the French royal court at the exact same time under the exact same maiden name: Anne d
2 CONC 'Halluin.
2 CONT
2 CONT To prevent catastrophic legal errors in royal grants, land deeds, and tax rolls, the king's clerks used distinct identifiers:
2 CONT * Anne d'Halluin l'Aînée (The Elder): Safely identified the Aunt.
2 CONT * Anne d'Halluin la Jeune (The Younger): Safely identified the Niece.
2 CONT Once they married, the clerks dropped "Elder and Younger" and switched entirely to their married titles (Dame de Genlis vs. Duchesse de Schomberg).
