1 NAME Léonor /Chabot de Saint-Gelais/ Baron de Jarnac, Seigneur de Saint-Gelais
2 GIVN Léonor
2 NSFX Baron de Jarnac, Seigneur de Saint-Gelais
2 _MARNM Chabot de Saint-Gelais
2 NICK Léonor Chabot de Saint-Gelais Baron de Jarnac
2 NICK Seigneur de Saint-Gelais
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1541
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1605
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00024661&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY Léonor was born in 1641, the son of Gui I Chabot de Saint-Gelais, baron de Jarnac, seigneur de Montlieu, and Louise de Pisseleu. In 1558 he married Marguerite de Durfort, widow of Philippe de Belleville, comte de Cognac,
2 CONC  and daughter of Symphorien de Durfort, seigneur de Duras, and Barbe Cauchon de Maupas. They had seven children of whom Gui and Charles would have progeny. Marguerite died at the start of 1571, and about 11 March that year Léonor 
2 CONC married Marie-Claude de Rochechouart, daughter of Charles de Rochechouart, seigneur de Saint Amand, and his second wife Françoise de Maricourt. Their three daughters would all have progeny.
2 CONT
2 CONT Léonor was a Protestant from 1561. At this time of religious exuberance Jeanne d'Albret, queen of Navarre, came to Jarnac with her son Henri to preach the tenets of Calvin to an enthusiastic crowd. Exasperated by the massacres of 
2 CONC Wassy and Sens, in March and April 1562 the Huguenots attacked religious buildings; at Jarnac they set fire to the Church of Saint-Pierre, as well as the chapels of Saint-Cybard and Saint-Gilles. The Protestant army, which had occ
2 CONC upied Jarnac without a fight, had to withdraw after the death of Louis I de Bourbon, prince de Condé, in the battle of Jarnac on 3 March 1569. The defeat did not prevent Léonor from having a new church built in Jarnac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Léonor became one of the most influential leaders of the Protestant party in Angoumois, Saintonge and Aunis. On 20 August 1570 he swore an oath with Henri de Navarre, Henri de Bourbon, the Protestant admiral de Coligny and several
2 CONC  other leaders, to defend the four places of La Rochelle, Montauban, Cognac and La Charité. During the promulgation of the Edict of Nantes he was delegated by his co-religionists to demand clarification of this edict, which was vi
2 CONC ewed with apprehension by the Protestants.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received Henri de Navarre in his castle on 11 October 1586. On 31 July 1593 he repurchased the Quint de Jarnac. Following this transaction, the land of Jarnac was once again entirely in the hands of its lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT Léonor was gentleman of the bed chamber (gentilhomme de la chambre) to King Henri IV; as his faithful companion he fought in the majority of his wars. He died in 1605.
