2 CONT *''''''Sir Richard Devereux1
2 CONT *''''''M, #48639, b. circa 1516, d. 13 October 1547
2 CONT *Father      Sir Walter Devereux, 1st Viscount Hereford2 b. 1491, d. 17 Sep 1558
2 CONT *Mother     Mary Grey3 b. c 1494, d. 22 Feb 1538
2 CONT *''''''     Sir Richard Devereux was born circa 1516. He married Dorothy Hastings, daughter of Sir George Hastings, 1st Earl Huntingdon, 3rd Lord Hastings and Anne Stafford, circa 1538.4 Sir Richard Devereux died on 13 October 154
2 CONC 7; d.s.p.1
2 CONT *''''''Family      Dorothy Hastings b. c 1511
2 CONT *Child
2 CONT **Sir Walter Devereux, 2nd Viscount Hereford, Lord Ferrers & Bourchier+1 b. 16 Sep 1539, d. 22 Sep 1576
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. V, p. 140 & 329.
2 CONT * 2.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. V, p. 326-329.
2 CONT * 3.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. V, p. 140, notes.
2 CONT * 4.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. V, p. 140.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1618.htm#i48639
2 CONT *<nowiki>------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT *Walter Devereux, 9th Baron Ferrers of Chartley, created 1st Viscount Hereford (1488 – 17 September 1558) was an English Peer.
2 CONT *He was born in either Chartley Castle or the Chartley Manor which replaced it as the residence of the Barons Ferrers of Chartley following the Wars of the Roses. He was a son of John Devereux, 8th Baron Ferrers of Chartley and Ce
2 CONC cily Bourchier.
2 CONT *His paternal grandparents were Anne Ferrers, 7th Baroness Ferrers of Chartley and her husband Walter Devereux, Lord Ferrers of Chartley. His maternal grandparents were William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier and Anne Woodville.
2 CONT *William Bourchier was a son of Henry Bourchier, 1st Earl of Essex and Isabel of Cambridge. Anne Woodville was a daughter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta of Luxembourg.   ....
2 CONT *He was first married to Mary Grey (1491- 22 February 1538) on 15 December 1503. She was a daughter of Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset and his second wife Cecily Bonville, Baroness Harington and Bonville. Cecily Bonville was t
2 CONC he daughter and heiress of William Bonville, 6th Baron Harington by his wife Katherine Neville. Her maternal grandparents were Alice Montagu, 5th Countess of Salisbury and her consort Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury (jure u
2 CONC xoris). By Mary Grey he had three children:
2 CONT **'''''' 1.Sir Richard Devereux (d. 13 October 1547). Married Dorothea Hastings. She was a daughter of George Hastings, 1st Earl of Huntingdon and Anne Stafford. They were parents to Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex.
2 CONT ** 2.Sir William Devereux of Mirevale Abbey. Married Jane Scudamore, daughter of John Scudamore. The marriage produced two daughters, Barbara Devereux and Margaret Devereux.
2 CONT ** 3.Henry Devereux, died unmarried.
2 CONT *In 1539, he married secondly Margaret Garneys, daughter of John Garneys of Kenton. They had two children:
2 CONT ** 1.Sir Edward Devereux, 1st Baronet of Castle Bromwich. Married Catherine Arden. They were parents of Walter Devereux, 5th Viscount Hereford, fourth-generation ancestors of Price Devereux, 9th Viscount Hereford and fifth-generat
2 CONC ion ancestors of Edward Devereux, 11th Viscount Hereford.
2 CONT ** 2.Katherine Devereux. Married Sir James Baskerville.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Devereux,_1st_Viscount_Hereford
2 CONT *<nowiki>-----------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT *''''''Richard DEVEREUX (Sir Knight)
2 CONT *''''''Died: 13 Oct 1547
2 CONT *Father: Walter DEVEREUX (1º V. Hereford)
2 CONT *Mother: Mary GREY
2 CONT *''''''Married: Dorothy HASTINGS ABT 1541, Bedenham, Herefordshire, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Walter DEVEREUX (1° E. Essex)
2 CONT **2. Elizabeth DEVEREUX
2 CONT **3. George DEVEREUX
2 CONT **4. Anne DEVEREUX
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/DEVEREUX.htm#Richard DEVEREUX (Sir Knight)1
2 CONT *<nowiki>-------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT *''''''DEVEREUX, Richard (by 1513-47), of Carmarthen, Carm. and Lamphey, Pemb.
2 CONT *Family and Education
2 CONT *''''''b. by 1513, 1st s. and h. app. of Walter Devereux, 3rd LordFerrers of Chartley, 1st Viscount Hereford, by 1st w. Mary, da. of Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset; bro. of William and half-bro. of Edward Devereux. m. settlem
2 CONC ent 1 July 1536, Dorothy, da. of George† Hastings, 1st Earl of Huntingdon, 2s. inc. George 3da. KB 20 Feb. 1547.2
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **Bailiff, Carmarthen 1534-5, mayor 1536-7; commr. for tenths of spiritualities, St. David’s diocese 1535; dep. steward, lordships of Arwystli and Cyfeiliog, Mont. in 1537; dep. justice and chamberlain of South Wales temp. Hen. VI
2 CONC II; j.p. Card. and Pemb. 1543, Glos. and Mon. 1547; custos rot. Carm. 1543; member council in the marches of Wales Mar. 1547.3
2 CONT *Biography
2 CONT *''''''The branch of the family to which Richard Devereux belonged was based in Herefordshire but its lands and influence extended deep into Wales. His father, a councillor in the marches of Wales in 1513 and steward of Princess M
2 CONC ary’s household when it came to Ludlow in 1525, was appointed chamberlain of South Wales in 1526 and thereafter built up a powerful position for himself, especially in Carmarthenshire and the town of Carmarthen, where he was mayor
2 CONC  in 1534-5. He benefited greatly from the overthrow of Rhys ap Gruffydd, who was executed for treason in 1531; Rhys had resented the grant of the chamberlainship to Ferrers and had attacked him during his holding of the King’s ses
2 CONC sions at Carmarthen in 1529. Richard Devereux was to die before his father and without attaining comparable stature. His life was centred on south-west Wales; he lived at Carmarthen, where during his father’s mayoralty he was town
2 CONC  bailiff and in 1536, the year in which he married, mayor in his turn. In the Parliament of June 1536 a private Act (28 Hen. VIII, c.37) was passed whereby his father conveyed various lands to him on his forthcoming marriage.4
2 CONT *''''''When Bishop Barlow of St. David’s began his trial of strength with the chapter there Devereux came out strongly on the side of the canons. His vigorous intervention answered to his dislike both of Barlow’s Protestantism and
2 CONC  of the bishop’s relatives in Haverfordwest and Slebech. Devereux was deputy to his father as steward of the lordships of Arwystli and Cyfeiliog in Montgomeryshire, and so figured in the dispute over them between Ferrers and the 2
2 CONC nd Earl of Worcester in 1537. In the quarrel of 1540 between the borough of New Carmarthen and its suburb Old Carmarthen, formerly governed by the priory there, Devereux sided with Old Carmarthen, which Ferrers claimed to govern a
2 CONC s successor to the prior. In 1543 he served in the French campaign under Sir John Wallop and was mentioned in a despatch. Three years later he acquired the episcopal palace at Lamphey in an exchange with Bishop Barlow. As a proper
2 CONC ty owner in his own right and heir-apparent to one of the chief estates in Wales he was much concerned with farming and trade, and in the mid 154os he referred three deals made on his behalf to Chancery for remedy.5
2 CONT *''''''Devereux’s parliamentary career may have begun with the enfranchisement of Wales at the Union. It is not known who was returned as knight for Carmarthenshire in 1542 but the election was contested and Devereux was one of th
2 CONC e candidates. He enlivened the town of Carmarthen by his encouragement of unruly behaviour and resort to force, and the fact that his adversary lodged a complaint with the Privy Council, which was noted in the Council’s register o
2 CONC n 24 Jan. 1542, shortly after the opening of the first session of Parliament, suggests that these methods proved successful. As the complaint was not referred to the Star Chamber but was sent to the president of the council in the
2 CONC  marches, the resulting proceedings, like nearly all of those before that body, have been lost. There was another contest in 1545 but this time Devereux is known to have carried the day by a majority of 80. Whether religion was an
2 CONC  issue at either election is not known, but Devereux’s comments on practices thought by him to be superstitious led in 1546 to his being examined by the Privy Council.6
2 CONT *''''''The reign of Edward VI began auspiciously for Devereux: within weeks of its opening he was knighted and put on the council in the marches. A few months later he was again elected to Parliament for Carmarthenshire, but wheth
2 CONC er he took his seat may be doubted for he died on 12 Nov. 1547, eight days after the Parliament opened. At the inquisition post mortem held in July 1548 he was found to have died possessed of the lordship of Lamphey, worth some £3
2 CONC 9 a year. By his will (which is not extant but is quoted in the inquisition) the whole of the lordship was to go to Lady Devereux during her widowhood and then to their younger son George for life, with remainder to the elder, the
2 CONC n aged six, who became 2nd Viscount Hereford and later Earl of Essex. In the absence of a testament the administration of Devereux’s goods was granted to his widow, who was sued in the common pleas during Michaelmas term 1549 for 
2 CONC money owing to a London mercer. Devereux was replaced in the Commons by John Perrot.7
2 CONT *Ref Volumes: 1509-1558
2 CONT *Author: P. S. Edwards
2 CONT *Notes
2 CONT *1. Did not serve for the full duration of the Parliament.
2 CONT *2. Date of birth estimated from first reference. Vis. Warws. (Harl. Soc. xii), 279-80; DWB (Devereux fam.).
2 CONT *3. Cal. Sheriffs etc. of Carmarthen (NLW ms 5586B), 7; J. E. Lloyd, Carm. ii. 467; LP Hen. VIII, viii, xii; SP1/117/230-1; APC, ii. 62, 448; C193/12/1; CPR, 1547-8, pp. 83, 86.
2 CONT *4. DWB; D. Williams, Modern Wales, 28-29.
2 CONT *5. Bull. Bd. of Celtic Studies, xv, 212-24; SP1/116/220, 158/145; LP Hen. VIII, xii, xviii, xxi; St.Ch.2/17/271; C1/1115/50.
2 CONT *6. LP Hen. VIII, xvii; PPC, vii. 297, 299; C219/18C/169; APC, i. 401.
2 CONT *7. Wards 9/137/106; CP40/1142, r. 695.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/devereux-richard-1513-47
2 CONT *________________
