1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eleanor Clifford, Countess of Cumberland (née Lady Eleanor Brandon; 1519 – 27 September 1547) was the third child and second daughter of Charles Brandon, 1st Duke of Suffolk and Princess Mary Tudor, the Dowager Que
2 CONC en consort of France.[1] She was a younger sister of Lady Frances Brandon and an elder sister of Henry Brandon, 1st Earl of Lincoln. She was also a younger paternal half-sister of Lady Anne Brandon and Lady Mary Brandon from her f
2 CONC ather's second marriage. After her mother's death in 1533, her father remarried to Catherine Willoughby and Eleanor became an elder half-sister of Henry Brandon, 2nd Duke of Suffolk and Charles Brandon, 3rd Duke of Suffolk.
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Sir William Brandon and Elizabeth Bruyn. Her maternal grandparents were Henry VII of England and his queen consort Elizabeth of York. She was thus a niece of Henry VIII.		
2 CONT Countess of Cumberland
2 CONT Lady Eleanor was a descendant of a member of the Tudor dynasty and therefore her marriage would advance the political ambitions of any given husband. In March 1533, a marriage contract was written up for Lady Eleanor and Henry Cli
2 CONC fford, the eldest son and heir of Henry Clifford, 1st Earl of Cumberland by Lady Margaret Percy.[2] However, since her mother died nine months later, she waited to go and live with her young husband and in-laws. In anticipation of
2 CONC  Eleanor's arrival, the Earl of Cumberland built two towers and the great gallery within Skipton Castle.[3] Eleanor married Clifford in June 1535; her uncle King Henry VIII was present.[3][4][5][6]
2 CONT In January 1536, Eleanor was designated the chief mourner for the funeral service of Catherine of Aragon, first Queen consort of Henry VIII, at Peterborough Cathedral.[7]
2 CONT There is not much known about her later life and she left only one letter:
2 CONT "Dear heart, After my most hearty commendations, this shall be to certify you that since your departure from me I have been very sick and at this present my water is very red, whereby I suppose I have the jaundice and the ague bot
2 CONC h, for I have none abide [no appetite for] meat and I have such pains in my side and towards my back as I had at Brougham, where it began with me first. Wherefore I desire you to help me to a physician and that this bearer my brin
2 CONC g him with him, for now in the beginning I trust I may have good remedy, and the longer it is delayed, the worse it will be. Also my sister Powys [Anne Brandon] is come to me and very desirous to see you, which I trust shall be th
2 CONC e sooner at this time, and thus Jesus send us both health.
2 CONT At my lodge at Carlton, the 14th of February. And, dear heart, I pray you send for Dr Stephens, for he knoweth best my complexion for such causes.
2 CONT By your assured loving wife, Eleanor Cumberland"
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT With Henry Clifford:
2 CONT * Lady Margaret Clifford (1540 - 28 September 1596); she married Henry Stanley, 4th Earl of Derby.
2 CONT * Henry Clifford; died an infant.
2 CONT * Charles Clifford; died an infant.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_Clifford,_Countess_of_Cumberland?fbclid=IwAR2aNJ65xG70E5mq3EgjHU9P8oqfH-3xj7_HcBuruDj9zUAu6jNXbuc8C2Y#]
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brougham_Castle
