2 CONT *'''Sir Edward Devereux
2 CONT *'''M, #67728, b. 3 August 1558, d. 22 September 1622
2 CONT *Father      Sir Walter Devereux, 1st Viscount Hereford b. 1491, d. 17 Sep 1558
2 CONT *Mother     Margaret Garneys b. c 1520, d. a 12 Feb 1594
2 CONT *'''     Sir Edward Devereux married Ursula Pitcher DID NOT MARRY.1 Sir Edward Devereux was born on 3 August 1558 at Castle Bromwich, Warwickshire, England. He died on 22 September 1622 at age 64.
2 CONT *'''Family      Ursula Pitcher''' b. c 1565
2 CONT *'''Child
2 CONT **Elizabeth Devereux+ b. 3 Apr 1586
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S74] Brent Ruesch's Research Notes.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2254.htm#i67728 _________________
2 CONT *'''DEVEREUX, Edward (c.1550-1622), of Castle Bromwich, Warws.
2 CONT *Family and Education
2 CONT *'''b. c.1550, yst. s.''' of Walter Devereux, 1st Visct. Hereford, by his 2nd w. Margaret, da. of Robert Garnish of Kenton, Suff. '''m. Katherine''', da. of Edward '''Arden of Park Hall, 5s. 4da. cr. Bt. 25 Nov. 1611. Kntd. 19 Aug
2 CONC . 1612.1
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **'''Sheriff, Warws. And Leics. 1593-4; j.p.q. Warws. from c.1591.
2 CONT *'''As the youngest son, Devereux inherited little land. In 1572 he purchased from his nephew''' Walter, 1st Earl of Essex, '''the reversion of the manor of Castle Bromwich and other property, then held by his mother in dower. He 
2 CONC did not come into full possession of the property until her death in 1599, but he had probably lived there as a country gentleman for most of his life. There are few references to his activities, though in the 1590s he had lodging
2 CONC s in London, and he was given an honorary admission to Gray’s Inn in 1595, probably through his great-nephew of the half-blood Robert, 2nd Earl of Essex, with whom his connexion was probably closer than appears from the surviving 
2 CONC evidence. This consists only of a report to Cecil in September 1601, after the Earl’s execution, that Devereux and his son had taken part in a suspicious meeting with others of his family and friends at Wolverhampton.2
2 CONT *'''No doubt it was Essex who obtained Devereux his one seat in the Commons. He was not politically active, however, nor a power in his shire. He built up his estates and had a new hall constructed at Castle Bromwich. In his last 
2 CONC will, made 25 July 1622, he apologized for not leaving tokens of remembrance to his friends and explained that the jewels and rings he had set aside for that purpose had been stolen. His goods he divided mainly between his wife an
2 CONC d eldest son''' Sir Walter, with only 5s. to '''his unmarried daughter''' Grace, ‘by reason of her uncivil behaviour’. The residue went to purchase lands for '''his grandson''' Essex Devereux, later Viscount Hereford. Sir Walter, 
2 CONC the sole executor, proved the will 3 Oct. 1622. '''Devereux died 22 Sept. and was buried in Aston church, near Birmingham.3
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/devereux-edward-1550-1622 __________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 14
2 CONT *Devereux, Walter (d.1558)    by William Hunt ‎
2 CONT *DEVEREUX, WALTER, Viscount Hereford (d. 1558), son of John, lord Ferrers, of Chartley, Staffordshire, and Cecily, sister of Henry Bourchier, earl of Essex, was born before 1490 (Doyle), succeeded his father as third baron Ferrers
2 CONC  in 1501, and on 7 Dec. 1509 received special livery of the lands of his inheritance, being then under full age (Cal. of Henry VIII, vol. i. No. 736). He was appointed high steward of Tamworth in 1510 (ib. 1354), and joint-constab
2 CONC le of Warwick Castle with Sir Edward Belknap in February 1511 (ib. 1499). He accompanied his brother-in-law, the Marquis of Dorset, on the expedition sent to Guipuscoa in 1512, in order to act with the Spaniards in an intended inv
2 CONC asion of Guienne (Herbert, History of Henry VIII, p. 127). In the December of that year he was appointed captain of the Imperyall Carrik (Cal. ii. No. 3591), and the next year commanded the Trinitye, receiving 6s. 8d. a day as pay
2 CONC  (ib. 4533). While serving under Admiral Sir Edward Howard he took a prominent part in the engagement off Conquêt on 25 April, in which the admiral fell (Herbert, p. 138). On 1 Aug. following he was appointed a member of the counc
2 CONC il of Wales and the marches. He was made a knight of the Garter in 1523, and served in the ineffectual campaign of the Duke of Suffolk against France. In 1525 he was appointed steward of the household of Mary, princess of Wales, a
2 CONC nd chief justice of South Wales, and the next year chamberlain of South Wales (Cal. iv. No. 2200), and appears to have been actively engaged in fulfilling the duties of these offices. When Henry made his expedition against France 
2 CONC in 1544, he marched in the rear guard of the army under the command of Lord Russell (Herbert, p. 690). He was sworn of the privy council of Edward VI in January, and created Viscount Hereford on 2 Feb. 1550 (Doyle). He died 27 Sep
2 CONC t. 1558, and was buried in the parish church of Stowe, near Stafford, under a monument erected during his lifetime (Dugdale). By his first wife, Mary, daughter of Thomas Grey, marquis of Dorset, he had two sons and a daughter. His
2 CONC  eldest son, Richard, married Dorothy, daughter of George Hastings, earl of Huntingdon, and predeceased him in 1547, leaving a son Walter, created earl of Essex [q. v.], and other children. His second wife was Margaret, daughter o
2 CONC f Robert Garnish of Kenton, Suffolk, by whom he had an '''only son, Sir Edward Devereux.
2 CONT *[Several notices of Walter Devereux, lord Ferrers, will be found in Calendar of Henry VIII, vols. i–vii.; Lord Herbert's Hist. of the Reign of Henry VIII, ed. 1870; Doyle's Official Baronage, ii. 167, where a portrait of Devereux
2 CONC  is given from the Stowe monument; Dugdale's Baronage, ii. 177.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Devereux,_Walter_(d.1558)_(DNB00) ________________
2 CONT *'''Burke's genealogical and heraldic history of the peerage, baronetage and ...
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=RVggAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA1001&lpg=PA1001&dq=Walter+Devereux,+1st+Viscount+Hereford&source=bl&ots=2AfD2zqevQ&sig=9JrE-AB1dQzJ1Gaam53c-C4fSqM&hl=en&ei=pAW0TOnGKob6swOS4tCcCA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&r
