2 _MARNM Savage
1 NAME Sir John Savage, VIII, Kt., of Rock Savage
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC St. Michael's & All Angels Churchyard
2 ADDR
3 ADR1  Market Place
3 CITY Macclesfield
3 STAE Cheshire East
3 POST SK10 1DY
3 CTRY England
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1525
2 ADDR
3 CITY Clifton
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 DEC 1597
2 PLAC Probably Rock Savage
2 ADDR
3 CITY Clifton
3 STAE England
1 OCCU Knight
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sir John Savage=
2 CONT * BIRTH Oct 1524 Cheshire, England
2 CONT * DEATH 5 Dec 1597 (aged 73) Cheshire, England
2 CONT * BURIAL St. Michael's & All Angels Churchyard, Macclesfield, Cheshire East Unitary Authority, Cheshire, England
2 CONT * PLOT Savage Chapel
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID  103535621 photos added by Peter Cox & Karen Kanatzar
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT Sir John Savage of Clifton and Rock Savage, Cheshire was born in 1524, the son of Sir John Savage of Clifton and Elizabeth Somerset, the daughter of Charles Somersett Earl of Worcestershire.[2]  He was only three years and nine mo
2 CONC nths old when his father died on 27 July 1528 [1]  but became Knight of Rock Savage, Mayor of Chester & Sheriff of Cheshire.  Through his mother's second marriage, John had a half-brother, Henry Brereton, gentleman, son of William
2 CONC  Brereton esq, and of Lady Elizabeth, late his wife and late wife of Sir John Savage Kt.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He  married twice;1st to Lady Elizabeth Manners, daughter of Thomas Manners, 1st Earl of Rutland and Eleanor Paston.  She died at Frodsham on 8 August 1570.[5]  He 2nd married Elinour Cotgrave, the daughter of John Cotgreve of Stu
2 CONC bbs, widow of Sir Richard Pexsall, about 1572 at Clifton, Cheshire, England, and they had no children;[2]  He died on 5 December 1597.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife, Elinour, was bequeathed by her first husband, Sir Richard Pexhull of Beaurepair in Southhamptonshire, lands in the counties of Southampton and Wiltshire, which her second husband, Sir John settled on his younger s
2 CONC on, Edward, and Edward's heirs by Polyxena, the daughter of William le Griz, of London, gent, and his wife, Katherine who was the natural sister of Sir John's second wife, Dame Elinour.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John assumed for his coat of arms six lions Sable and the lion's paw Sable erected for his crest, where before he had borne the Daniell's coat of arms.[4] He built the house at Clifton, called Rocksavage and from 17 Elizabeth,
2 CONC  started styling himself of Rocksavage at times and sometimes of Clifton, but from 21 Elizabeth he styled himself only as John Savage of Rocksavage.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 October, 1558, Henry Brereton, gentleman, son of William Brereton esq, deceased, and of Lady Elizabeth, late his wife and late wife of Sir John Savage Kt, also deceased, quitclaimed to Sir John Savage Kt, son and administrato
2 CONC r of the said Lady Elizabeth, of all suits and actions etc. which he has or may have against the said Sir John as such administrator, and of his share of the goods, chattels etc: of the said Lady Elizabeth, in consideration of an 
2 CONC annuity of 20 marks.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Savage was sheriff of Cheshire seven times between 1560 and 1591, mayor of Cheshire three times between 1569 and 1597.[4] He was the seneschal of Halton Castle, and also served at various times as a member of parliament f
2 CONC or Cheshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 December, 27 Elizabeth [1584], Sir John Savage of Rocksavage, knight, granted to John Egerton of Olton, esquire, the wardship and marriage of Thomas Venables son and heir of Robarte Venables late of Antrobus, Cheshire, gentl
2 CONC eman, and the custody and wardship of the lands &c. of which Robert died seised and which came to Thomas as his heir; the lands &c. are held of Sir John Savage by knight's service of 1/100 part of one knight's fee; the indenture o
2 CONC f the grant was endorsed: Delivered to Edward Savage, gent, to the use of John Egerton, esquire, in the presence of Francis Savage, Richard Gregge and Edward Nangreve.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Savage of Rocksavage died in October 1597,[2] or he died on 5 December, 1597 and was buried at Macclesfield on 24 January, 1597/8.[4] A description of the funeral arrangements is reproduced in the introduction to Cheshire
2 CONC  and Lancashire Funeral Certificates, A.D. 1600 to 1678.[6] His tomb together with that of his first wife can be found in the Savage Chapel of St Michael's Church in Macclesfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Knight of the Garter==
2 CONT Lady Frances Savage as daughter of Sir John Savage, Knight of Rock Savage b: 10/1524 d: 12/5/1597 and Lady Elizabeth Manners b: 1506, d: 08/08/1570. Frances Savage was b: 1567, of Clifton, Cheshire and d: aft 1598. It is thought F
2 CONC rances Savage was possibly m: to Earl Sir ?Edward? de Vere b: abt 1550 son of Earl John de Vere and Margery Golding. Edward de Vere was heir of his father. In abt 1593, Frances Savage m: Sir Thomas Wilkes, Knight, Clerk of the Pri
2 CONC vy Counsel to Queen Elizabeth I. Sir Thomas Wilkes, Knight, Clerk was b: abt 1538 of Surrey or London, England d: abt 03/02/1597 Roen, France, buried in England; Thomas was son of Sir Thomas Wilkes, Esq. and Merchant of the Staple
2 CONC  of Calais d: 1558 and probably his wife Joan Leveson. Sir Thomas Wilkes was 1st married to Margaret Smith b: abt 1560 London, England d: 05/14/1596 Rickmansworth, Hertfordshire, England. Margaret Smith was daughter of Ambrose Smi
2 CONC th and Joan Johanna Coe, Coo or Cope, daughter of Sir John Coe and Dorothy Hale.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Rocksavage==
2 CONT "an Elizabethan mansion, now in ruins, at SJ526799 in Clifton (now a district of Runcorn), Cheshire, England. Built for Sir John Savage, MP in 1565–8, Rocksavage was one of the great Elizabethan houses of the county, a leading exa
2 CONC mple of the Elizabethan prodigy house; in 1674, it was the second largest house in Cheshire. James I visited in 1617. The house was abandoned when it passed into the Cholmondeley family early in the 18th century,[1] and by 1782 on
2 CONC ly ruins remained." From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on John Savage:
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT After his father's death, his mother, Elizabeth, married Sir William Brereton, younger son of Sir Randle Brereton of Malpas, and this William was of the bedchamber to Henry VIII and on 17 May, 1536, "beheaded for matters touching 
2 CONC Queen Anne".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT Sir John Savage 1493–1528
2 CONT Lady Elizabeth Somerset Savage Brereton 1497–1545
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouse'''
2 CONT Lady Elizabeth Manners Savage unknown–1570
2 CONT Elinour Cotgrave Savage, bequeathed by her first husband, Sir Richard Pexhull
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Henry Savage[2] of Barrow;[1]
2 CONT Margaret who was married to Sir Richard Bulkle
2 CONT Mary Savage Hitchcock 1514–1570
2 CONT Anne Savage Robinson unknown–1570
2 CONT y,[2] or Buckley of Beaumorris in Wales;[1]
2 CONT Mary who was married to John Hamden;[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children''' With Elizabeth 11x*, Elinour** [5]
2 CONT *John Savage1548 - died an infant 
2 CONT *Margaret Savage Brereton 1549–4/7/1597 m 1/24/1561 Sir William Brereton of Brereton, Cheshire, later baron of Laghlin in Ireland
2 CONT *Elizabeth who was married to Thomas Langton Baron of Newton,[2] Lancashire; Elizabeth was born in 1552 and married Thomas in 1580, Thomas was divorced from Margaret Shirborne on 25 June, 1580, because the marriage was in "impuber
2 CONC tate"
2 CONT *Sir John Savage 1st Bt.1554–1615 created Baronet 1611,m Mary Allington
2 CONT *Thomas, born 1556 - died young
2 CONT *Lady Mary Savage Browne 1556–1632
2 CONT *Eleanora Savage 1557 - 1st m 1577 Sir Henry Bagnall, son/heir of Nicholas, 2nd m Sir Sackvill Trever
2 CONT *Edward Savage 1560 m Polinex, the daughter of William Grice of Norfolk
2 CONT *Francis Savage 1562, died young 
2 CONT * Mary Savage Washbourne 1563 - m Sir Richard Milles in Hampshire
2 CONT * Frances Savage 1567 - m Thomas Wilkes, Esq. in the county of Surrey
2 CONT
