1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Stanley,_3rd_Earl_of_Derby Knight of the Garter
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Stanley, 3rd Earl of Derby From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The 3rd Earl of Derby. Edward Stanley, 3rd Earl of Derby (c. 10 May 1509–24 October 1572) was an English nobleman. At the age of thirteen, Edward received the titles and estates of his father, the 2nd Earl of Derby, and King Henry
2 CONC  VIII took responsibility for bringing him up until he was of age. His commissioners, including Cardinal Thomas Wolsey were responsible for most of his affairs. In 1528, he accompanied Cardinal Wolsey on a mission to France, and i
2 CONC n 1530, he was one of the peers who gave Pope Clement VII the declaration regarding Henry's divorce from Catherine of Aragon. That same year, the Duke of Norfolk decided to wed his eldest daughter, Catherine, to Edward; however, t
2 CONC his was done without the King's permission (which was required). The King rebuked Howard but allowed the marriage to continue; unfortunately, Catherine became a victim of the plague a few weeks after the marriage. Undeterred, Howa
2 CONC rd's half-sister, Dorothy, became Edward's second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Stanley
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel (1533-1590) wife of James Stanley
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane (d.1569)wife of Edward Sutton
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary (d.1609) wife of Edward Stafford
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann wife of Charles Stourton
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1532, Edward accompanied King Henry to Boulogne, where they met with King Francis I of France. After this meeting, Edward became a Knight of the Bath. A few years later, Edward took a major role in quelling the Pilgrimage of Gr
2 CONC ace, a large (mainly church-related) rebellion started in Lincolnshire and spread into North England. In 1542, Edward accompanied the Duke of Norfolk on a raid into Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Edward VI ascended to the throne in 1547, Edward became a Knight of the Garter, and in 1550, he was one of the peers who were present at the peace proceedings with Scotland and France. A year later, various charges were broug
2 CONC ht against him (with little or no evidence), mainly due to his opposition to clerical reform. However, when Queen Mary ascended to the throne, he was again in favour, and was appointed Lord High Steward and became a Privy Councill
2 CONC or. He was a commissioner of Lady Jane Grey's trial, and was frequently present during the trials of accused heretics. He remained in favour under Queen Elizabeth I's reign, and remained on her Privy Council. She eventually appoin
2 CONC ted him Chamberlain of Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Stanley died at Lathom House, and his titles and estates were passed on to his eldest son, Henry Stanley.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://atropesend.blogspot.com/2010/05/anges-henry-viii-anne-boleyn-and.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 3rd Earl of Derby
2 CONT
