1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Catherine Neville1
2 CONT *'''F, #52532, b. circa 1520
2 CONT *Father	Sir George Neville, Lord Abergavenny, Constable of Dover Castle, Warden of the Cinque Ports1 b. c 1469
2 CONT *Mother	Mary Stafford1 b. c 1503
2 CONT *'''     Catherine Neville was born circa 1520 at of Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, England.2 She married Sir John St. Leger''', son of Sir George St. Leger and Anne Knyvett, '''circa 1540 at England.1
2 CONT *'''Family     Sir John St. Leger b. c 1515
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 95.
2 CONT *[S61] Unknown author, Family Group Sheets, Family History Archives, SLC.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1748.htm#i52532 
2 CONT ________________
2 CONT *'''Lady Catherine Neville1
2 CONT *'''F, #1057, b. after 1519
2 CONT *Last Edited=12 Nov 2013
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=1.18%
2 CONT *'''     Lady Catherine Neville was born after 1519. She was the daughter''' of Sir George Neville, 3rd Lord Abergavenny and Lady Mary Stafford.1 '''She married John St. Leger''', son of Sir George St. Leger and Anne Knyvett, '''c
2 CONC irca 1540.1
2 CONT *'''      From circa 1540, her married name became St. Leger.1
2 CONT *'''Children of Lady Catherine Neville and John St. Leger
2 CONT **Mary St. Leger2 d. 1623
2 CONT **John St. Leger2
2 CONT **Charles St. Leger2
2 CONT **Daniel St. Leger2
2 CONT **Elizabeth St. Leger2
2 CONT **Ulalia St. Leger2
2 CONT **Margaret St. Leger2
2 CONT **Jane St. Leger2
2 CONT **Frances St. Leger+2
2 CONT **Catherine St. Leger2 b. 1572, d. 1620
2 CONT **Dudley St. Leger2 b. 1578, d. 1627
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S8] BP1999 volume 1, page 17. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S8]
2 CONT *[S4567] Bill Norton, "re: Pitman Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger LUNDY (101053), 6 April 2010 and 19 April 2011. Hereinafter cited as "re: Pitman Family."
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p106.htm#i1057 
2 CONT _________
2 CONT *'''Catherine NEVILLE
2 CONT *'''Born: 1520, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, Wales
2 CONT *Father: George NEVILLE (3° B. Abergavenny)
2 CONT *Mother: Mary STAFFORD (B. Abergavenny)
2 CONT *'''Married: John St. LEGER of Annery (Sir Knight) ABT 1540, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Frances St. LEGER
2 CONT **2. John St. LEGER
2 CONT **3. Charles St. LEGER
2 CONT **4. Daniel St. LEGER
2 CONT **5. Elizabeth St. LEGER
2 CONT **6. Ulalia St. LEGER
2 CONT **7. Margaret St. LEGER
2 CONT **8. Catherine St. LEGER (b. 1572 - d. 1620)
2 CONT **9. Jane St. LEGER
2 CONT **10. Mary St. LEGER
2 CONT **11. Dudley St. LEGER (b. 1578 - d. 1627)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/NEVILLE4.htm#Catherine NEVILLE3 
2 CONT ____________
2 CONT *'''ST. LEGER, Sir John (by 1516-93/96), of Annery in Monkleigh, Devon.
2 CONT *b. by 1516, 1st s. of Sir George St. Leger of Annery by Anne, da. of Edmund Knyvet. '''m. by June 1535, Catherine, da. of George Neville, 5th Lord Bergavenny, at least 2s. 4da.''' suc. fa. 1533/37. Kntd. 1544/1 Oct. 1547.1
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **Commr. relief, Devon 1550, musters 1569; j.p. 1554, q. 1558/59-d.; dep. lt. Devon and Cornw. 1558, Devon 1569; sheriff, Devon 1560-1.2
2 CONT *John St. Leger was only a boy when his father and grandmother arranged that he should marry a daughter of the wealthy royal favourite Sir William Compton; the bride was to bring a dowry of £2,346 and both families were to settle 
2 CONC lands on the couple. The marriage did not take place, seemingly because Catherine Compton died, and it was '''replaced by a match with Catherine Neville, a granddaughter of the 3rd Duke of Buckingham. St. Leger was married by 1535
2 CONC ''' and within two years he had livery of an inheritance comprising lands in nine counties. He served in the French campaign of 1544. According to Sir William Paget, Henry VIII on his deathbed chose St. Leger for creation as a bar
2 CONC on, but the Council revised the King’s plan after his death.3
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/st-leger-sir-john-1516-9396 
2 CONT ___________
2 CONT *'''ST. LEGER (SELLENGER), Sir John (by 1516-93/96), of Annery in Monkleigh, Devon.
2 CONT *b. by 1516, 1st s. of Sir George St. Leger of Annery by Anne, da. of Edmund Knyvet. '''m. by June 1535, Catherine, da. of George Neville, 5th Lord Bergavenny, at least 2s. 4da.''' suc. fa. bet. 1544 and 1547.
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **Commr. relief, Devon 1550, musters 1569; j.p. from 1554, q. from c.1559, sheriff 1560-1; dep. lt. Cornw. and Devon 1558, Devon 1569.1
2 CONT *St. Leger inherited considerable west-country estates, and remained an active local official from the reign of Henry VIII to almost the end of Elizabeth’s reign. He conformed to the state church, whether Catholic or protestant, b
2 CONC ut that his sympathies were with the reformers is suggested by the end Earl of Bedford’s appointing him a deputy lieutenant and by Elizabeth’s making him a visitor for the diocese of Exeter and giving him a rectory and advowson. S
2 CONC t. Leger thrice sat as knight of the shire, and came in for Arundel (through his relative the 12th Earl of Arundel) and Tregony (through the Pomeroy family or the 2nd Earl of Bedford). His parliamentary career is of no special int
2 CONC erest. He was on the succession committee (31 Oct. 1566), and was one of 30 Commons MPs summoned on 5 Nov. to hear the Queen’s message on the succession. He claimed privilege for a servant 9 Apr. 1571, at a time when he was himsel
2 CONC f up to his ears in debt, and served on committees concerned with navigation (8 May 1571), Devon harbours (11 May), tanners (11 May), and cloth (8 Feb. 1581).
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/st-leger-%28sellenger%29-sir-john-1516-9396 
2 CONT ___________
2 CONT *'''Sir John St. Leger''' (died 1596), of Annery in the parish of Monkleigh, Devon, was an English politician. Sheriff of Devon in 1560, he was Member of Parliament for Dartmouth, Devon, in 1555-1558, Devon in 1559–1563, Arundel, 
2 CONC Sussex, in 1563-1571, Devon again in 1571-1583 and Tregony, Cornwall in 1584-1585.[2] He was knighted in 1544.
2 CONT *He was the son of Sir George St. Leger (c.1475-1536), of Annery, by his wife, Anne Knyvett, daughter of Edmund Knyvett. His paternal grandparents were Sir James St. Leger, of Shipton, and Lady Anne Butler, heiress of Annery, daug
2 CONC hter of Thomas Butler, 7th Earl of Ormonde[citation needed] and great-aunt of Queen Anne Boleyn. One of his great-uncles was Sir Thomas St Leger (c.1440-1483), the husband of Anne of York, Duchess of Exeter (1439-1476), sister of 
2 CONC Kings Edward IV and Richard III. His great-grandfather was Sir John St Leger (c.1404-1442) lord of the manor of Ulcombe in Kent, whose three sons Randolf (or Ralph), Thomas and James, all made advantageous marriages. The manor of 
2 CONC Ulcombe had been held by the family from the See of Canterbury from shortly after the Norman Conquest of 1066, during which a St Leger knight is supposed by tradition to have supported William the Conqueror with his hand on disemb
2 CONC arking from his ship at Pevensey.[3] The name was Latinised to de Sancto Leodegario.
2 CONT *'''He married Catherine Neville''', daughter of George Neville, 5th Baron Bergavenny and '''according Virgoe (1982) left two sons[4] (only one according to Chope (1917)[5]) and four daughters:
2 CONT **John St Leger, eldest son, a soldier in Ireland, died unmarried and in poverty
2 CONT **Dudley St Leger, possibly illegitimate[6]
2 CONT **Mary St Leger, the eldest, who married Sir Richard Grenville (1542-1591) of Stowe, Kilkhampton, Cornwall and Bideford, Devon, the famous captain of Revenge.
2 CONT **Frances St Leger, who married John Stucley (1551-1611), of Affeton, Devon[9] and was the mother of Sir Lewis Stucley[10]
2 CONT **Margaret St Leger, who married Richard Bellew of Ash, Braunton and Alverdiscott, Devon. A heraldic mural monument to Richard Bellew and his wife Margaret St Leger survives in Braunton parish Church.
2 CONT **Eulalia St Leger, who married firstly Edmund Tremayne of Collacombe, Devon, and secondly Tristram Arscott (1544-1621) of Tetcott, Devon, the latter who bought Annery from his father-in-law John St Leger.[9][11]
2 CONT *Under a royal grant dated 10 June 1543 "John St Leger, King's servant" acquired the following former monastic lands following the Dissolution of the Monasteries:[12]
2 CONT **Canonsleigh Priory in the parish of Burlescombe, site and demesne of.
2 CONT **Priory manor of Hockworthy.
2 CONT **Torre Abbey, site and demesne of, subject to a 21-year lease granted at Dissolution to Hugh I Pollard of King's Nympton.[13] St Leger sold it in 1543 to Hugh Pollard.[14]
2 CONT **Manor of Cullompton, formerly held by Buckland Abbey.
2 CONT **Manor of Upton Weaver (or Higher Upton) in the parish of Cullompton, formerly held by St Nicholas's Priory, Exeter.
2 CONT **Other non-monastic property in Devon
2 CONT **Small landholding in Cornwall.
2 CONT *The lands were exchanged with the king for other lands of St Leger, with an excess balance paid by him of £116 9s 9d.[15]
2 CONT *Despite accumulating much land, he became encumbered with debts and parted with many of his estates. He died "a poor man"[2] and on the death of his son John without progeny the St Leger family of Annery was extinguished. However
2 CONC , another line descended from Randolf St Leger (d.1470), Sheriff of Kent and one of his great uncles, father of Sir Anthony St Leger (d.1559), KG, Lord Deputy of Ireland, whose descendants were created Viscount Doneraile, later in
2 CONC herited the manor of Eggesford in Devon.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_St._Leger_(died_1596) 
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT *George Neville or Nevill, 5th and de jure 3rd Baron Bergavenny KG, PC (c.1469 – 1535/6) was an English courtier. He held the office of Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports.
2 CONT *George Neville was born in Abergavenny, Monmouthshire,[citation needed] the son of George Neville, 4th Baron Bergavenny, by his first wife, Margaret Fenne (d. 28 September 1485), daughter of Hugh Fenne, sub-treasurer of England.[
2 CONC 1] Margaret was born about 1444 at Scoulton Burdeleys in Norfolk.[citation needed]
2 CONT *His younger brother Sir Thomas Nevill was a trusted councillor of King Henry VIII and Speaker of the House of Commons. His youngest brother, the courtier Sir Edward Neville, was executed by Henry in 1538 for treason.
2 CONT *Neville fought against the Cornish rebels on 17 June 1497 at the Battle of Blackheath.[2] At the coronation of King Henry VIII in 1509, he held the office of Chief Larderer.[3] On 18 December 1512, King Henry VIII granted him the
2 CONC  castle and lands of Abergavenny.[4] From 1521 to 1522 he was imprisoned on suspicion of conspiring with his father-in-law, the Duke of Buckingham. At the coronation of Anne Boleyn in 1533, Nevill once again held the honour of Chi
2 CONC ef Larderer and was allowed to officiate.[2]
2 CONT *Neville was buried before 24 January 1536[citation needed] at Birling, Kent.[5] His heart was buried at Mereworth.[5]
2 CONT *Neville married firstly Joan FitzAlan (d. 14 November 1508), the daughter of Thomas FitzAlan, 17th Earl of Arundel, and Margaret Woodville (d. before 4 August 1492), second daughter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers. She was 
2 CONC a younger sister of Elizabeth Woodville, wife of Edward IV. According to Hawkyard, the marriage was childless; however according to Cokayne and Richardson, there were two daughters of the marriage:[6][5][7][8]
2 CONT **Elizabeth Neville, who married Henry Daubeney, 1st Earl of Bridgewater.[7]
2 CONT **Jane Neville, who married Henry Pole, 1st Baron Montagu, elder brother of Cardinal Reginald Pole, executed in 1539.[9]
2 CONT *He married secondly, before 5 September 1513, Margaret Brent, the daughter of John Brent of Charing, Kent,[10] and Anne Rosmoderes,[citation needed] by whom he had no issue.[11]
2 CONT *He married thirdly, about June 1519, Lady Mary Stafford, youngest daughter of Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham, by Lady Eleanor Percy, by whom he had three sons and five daughters:[12]
2 CONT **Henry Neville, 6th Baron Bergavenny.
2 CONT **John Neville.
2 CONT **Thomas Neville.
2 CONT **Mary Neville, who married firstly Thomas Fiennes, 9th Baron Dacre; secondly John Wotton; and thirdly **Francis Thursby, esquire.
2 CONT **'''Katherine Neville, who married Sir John St. Leger.
2 CONT **Margaret Neville, who married firstly John Cheney, and secondly Henry Poole, esquire.
2 CONT **Dorothy Neville (d.1559), who married, as his first wife, William Brooke, 10th Baron Cobham, by whom she had a daughter, Frances Brooke, who married firstly Thomas Coppinger (1546–1580), and secondly Edward Becher.[13]
2 CONT **Ursula Neville, who married Sir Warham St Leger.
2 CONT *He married fourthly his former servant, Mary Brooke alias Cobham, by whom he had a daughter whose name is unknown.[10]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Nevill,_5th_Baron_Bergavenny 
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT *'''Plantagenet Ancestry: A Study In Colonial And Medieval Families, 2nd Edition ... 
2 CONT *https://books.google.com/books?id=kjme027UeagC&printsec=frontcover&dq=Plantagenet+ancestry&hl=en&sa=X&ei=U8VLUYTME-boiALhroHwBw&ved=0CEcQ6AEwBA#v=onepage&q=andrew&f=false
