2 CONT *'''Dorothy NEVILLE
2 CONT *'''Born: ABT 1526/34, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, England
2 CONT *'''Died: 22 Sep 1559, Cobham, Kent, England
2 CONT *'''Buried: 3 Oct 1559, Cobham, Kent, England
2 CONT *Notes: '''married to William Brooke, later 10th Lord Cobham, when she was very young. The Brookes were the most important family in Kent and the Nevilles the second most important. It is unlikely they actually lived together befo
2 CONC re 1550, in part because William was so often out of the country. Most records agree that the marriage was not happy, although Dorothy did give her husband two children''', Dorothy and Frances. '''Dorothy lived at Cobham Hall with
2 CONC  her mother-in-law while her husband lived in London. Some accounts have them separated as early as 1553, but in 1554, when William was arrested and condemned for his part in Wyatt’s Rebellion, his wife is said to have been respon
2 CONC sible for his pardon. Certainly her brother''', Henry, by then Lord Bergavenny, interceded to secure Brooke’s release.
2 CONT *Father: George NEVILLE (3° B. Abergavenny)
2 CONT *Mother: Mary STAFFORD (B. Abergavenny)
2 CONT *'''Married: William BROOKE (5° B. Cobham) 4 Jun 1535 / 1550, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, Wales
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Dorothy BROOKE (Maid of Honour)
2 CONT **2. Frances BROOKE
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/NEVILLE4.htm#Dorothy NEVILLE1 ______________
2 CONT *George Neville or Nevill, 5th and de jure 3rd Baron Bergavenny KG, PC (c.1469 – 1535/6) was an English courtier. He held the office of Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports.
2 CONT *George Neville was born in Abergavenny, Monmouthshire,[citation needed] the son of George Neville, 4th Baron Bergavenny, by his first wife, Margaret Fenne (d. 28 September 1485), daughter of Hugh Fenne, sub-treasurer of England.[
2 CONC 1] Margaret was born about 1444 at Scoulton Burdeleys in Norfolk.[citation needed]
2 CONT *His younger brother Sir Thomas Nevill was a trusted councillor of King Henry VIII and Speaker of the House of Commons. His youngest brother, the courtier Sir Edward Neville, was executed by Henry in 1538 for treason.
2 CONT *Neville fought against the Cornish rebels on 17 June 1497 at the Battle of Blackheath.[2] At the coronation of King Henry VIII in 1509, he held the office of Chief Larderer.[3] On 18 December 1512, King Henry VIII granted him the
2 CONC  castle and lands of Abergavenny.[4] From 1521 to 1522 he was imprisoned on suspicion of conspiring with his father-in-law, the Duke of Buckingham. At the coronation of Anne Boleyn in 1533, Nevill once again held the honour of Chi
2 CONC ef Larderer and was allowed to officiate.[2]
2 CONT *Neville was buried before 24 January 1536[citation needed] at Birling, Kent.[5] His heart was buried at Mereworth.[5]
2 CONT *Neville married firstly Joan FitzAlan (d. 14 November 1508), the daughter of Thomas FitzAlan, 17th Earl of Arundel, and Margaret Woodville (d. before 4 August 1492), second daughter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers. She was 
2 CONC a younger sister of Elizabeth Woodville, wife of Edward IV. According to Hawkyard, the marriage was childless; however according to Cokayne and Richardson, there were two daughters of the marriage:[6][5][7][8]
2 CONT **Elizabeth Neville, who married Henry Daubeney, 1st Earl of Bridgewater.[7]
2 CONT **Jane Neville, who married Henry Pole, 1st Baron Montagu, elder brother of Cardinal Reginald Pole, executed in 1539.[9]
2 CONT *He married secondly, before 5 September 1513, Margaret Brent, the daughter of John Brent of Charing, Kent,[10] and Anne Rosmoderes,[citation needed] by whom he had no issue.[11]
2 CONT *He married thirdly, about June 1519, Lady Mary Stafford, youngest daughter of Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham, by Lady Eleanor Percy, by whom he had three sons and five daughters:[12]
2 CONT **Henry Neville, 6th Baron Bergavenny.
2 CONT **John Neville.
2 CONT **Thomas Neville.
2 CONT **Mary Neville, who married firstly Thomas Fiennes, 9th Baron Dacre; secondly John Wotton; and thirdly **Francis Thursby, esquire.
2 CONT **Katherine Neville, who married Sir John St. Leger.
2 CONT **Margaret Neville, who married firstly John Cheney, and secondly Henry Poole, esquire.
2 CONT **'''Dorothy Neville (d.1559), who married, as his first wife, William Brooke, 10th Baron Cobham, by whom she had a daughter''', Frances Brooke, who married firstly Thomas Coppinger (1546–1580), and secondly Edward Becher.[13]
2 CONT **Ursula Neville, who married Sir Warham St Leger.
2 CONT *He married fourthly his former servant, Mary Brooke alias Cobham, by whom he had a daughter whose name is unknown.[10]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Nevill,_5th_Baron_Bergavenny _______________
2 CONT *'''Sir William Brooke, 10th Baron Cobham, KG''' (1 November 1527 – 6 March 1597)[1] was Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports, and a Member of Parliament for Hythe. Although he was viewed by some as a religious radical during the Somer
2 CONC set protectorate, he entertained Elizabeth at Cobham Hall in 1559, signalling his acceptance of the moderate regime.
2 CONT *'''William Brooke''' was the son of George Brooke, 9th Baron Cobham (d. 29 September 1558), and Anne Braye (d. 1 November 1558).[2]
2 CONT *Brooke's father died in 1558 when he was just over thirty. '''Brooke married Dorothy Neville''', daughter of George Neville, 5th Baron Bergavenny[2] '''in 1545, but the marriage was unhappy, and they separated after 1553'''. Broo
2 CONC ke seems to have attended The King's School, Canterbury and Queens' College, Cambridge before 1544.[3] He spent much of his younger life in Europe. In the early 1540s he visited Padua. At the end of the decade he served in norther
2 CONC n France, where his father was in charge of Calais and in 1549 accompanied Paget's embassy to Brussels.
2 CONT *Like his father, Brooke sympathized with the anti-Marian nobles; he sided with the rebels during Wyatt's rebellion, and the intervention of his brother-in-law, Henry Nevill was needed to keep him from prison. In 1555 he served as
2 CONC  MP for Rochester.[2]
2 CONT *In the late 1550s, Brooke's opportunities expanded in a number of areas. His father died, making him Baron Cobham; '''his first wife died, leaving him free to marry''' Frances Newton (at Whitehall in 1560). He became Warden of th
2 CONC e Cinque Ports, a position in which he wielded great power over a large number of seats in Parliament. Most important, the accession of Elizabeth, and his close friendship with William Cecil made him a powerful noble. Elizabeth de
2 CONC puted him to inform Philip II of Mary's death. This embassy was only the first in a long series of missions and intrigues. Along with Cecil, he numbered among his friends some nobles, such as Thomas Howard and the Earl of Arundel,
2 CONC  whose loyalty to Elizabeth was far from certain. He suffered some months' house imprisonment as a result of a very tangential role in the Ridolfi plot. In 1578, he joined Francis Walsingham's failed mission to the Low Countries; 
2 CONC on this mission he presumably served as Cecil's agent. In the late 1580s, he helped John Whitgift search for the author of the Martin Marprelate tracts.
2 CONT *Brooke was made a Knight of the Garter on 14 April 1585, and appointed to the Privy Council by 12 February 1586.[2] He was involved in a minor capacity in the events that ended with the death of Mary, Queen of Scots. During the A
2 CONC rmada crisis, he was on a diplomatic mission to Alexander Farnese, Duke of Parma. By the early 1590s he had assumed a less active role in government. His daughter married Robert Cecil in 1589. His second wife died in 1592. He succ
2 CONC eeded Baron Hunsdon, as Lord Chamberlain in August 1596, and held the office until his death on 6 March 1597.[2]
2 CONT *'''William Brooke married firstly Dorothy Neville (d. 22 September 1559)''', daughter of George Nevill, 5th Baron Bergavenny, by his third wife, Mary Stafford, daughter of Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham,[2] '''by whom he
2 CONC  had a daughter''', Frances Brooke (b.1549), who married firstly Thomas Coppinger (1546–1580), and secondly Edward Becher (born c.1545).[4]
2 CONT *He married secondly Frances Newton, by whom he had four sons and three daughters:
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Brooke,_10th_Baron_Cobham _____________________
2 CONT *'''BROOKE, alias COBHAM, William (1527-97), of of the Blackfriars, London and Cobham, Kent.
2 CONT *b. 1 Nov. 1527, 1sts s. of George Brooke, 8th Lord Cobham, and bro. of George, Henry†, John† and Thomas†. educ. Padua 1542-5. '''m. (1) by 4 June 1535, Dorothy (d. 22 Sept. 1559), da. of George, 5th Lord Bergavenny, 1da.''', (2) 
2 CONC 25 Feb. 1560, Frances, da. of Sir John Newton alias Cradock of East Harptree Som. and Hanham, Glos., 4s. inc. Henry† and William†. Kntd. 1 Dec. 1548, KG nom. 23 Apr. 1584, inst. 15 Apr. 1585. suc. fa. as 10th Lord Cobham 29 Sept. 
2 CONC 1558.2
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **Esquire of the body by 1553; constable, Dover castle, Kent 16 Dec. 1558-d.; ld. warden, the Cinque Ports 16 Dec. 1558-d.; j.p. Kent 1558/59, q. 1561-d.; ld. lt. Kent 1559-d.; commr. Rochester bridge 1571; PC 1586-d.; keeper, Elt
2 CONC ham pk., Kent 1592-d., ld. chamberlain, the Household 8 Aug. 1596-7.3
2 CONT *Early in 1540 it was said that Lord Cobham had wanted to send two of his sons to the Continent with the Duke of Cleves’s chancellor but that Cromwell would not allow them to leave England; in the following year, with Cromwell rem
2 CONC oved from the scene, William Brooke was licensed to go abroad ‘for his further increase of virtue and learning’, taking with him two servants, three horses and £20 in money. He also took with him a number of ‘remembrances’ from hi
2 CONC s father, to say his prayers and hear mass devoutly, to remember his marriage vows, to obey his tutor and be diligent in his study of the civil law, rhetoric and Greek, and to write home as often as possible: these precepts the yo
2 CONC ung traveller subscribed, ‘I will perform all these things by the grace of God. By me, your son, William Brooke’.
2 CONT *Although he was sent to Padua to study, the outbreak of England’s war with France evidently made Brooke chafe at being in Italy, and in the autumn of 1545 he left for the Netherlands determined to take part in any action he might
2 CONC  find. Early in the following year he returned to England on a short visit and in November he was given a military appointment at Calais, where his father was deputy. The letter ordering the grant of this office was put forward by
2 CONC  Sir William Paget, and Lord Cobham called his son Paget’s servant in a letter of April 1547 asking Paget to agree to William’s offering his service to the King of France for a year. During the wars of Edward VI’s reign Brooke led
2 CONC  a band of 100 men, and in July 1550 he conveyed £7,000 from England to the treasurer of Calais. In the summer of 1551 he formed part of the embassy to France led by his brother-in-law the Marquess of Northampton, bringing home a 
2 CONC report from the ambassadors in June and returning to France with fresh instructions from the Council.4
2 CONT *Brooke was three days short of 20 when the Parliament of 1547 opened. That such a stripling should have caused one of the Cinque Ports to break (so far as is known for the first time under the Tudors) the ordinance confining thei
2 CONC r representation to residents is proof enough of Brooke’s enjoyment of powerful backing; apart from his father, a man of weight in Kent, his patron Paget is most likely to have procured his election, perhaps with the aid of the Pr
2 CONC otector Somerset himself. Brooke’s knighthood, conferred on 1 Dec. 1548 during the second session of the Parliament, suggests that he then stood well with the Protectoral regime, although his connexion with Northampton must later 
2 CONC have aligned him with Somerset’s rival Northumberland. He did not sit in the Parliament of March 1553, and it is not known whether he played any part in the succession crisis of that summer, but when six months later his uncle Sir
2 CONC  Thomas Wyatt II raised Kent against Queen Mary he was one of those implicated, although how deeply cannot be known; he pleaded not guilty to an indictment for treason, as did his younger brothers George and Thomas, and all three 
2 CONC were pardoned, while his father was let off at the cost of giving the Queen an obligation for £450. Brooke’s election 18 months later for Rochester, which had been the starting-point of the rebellion, could therefore have given li
2 CONC ttle satisfaction at court, and even less when towards the close of this Parliament he joined the opposition to one of the government’s bills. According to the Complete Peerage he received a writ of summons to the Lords for the se
2 CONC cond session of the following Parliament, that of 1558, but no trace of this summons has been found and he is not recorded as having taken his place in the Upper House until 1559.5
2 CONT *With the accession of Elizabeth, six weeks after Brooke’s own succession to his barony, new opportunities opened out for him. The first important task assigned to him was the distasteful one of announcing Mary’s death to King Phi
2 CONC lip in Brussels. He was appointed to succeed Sir Thomas Cheyne as lord warden of the Cinque Ports, placed on the commission of the peace for Kent, and in the following year made lord lieutenant of the county. Thenceforward, for ne
2 CONC arly 40 years, Lord Cobham was a busy servant of the crown, entrusted with the defence of Kent, the mustering of soldiers, the suppression of piracy, the staying of ships in times of embargo and a host of minor duties committed to
2 CONC  him as need arose by the Council. He twice went on embassies to the Netherlands, in 1578 with Sir Thomas Walsingham† and in 1588 with Thomas, 4th Earl of Derby. In 1586 he was sworn a member of the Privy Council and ten years lat
2 CONC er was made lord chamberlain. He was then only three months off his seventieth birthday, and he died in the following March at Cobham Hall, which he had enlarged with a new south wing and a north wing still unfinished at his death
2 CONC .6
2 CONT *He had made his will on 24 Feb. 1597, trusting ‘wholly and only’ by the merits of Christ’s passion to attain salvation and asking to be buried ‘after a laudable sort, and without vain pomp’, in the church at Cobham. His household
2 CONC  servants at Cobham and at the Blackfriars, London, were to receive an extra half year’s wages, and the houses to be delivered, ten days after his funeral, to his son and heir Henry Brooke. To Sir John Leveson†, Thomas Fane† and W
2 CONC illiam Lambarde†, three of his executors, Cobham left the site of the old college of Cobham on which they were to build a new college for poor people to live in; all his jewels, ornaments and plate, except those items which he bes
2 CONC towed otherwise in his will, were to be sold to pay his funeral expenses, debts and legacies and to provide the money for the perpetual maintenance of this almshouse. Cobham named as another executor his cousin, Sir Edward Wotton†
2 CONC , and desired his friend Lord Burghley, and his son-in-law Sir Robert Cecil†, to be overseers of his will.7
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/brooke-william-1527-97 ___________________
