1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland==
2 CONT '''Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland (1525–Aug 1564)[1] was an English peer, member of the House of Lords and Knight of the Garter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He inherited the earldom from Ralph Neville, 4th Earl of Westmorland and was succeeded by his son''' Charles Neville, 6th Earl of Westmorland. '''He married Anne Manners''', daughter of Thomas Manners, 1st Earl of Rutland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Their daughter''' Eleanor married William Pelham.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Neville,_5th_Earl_of_Westmorland
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________
2 CONT *'''Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland1
2 CONT *'''M, #14157, b. between 1524 and 1525, d. 10 February 1563/64
2 CONT *Last Edited=18 Jan 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.46%
2 CONT *'''     Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland was born between 1524 and 1525.1 He was the son''' of Ralph Neville, 4th Earl of Westmorland and Lady Catherine Stafford.1 '''He married, firstly, Lady Anne Manners''', daughter of T
2 CONC homas Manners, 1st Earl of Rutland and Eleanor Paston, '''on 3 July 1536 at Holywell, Shoreditch, London, England.2 He married, secondly, Jane Cholmley''', daughter of Sir Roger Cholmley and Katherine Constable, '''after 1549.2 He
2 CONC  married, thirdly, Margaret Cholmley''', daughter of Sir Roger Cholmley and Katherine Constable, '''before 21 June 1560.2 He died on 10 February 1563/64 at Kelvedon, Essex, England.3 His will (dated 18 August 1563) was proven (by 
2 CONC probate) on 22 September 1564 at York, Yorkshire, England.3 He was buried at Staindrop, County Durham, England.3
2 CONT *'''     He was styled as Lord Neville between 1525 and 1549.1 He was invested as a Knight on 30 September 1544 after the surrender of Boulogne.1 He held the office of Carver to the King in 1545.1 He fought in the the Earl of Here
2 CONC ford's great and successful raid into Scotland in September 1545.1 On 7 June 1546 he was attached to Lord Lisle's embassy to France for the negotiation of the Treay of Camp.1 On 1 October 1546 he was committed to the Fleet for plo
2 CONC tting to murder his father and wife (he was heavily in debt for gambling). His father was ordered on 24 March 1546/7 to take him back and pay his debts.1 He succeeded to the title of 5th Lord Neville [E., 1459] on 24 April 1549. H
2 CONC e gained the title of 5th Earl of Westmorland [E., 1397] on 24 April 1549.1 He succeeded to the title of 8th Lord Neville, of Raby [E., 1295] on 24 April 1549. He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) before 26 February 1550/5
2 CONC 1.4 He was Chief Commissioner to divide the debatable land between England and Scotland on 8 March 1551/52.4 He held the office of Lord-Lieutenant of the Bishopric of Durham on 16 May 1552.4 He was invested as a Knight, Order of t
2 CONC he Garter (K.G.) on 16 December 1552.4 On 16 June 1553 he was one of the 26 peers who agreed to settle the crown on Lady Jane Grey, but swiftly declared for Mary.5 He was commander of the the capture of Scarborough Castle in April
2 CONC  1557 then held in rebellion by Thomas Stafford.4 He was Chief Commissioner to treat with the commissioenrs of Mary, Queen of Scots on 25 May 1557.4 He was Captain General of the the Horse in the North on 30 May 1557.4 He held the
2 CONC  office of Steward of life of Galtres Forest, Yorkshire on 3 July 1557.4 He was Lieutenant-General of the the North between 22 January 1558 and December 1558.4 He was Commissioner to fortify the castle and town of Berwick on 11 Ju
2 CONC ne 1558.4 He was admitted to Gray's Inn on 22 January 1561/62.4
2 CONT *'''Children of Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland and Lady Anne Manners
2 CONT **Ralph Neville3 b. a 1542, d. b 1564
2 CONT **Charles Neville, 6th Earl of Westmorland+ b. bt 18 Aug 1542 - 28 Aug 1543, d. 16 Nov 1601
