1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The "Anne de La Guiche" mention in that 1920s article is a perfect example of a genealogical red herring. If we follow the biological trail, the "Motherhood Receipt" belongs to Françoise.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Biological Audit: The Mothers of the House of Schomberg
2 CONT To keep your database from crashing under the weight of three wives named "Anne" or "Françoise," here is the definitive breakdown of who actually gave birth to whom:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. Françoise d’Espinay (The Actual Biological Mother)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Role: 1st Wife of Henri de Schomberg (m. 1598).
2 CONT The "Receipt": She died in 1602.
2 CONT The Issue: She gave birth to Charles de Schomberg (the future 3rd Duke).
2 CONT The Logic: Because she died in 1602, and Charles was born around 1601, she is the only one who fits the biological timeline for the heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. Anne de La Guiche (The Daughter's Mother)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Role: 2nd Wife of Henri de Schomberg.
2 CONT The Issue: She gave birth to the daughters, most notably Jeanne de Schomberg (Duchess of Liancourt).
2 CONT The Confusion: Many sources call her the "biological mother" of the line, but specifically for the 3rd Duke, she was his step-mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. Anne d’Halluin (The Non-Biological "Title" Mother)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Role: 3rd Wife of Henri (and later wife of Charles).
2 CONT The Issue: None.
2 CONT The Role: She provided the Legal Filiation (the Duchy), not the DNA.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Succession Hierarchy
2 CONT Relationship to Charles (3rd Duke)	Name	                        Why they are in the file
2 CONT Biological Mother	                        Françoise d’Espinay	Provided the Schomberg/Espinay DNA.
2 CONT Step-Mother #1	                        Anne de La Guiche	        Intermediate wife (often confused in newspapers).
2 CONT Step-Mother #2 & Wife	                Anne d’Halluin	                Provided the Duc d'Halluin title.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Why the Newspaper called La Guiche the "Second Wife"'''
2 CONT The 1920 article you found was likely ignoring Françoise d'Espinay because she died so young (1602) and her marriage predated Henri's greatest fame. In the eyes of that specific journalist, the "Schomberg Era" began later, leading
2 CONC  them to mislabel Anne de La Guiche as the second wife and Anne d'Halluin as the third (or vice versa).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The "Cawley" Verdict'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Biological Fact: Charles = Schomberg + Espinay.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Legal Fact: Duke of Halluin = Schomberg + Halluin (via marriage).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Correction: Anne de La Guiche is a "collateral" figure in this specific Halluin quest. She didn't provide the blood for the Duke, nor the title for the Duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT
