1 NOTE She was queen of England for nine days.  When Lady Jane was 15 years old, 
2 CONT England's powerful lord chamberlain John Dudley, duke of Northumberland, 
2 CONT arranged a marriage for her with his son, Guildford Dudley (d. 1554). The 
2 CONT duke's purpose was to change, through Lady Jane, the royal succession upon 
2 CONT the death of the ailing young king, Edward VI, so that he could continue 
2 CONT to control the country through her. Edward approved the marriage and 
2 CONT secured witnesses to a deed declaring Lady Jane his successor. Upon the 
2 CONT death of the king, on July 6, 1553, Lady Jane was proclaimed queen, but 
2 CONT Edward's half sister, Mary Tudor, contested the succession. Lady Jane was 
2 CONT subsequently imprisoned in the Tower of London. She and her husband were 
2 CONT accused of treason, and both were beheaded on February 12, 1554.
1 NOTE also known as Queen Jane of England, was a claimant to the Kingdom of England and Kingdom of Ireland, who was de facto monarch of England for just over a week in 1553.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a grand-niece of Henry VIII of England. Under the influence of Jane's powerful father-in-law, John Dudley, 1st Duke of Northumberland, Henry VIII's son Edward VI of England willed the crown to Jane, his first cousin once r
2 CONC emoved, under a "Devise for the Succession,"[2] excluding from the line of succession his half-sisters, the future Mary I of England and Elizabeth I of England. On 6 July 1553, at the age of 15, Edward VI died of tuberculosis and 
2 CONC Jane was proclaimed Queen, in breach of the laws of England under the Third Succession Act of 1543.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Northumberland's efforts, within only nine days, Mary Tudor managed to find sufficient support for her claim to the throne, riding into London in a triumphal procession on 19 July. Parliament declared Mary the rightful Que
2 CONC en and denounced and revoked Jane's proclamation. Mary imprisoned Jane and her husband in the Gentleman Gaoler's apartments at the Tower of London. Northumberland was executed on 21 August 1553.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane and her husband Lord Guilford Dudley were charged with high treason,[3] and tried on 13 November 1553.[3] Both defendants were found guilty and sentenced to death;[3] Jane was ordered to "be burned alive on Tower Hill or behe
2 CONC aded as the Queen pleases."[4] Although initially her life was spared,[3] the Protestant rebellion of Thomas Wyatt the younger in January 1554 sealed Jane's fate, as Jane's father Henry Grey, 1st Duke of Suffolk and other nobles c
2 CONC alled for Jane's restoration as Queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Five days after Wyatt's arrest the execution of Jane and Guilford took place. Jane was taken to Tower Green inside the Tower of London and beheaded in private, the privilege of privacy granted on the orders of Queen Mary, as a ges
2 CONC ture of respect for her cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jane had a reputation as one of the most learned women of her day, and the historical writer Alison Weir describes her as one of "the finest female minds of the century". She is sometimes reckoned the first Queen regnant of E
2 CONC ngland.[5
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane received a comprehensive education, and studied Latin, Greek and Hebrew as well as contemporary languages. Through the teachings of her tutors, she became a committed Protestant. Jane had a difficult childhood. Even for the h
2 CONC arsher standards of the time, Frances Brandon was an abusive, cruel, and domineering woman who felt that Jane was weak and gentle and held her under a strict disciplinary regime.[7] Her daughter's meekness and quiet, unassuming ma
2 CONC nner irritated Frances who sought to 'harden' the child with regular beatings. Devoid of a mother's love and craving affection and understanding, Jane turned to books as solace and quickly mastered skills in the arts and languages
2 CONC .[
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward VI died on 6 July 1553. Four days later, Northumberland had Lady Jane Grey proclaimed Queen of England on 10 July 1553 - once she had taken up a secure residence in the Tower of London (English monarchs customarily resided 
2 CONC in the Tower from the time of accession until their coronation). Jane refused to name her husband Dudley as king by letters patent and deferred to Parliament. She offered to make him Duke of Clarence instead.
1 NOTE also known as Queen Jane of England, was a claimant to the Kingdom of England and Kingdom of Ireland, who was de facto monarch of England for just over a week in 1553.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a grand-niece of Henry VIII of England. Under the influence of Jane's powerful father-in-law, John Dudley, 1st Duke of Northumberland, Henry VIII's son Edward VI of England willed the crown to Jane, his first cousin once r
2 CONC emoved, under a "Devise for the Succession,"[2] excluding from the line of succession his half-sisters, the future Mary I of England and Elizabeth I of England. On 6 July 1553, at the age of 15, Edward VI died of tuberculosis and 
2 CONC Jane was proclaimed Queen, in breach of the laws of England under the Third Succession Act of 1543.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Northumberland's efforts, within only nine days, Mary Tudor managed to find sufficient support for her claim to the throne, riding into London in a triumphal procession on 19 July. Parliament declared Mary the rightful Que
2 CONC en and denounced and revoked Jane's proclamation. Mary imprisoned Jane and her husband in the Gentleman Gaoler's apartments at the Tower of London. Northumberland was executed on 21 August 1553.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane and her husband Lord Guilford Dudley were charged with high treason,[3] and tried on 13 November 1553.[3] Both defendants were found guilty and sentenced to death;[3] Jane was ordered to "be burned alive on Tower Hill or behe
2 CONC aded as the Queen pleases."[4] Although initially her life was spared,[3] the Protestant rebellion of Thomas Wyatt the younger in January 1554 sealed Jane's fate, as Jane's father Henry Grey, 1st Duke of Suffolk and other nobles c
2 CONC alled for Jane's restoration as Queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Five days after Wyatt's arrest the execution of Jane and Guilford took place. Jane was taken to Tower Green inside the Tower of London and beheaded in private, the privilege of privacy granted on the orders of Queen Mary, as a ges
2 CONC ture of respect for her cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jane had a reputation as one of the most learned women of her day, and the historical writer Alison Weir describes her as one of "the finest female minds of the century". She is sometimes reckoned the first Queen regnant of E
2 CONC ngland.[5
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane received a comprehensive education, and studied Latin, Greek and Hebrew as well as contemporary languages. Through the teachings of her tutors, she became a committed Protestant. Jane had a difficult childhood. Even for the h
2 CONC arsher standards of the time, Frances Brandon was an abusive, cruel, and domineering woman who felt that Jane was weak and gentle and held her under a strict disciplinary regime.[7] Her daughter's meekness and quiet, unassuming ma
2 CONC nner irritated Frances who sought to 'harden' the child with regular beatings. Devoid of a mother's love and craving affection and understanding, Jane turned to books as solace and quickly mastered skills in the arts and languages
2 CONC .[
