1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Margaret Willoughby1
2 CONT *F, #23704
2 CONT *Last Edited=3 Jun 2008
2 CONT *'''     Margaret Willoughby was the daughter of Henry Willoughby.1 She married Sir Matthew Arundell, son of Sir Thomas Arundell and Margaret Howard.2
2 CONT *'''      Her married name became Arundell.2
2 CONT *'''Children of Margaret Willoughby and Sir Matthew Arundell
2 CONT ** 1.Thomas Arundell, 1st Baron Arundell of Wardour+1 b. c 1560, d. 7 Nov 1639
2 CONT ** 2.William Arundell2 b. a 1560, d. 16 Feb 1592
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 263. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT * 2.[S21] L. G. Pine, The New Extinct Peerage 1884-1971: Containing Extinct, Abeyant, Dormant and Suspended Peerages With Genealogies and Arms (London, U.K.: Heraldry Today, 1972), page 9. Hereinafter cited as The New Extinct Peer
2 CONC age.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p2371.htm#i23704 ________________________
2 CONT *'''Margaret WILLOUGHBY
2 CONT *'''Born: 1538, Wollaton, Nottingham, England
2 CONT *'''Died: AFT 1578
2 CONT *'''Notes: upon the death of her father, Margaret and her younger brother Francis were sent to live in the household of her mother’s half brother, George Medley at Tilty in Essex and in the Minories, London. After Wyatt’s Rebellio
2 CONC n in 1554, the house in the Minories was searched and Medley was briefly in prison. Margaret’s uncle, Henry Grey, Duke of Suffolk, and her cousin, Lady Jane Grey, were executed at that time. Margaret seems to have joined the house
2 CONC hold of the widowed Duchess of Suffolk and been with her at the court of Queen Mary, although she was only eleven at the time. The Duchess was at court from Jul 1554 until May 1555. At Christmas 1555, still a very young girl to be
2 CONC  a maid of honor, Margaret joined the household of Elizabeth Tudor at Hatfield. It was while she was there that John Harington wrote his poem in praise of six of Elizabeth's gentlewomen. He calls Margaret "worthye willobe" and com
2 CONC ments upon her "pearcing eye". It is not clear if she stayed on after Elizabeth's household was reorganized by order of Queen Mary in Jun 1556. At fifteen or sixteen, in 1559 or 1560, Margaret married Matthew Arundell of Wardour. 
2 CONC On 16 Jul 1565, Margaret supped with her cousin, the Lady Mary Grey, and two other gentlewomen. At nine that evening, Mary married Thomas Keyes without the Queen’s permission. Margaret knew about the wedding but remained outside t
2 CONC he chamber where it was performed so that she could say she had not actually witnessed the exchange of vows. She resumed her friendship with her cousin after the Lady Mary was released from captivity and was mentioned in Mary’s wi
2 CONC ll in 1578. 
2 CONT *Father: Henry WILLOUGHBY (Sir Knight)
2 CONT *Mother: Anne GREY
2 CONT *'''Married: Matthew ARUNDELL HOWARD (Sir) 1559, Wollaton, Nottingham, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Catherine ARUNDELL HOWARD
2 CONT **2. Thomas ARUNDELL HOWARD (1° B. Arindell of Wardour)
2 CONT **3. William ARUNDELL HOWARD (d. 16 Feb 1592)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/WILLOUGHBY1.htm#Margaret WILLOUGHBY1 ____________________________
2 CONT *'''Margaret Willoughby1,2,3,4,5
2 CONT *'''F, #90231, b. circa 1538
2 CONT *Father      Sir Henry Willoughby1,2,3,4,6,5 b. c 1515, d. 17 Aug 1548
2 CONT *Mother     Anne Grey1,2,3,4,6,5 b. c 1519
2 CONT *'''     Margaret Willoughby was born circa 1538 at of Wollaton, Northamptonshire, England.1 A settlement for the marriage Margaret Willoughby and Sir Matthew Arundel, Burgess of Shaftesbury, Lord of Wardour was made on 20 Decembe
2 CONC r 1559; They had 2 sons (Sir Thomas, 1st Lord Arundell; & William, Esq.).1,2,3,4,6,5
2 CONT *'''Family      Sir Matthew Arundel, Burgess of Shaftesbury, Lord of Wardour b. bt 1532 - 1534, d. 24 Dec 1598
2 CONT *Child
2 CONT **Sir Thomas Arundel, 1st Lord Arundel of Wardour+1,2,4,6 b. c 1560, d. 7 Nov 1639
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 182.
2 CONT * 2.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. I, p. 44-45.
2 CONT * 3.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. II, p. 234.
2 CONT * 4.[S6] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry: 2nd Edition, Vol. I, p. 465.
2 CONT * 5.[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. III, p. 42.
2 CONT * 6.[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. II, p. 170.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p3003.htm#i90231 ______________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Matthew Arundell of Wardour Castle in Wiltshire (ca. 1533 – 24 December 1598), known between 1552 and 1554 as Matthew Howard and after his death sometimes called Matthew Arundell-Howard, was an English gentleman, landowner
2 CONC , and member of parliament in the West of England.
2 CONT *Although the ancestor of a family of Roman Catholic recusants, Arundell himself conformed to the Church of England.
2 CONT *A member of the ancient knightly family of Arundell of Cornwall, Arundell was the son of Sir Thomas Arundell (attainted and executed in 1552) and of Margaret Howard (died 1571), a sister of Queen Catherine Howard. His maternal gr
2 CONC andparents were Lord Edmund Howard (died 1539), the third son of Thomas Howard, 2nd Duke of Norfolk, and Joyce Culpeper (c. 1480–1531). His great aunt Elizabeth, Countess of Wiltshire, was the mother of Anne Boleyn, who was thus t
2 CONC he first cousin of Arundell's mother as well as being the mother of Queen Elizabeth I.[1] He was a descendant of the 11th century landowner Roger de Arundell, who possessed .... etc.
2 CONT *Arundell had been contracted to marry Katherine, one of the daughters of Thomas Wriothesley, 1st Earl of Southampton, but in the event she married Sir Thomas Cornwallis.[6] '''In 1559 Arundell married Margaret Willoughby, a daugh
2 CONC ter of Sir Henry Willoughby, of Wollaton, Nottinghamshire, and of Anne Grey (died 1548), daughter of Thomas Grey, 2nd Marquess of Dorset and Margaret Wotton. As a child Margaret and her sister and brother had been taken in by Henr
2 CONC y Grey, 3rd Marquess of Dorset, and his wife, Frances Brandon, after their father was slain in the suppression of Kett's Rebellion in 1549, and had grown up with Dorset's daughters, Lady Jane Grey, Lady Catherine Grey, and Lady Ma
2 CONC ry Grey. Margaret was present at Mary Grey's secret marriage on 16 July 1565 to the Queen's serjeant porter, Thomas Keyes, and was bequeathed a tankard of gold and silver in Mary Grey's will. As a young lady Margaret had previousl
2 CONC y served in the household of Princess Elizabeth at Hatfield House.[7]
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Arundell's main seat, Wardour Castle, had been held by knight service of the Earl of Pembroke, so had escheated to Pembroke in 1552. In 1570 Arundell was able to buy it back to live in, together with the manor of Sutto
2 CONC n Mandeville, and the next year Lord Pembroke granted him the site of Shaftesbury Abbey.[8] As well as living at Wardour, the Arundells kept a town house in London.[2] '''They had two sons.[1] .... etc.
2 CONT *His brother Charles Arundell (died 1587) was openly a recusant and fled the country after the Babington Plot. In the 1580s he was seen as a leader of the English Roman Catholic exiles in France.[11] '''Arundell's own elder son wa
2 CONC s imprisoned as a suspected Imperial spy, but Arundell himself conformed to the Church of England. In 1588, Arundel was one of a small number of knights considered for a peerage on account of "great possessions". The following yea
2 CONC r he was appointed a Deputy Lieutenant for Wiltshire.[10]
2 CONT *'''Sir John Harington (1561–1612), a courtier often claimed as the inventor of the water closet, reported an occasion at Wardour in the early 1590s at which a conversation about sanitation first prompted his interest in the subje
2 CONC ct. Apart from Harington, those present were Arundell and his son Thomas, Thomas's wife, Mary, her brother Henry Wriothesley, 3rd Earl of Southampton, and Sir Henry Danvers. However, fifty years later Wardour Castle still depended
2 CONC  on medieval garderobes as privies.[12]
2 CONT *'''In his final months Arundell was in pain from bladder stones. Following his death on 24 December 1598 he was buried at the parish church of Tisbury. In his Will, proved on 6 February 1598/99, he gave £2,000 – at the time an en
2 CONC ormous sum, equal to almost twice the annual income of his more powerful connection the Earl of Southampton[13] – to the poor.[10][14]As Custos Rotulorum of Dorset he was succeeded by Sir Walter Raleigh.
2 CONT *'''Arundell's son Thomas distinguished himself in battle against the Turks in the service of the Emperor Rudolf II, who created him a Count of the Holy Roman Empire. This foreign title annoyed Queen Elizabeth, who in 1597 impriso
2 CONC ned Thomas Arundell in the Fleet as a suspected Roman Catholic spy, but nothing could be proved against him and Thomas was soon released into Arundell's custody.[15] In 1605, some years after Arundell's death in 1598, his elder so
2 CONC n was summoned to the House of Lords by King James I as Baron Arundell of Wardour[1] and was briefly suspected of being one of the Gunpowder Plot conspirators.[9]
2 CONT *The title survived until the death of John Arundell, 16th Baron Arundell of Wardour (1907–1944), when it became extinct.[16]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthew_Arundell _______________________
2 CONT *Thomas Arundell, 1st Baron Arundell of Wardour (ca. 1560 – 7 November 1639) was the eldest son of '''Sir Matthew Arundell of Wardour Castle in Wiltshire (ca. 1532/34–24 December 1598), and Margaret Willoughby''', the daughter of 
2 CONC Sir Henry Willoughby, of Wollaton, Nottinghamshire. He distinguished himself in battle against the Ottoman Turks in the service of the Emperor Rudolf II, and was created a Count of the Holy Roman Empire. His assumption of the titl
2 CONC e displeased Queen Elizabeth, who refused to recognize it, and imprisoned him in the Fleet. In 1605 Arundell was created 1st Baron Arundell of Wardour. In the same year he was briefly suspected of complicity in the Gunpowder Plot.
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Arundell (ca. 1560 – 7 November 1639) was the eldest son of '''Sir Matthew Arundell of Wardour Castle in Wiltshire (ca. 1532/34–24 December 1598), a member of the ancient family of Arundell of Cornwall, and Margaret Wi
2 CONC lloughby, the daughter of Sir Henry Willoughby, of Wollaton, Nottinghamshire. His father inherited extensive former monastic lands, and served in a number of administrative capacities, including high sheriff, custos rotulorum, and
2 CONC  Deputy Lieutenant of Dorset. In her youth his mother served for several years in the household of Princess Elizabeth at Hatfield.[1] Arundell's paternal grandparents were Sir Thomas Arundell (executed on 26 February 1552) and Mar
2 CONC garet Howard (ca. 1515-10 October 1571), sister of Queen Catherine Howard.[2]
2 CONT *In 1580 Arundell was imprisoned for  .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Arundell,_1st_Baron_Arundell_of_Wardour _______________________
