1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Anne Dennis'''1
2 CONT F, #219270
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Jan 2017
2 CONT '''Anne Dennis is the daughter''' of Sir Robert Dennis.2 '''She married Sir John Chichester,''' son of Sir John Chichester.1
2 CONT '''Her married name became Chichester.'''
2 CONT '''Children of Anne Dennis and Sir John Chichester'''
2 CONT 1. Sir Robert Chichester+2 d. 24 Apr 1624
2 CONT 2. Elizabeth Chichester+3 d. 26 Jan 1656/57
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1. [S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 774. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT 2. [S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT 3. [S18] Matthew H.C.G., editor, Dictionary of National Biography on CD-ROM (Oxford, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1995). Hereinafter cited as Dictionary of National Biography.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p21927.htm#i219270
2 CONT ________
2 CONT Sir Robert Dennis, JP (died 1592) of Holcombe Burnell in Devon, was a Member of Parliament for Devon in 1555 and served as Sheriff of Devon.
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT Robert Dennis was the eldest son of Sir Thomas Denys (c. 1477 – 1561) of Holcombe Burnell,[1] Sheriff of Devon nine times between 1507/8 to 1553/4 and Member of Parliament for Devon who acquired large estates in Devon at the Disso
2 CONC lution of the Monasteries. In 1577 he was commissioned as one of two Colonels of the East Division of the Devon Trained Bands.[2] His mother (his father's second wife) was Elizabeth Donne, a daughter of Sir Angel Donne,[3] an Alde
2 CONC rman of London, by his wife Anne Hawarden (alias Hawardine), of Cheshire, and widow of Thomas Murfyn,[3] Lord Mayor of London.
2 CONT Dennis married twice. His first marriage was some time before 4 April 1552 to Mary Blount, the second daughter of William Blount, 4th Baron Mountjoy,[4] and first cousin to Lady Jane Grey, the Nine Day Queen of England.[a] Their c
2 CONC hildren were:
2 CONT *'''Anne Dennis, wife of Sir John Chichester (died 1586) of Raleigh in the parish of Pilton, North Devon, Sheriff of Devon in 1585''' and elder brother of Edward Chichester, 1st Viscount Chichester, of Eggesford, Devon. '''Her gra
2 CONC ndson''' was Sir John Chichester, 1st Baronet (died 1667).[5]
2 CONT *Gertrude Dennis, who married firstly (as his second wife) John Arundell (died 1580) of Trerice, Cornwall, Member of Parliament for Mitchell, Cornwall, in 1555 and 1558, and High Sheriff of Cornwall in 1573–1574. She married secon
2 CONC dly Edward Parker, 12th Baron Morley (c. 1550 – 1618)[6] of Norfolk.
2 CONT His second marriage, at some time before 12 October 1555, was to Margaret Godolphin, a daughter and co-heiress[7] of Sir William Godolphin of Godolphin in Cornwall. By her he had three sons and five daughters including:[6]
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Denys, eldest son and heir, who married Anne Paulet, daughter of William Paulet, 3rd Marquess of Winchester,[6] but died without male children leaving two daughters and co-heiresses:
2 CONT **Anne Denys, heiress of Bicton, who married Sir Henry Rolle (died 1616) of Stevenstone in Devon, an ancestor of John Rolle, 1st Baron Rolle (died 1842) and Barons Clinton, the latter of whom restored Livery Dole after the World W
2 CONC ar II bomb damage. The manors of Bicton, Littleham, Exmouth and East Budleigh passed thereupon to the Rolle family, with whose descendant Baron Clinton, much remains today in 2015, held by Clinton Devon Estates.[8]
2 CONT **Margaret Denys (died 1632), heiress of Holcombe Burnell. In 1613 she married Sir Arthur Mainwaring of Ightfield, Shropshire, who was Carver to Prince Henry, the eldest son of King James I.[9] He sold Holcombe Burnell.[7]
2 CONT Their other children included Arthur; James; Elizabeth, who was the wife successively of John Stewer, Sir Thomas Acton and Gilbert Blackaller; Margaret; Jane, who married Sir ... Fowks; Phillipa (died 1655), who married William Dr
2 CONC ake (died 1625) of Wiscomb; and Mary, who died childless.[6]
2 CONT Dennis died in 1592 and according to W. G. Hoskins, the Easter Sepulchre monument in Holcombe Burnell church was used as his tomb.[10]
2 CONT Career
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Dennis_(MP)
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT '''Sir John Chichester''' (died 31 March 1586) lord of the manor of Raleigh in the parish of Pilton, near Barnstaple, North Devon, was Sheriff of Devon in 1576/7[1] and/or in 1585[2] and died of gaol fever contracted whilst acting
2 CONC  as a magistrate at the Lent Black Assizes of Exeter in 1586.
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT '''John Chichester was the eldest son and heir''' of Sir John Chichester (1519/20–1569)[2] of Raleigh, a leading member of the Devonshire gentry. His mother was Gertrude Courtenay, a daughter of Sir William Courtenay (1477–1535) o
2 CONC f Powderham.[3]
2 CONT He had at least six brothers, three of whom became Governor of Carrickfergus: Sir John Chichester, junior, who was killed at the Battle of Carrickfergus (1597); Arthur Chichester, 1st Baron Chichester (1563–1624/5), who succeeded 
2 CONC him; and Edward Chichester, 1st Viscount Chichester (1568–1648) of Eggesford, Devon,[2][4] who became governor on Arthur's death.
2 CONT '''Marriage and children'''
2 CONT '''John Chichester married Anne Dennis, the eldest daughter''' of Sir Robert Dennis (died 1592), of Holcombe Burnell,[2] in Devon, by his wife Mary Blount who was one of the two daughters and co-heiresses of William Blount, 4th Ba
2 CONC ron Mountjoy (c. 1478–1534), KG,[5] and a first cousin of Lady Jane Grey.[6] '''Their children included:'''
2 CONT *Sir Robert Chichester (1579–1627), eldest son and heir (whose kneeling effigy and monument survives in Pilton Church), father of Sir John Chichester, 1st Baronet (1626-1667).[2]
2 CONT *John Chichester (1605–1627), second son, died childless aged 22.[2]
2 CONT *Mary Chichester, eldest daughter, who married twice, firstly to Thomas Francis of Combe Florey in Somerset; secondly to John Smith of Tregonnacke in Cornwall.[2]
2 CONT *Gertrude Chichester, second daughter, wife of Amias Copleston (1581/2–1621) of Warleigh in the parish of Tamerton Foliot in Devon, and lord of the Manor of Copleston in Devon.
2 CONT *Elizabeth Chichester (died 26 January 1656/7), third daughter, who married William Coryton (1580–1651). She was the mother of Sir John Coryton, 1st Baronet (1621–1680).
2 CONT Death
2 CONT '''Chichester died in 1586''' of gaol fever contracted whilst serving as a magistrate at the Lent Black Assizes of Exeter in 1586,[2] which accounted for the deaths of many people, including several other prominent Devonshire magi
2 CONC strates and visiting circuit judges.
2 CONT References
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Chichester_(died_1586)
2 CONT _________
2 CONT '''John CHICHESTER of Raleigh (Sir Knight)'''
2 CONT Born: 25 Feb 1548/9
2 CONT Died: 31 Mar 1586
2 CONT Notes: Sheriff of Devon 1586. Killed with the judge of assize and others by an infectious smell from the prisoners at the Lent assizes in Exter Castle, 1585.
2 CONT Father: John CHICHESTER (Sir Knight)
2 CONT Mother: Gertrude COURTENAY
2 CONT '''Married: Anne DENYS''' (dau. of Sir Robert Denys of Holcombe Burnel and Mary Blount)
2 CONT '''Children''':
2 CONT 1. Robert CHICHESTER (Sir)
2 CONT 2. Gertrude CHICHESTER
2 CONT 3. Mary CHICHESTER
2 CONT 4. Elizabeth CHICHESTER
2 CONT 5. John CHICHESTER
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/CHICHESTER.htm#John%20CHICHESTER%20of%20Raleigh%20(Sir%20Knight)2
2 CONT __________
2 CONT Sir Robert DENYS of Holcombe Burnel and Bicton, Knight
2 CONT Born: BEF 153
2 CONT Died: 1593
2 CONT Buried: Holcombe Burnel
2 CONT Father: Thomas DENYS of Holcombe Burnel (Sir)
2 CONT Mother: ¿?
2 CONT Married 1: Mary BLOUNT 4 Apr 1552
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT '''1. Anne DENYS (m. Sir John Chichester)'''
2 CONT 2. Gertrude DENYS (B. Morley) (m1. Edward Parker, 3° B. Morley - m2. Sir John Arundell of Trerice)
2 CONT Married 2: Margaret GODOLPHIN (dau. of Sir William Godolphin of Godolphin) 12 Oct 1555
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 3. Thomas DENYS of Holcombe Rogus (Sir) (See his Biography)
2 CONT 4. Son DENYS
2 CONT 5. Son DENYS
2 CONT 6. Dau. DENYS
2 CONT 7. Dau. DENYS
2 CONT 8. Dau. DENYS
2 CONT 9. Dau. DENYS
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/Bios/RobertDenys.htm
2 CONT __________
2 CONT Mary BLOUNT
2 CONT Died: BEF Oct 1555
2 CONT Father: William BLOUNT (4° B. Mountjoy)
2 CONT Mother: Dorothy GREY (B. Willoughby of Broke)
2 CONT Married: Robert DENYS of Holcombe Burnel (Sir Knight) (See his Biography) 4 Apr 1552
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT '''1. Anne DENYS (m. Sir John Chichester)'''
2 CONT 2. Gertrude DENYS (B. Morley) (m1. Edward Parker, 3° B. Morley - m2. Sir John Arundell of Trerice)
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BLOUNT1.htm#Mary%20BLOUNT1
2 CONT ________
2 CONT Sir John Chichester (1519/20 – 1569) of Raleigh in the parish of Pilton, near Barnstaple in North Devon, was a leading member of the Devonshire gentry, a naval captain, and ardent Protestant who served as Sheriff of Devon in 1550-
2 CONC 1551, and as Knight of the Shire for Devon in 1547, April 1554, and 1563, and as Member of Parliament for Barnstaple in 1559, over which borough his lordship of the manor of Raleigh, Pilton had considerable influence.
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT The Chichester family had been seated at the manor of Raleigh since the mid-14th. century. He was the son of Edward Chichester (died 27 July 1526) of Great Torrington, who predeceased his own father, also Sir John (1474-1537), by 
2 CONC his wife Lady Elizabeth Bourchier (died 1548), whose small monumental brass exists in St Brannock's Church, Braunton, a daughter of John Bourchier, 1st Earl of Bath (1470–1539) whose seat was at Tawstock Court, 3 miles south of Ra
2 CONC leigh. In the 16th and 17th centuries these two houses, Raleigh and the new Tawstock Court built in 1574, were probably the largest in North Devon.[1] He succeeded his grandfather in 1536.[2]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT He married Gertrude Courtenay, a daughter of Sir William III Courtenay (1477–1535) "The Great",[5] of Powderham, Devon, MP for Devon in 1529, thrice Sheriff of Devon, in 1522, 1525-6, 1533-4, an Esquire of the Body to King Henry V
2 CONC III, whom he accompanied to the Field of the Cloth of Gold.[6] He was 6th in descent from Hugh Courtenay, 2nd Earl of Devon (died 1377), and his own grandson William Courtenay (1527–1557) of Powderham became himself de jure 2nd Ea
2 CONC rl of Devon under the 1553 creation of that title. The arms of Courtenay quartered with Redvers appear amongst the many heraldic escutcheons shown on Chichester's monument in Pilton Church. He had by her seven sons and nine daught
2 CONC ers,[7] who married into many of the leading gentry families of Devonshire, two of them marrying children of first cousins of Lady Jane Grey (1536/1537-1554), The Queen of Nine Days.[8] The marriages of his children are represente
2 CONC d heraldically on a panel on his monument in Pilton Church.
2 CONT Heraldic Panel
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT He had the following 7 sons:[10]
2 CONT *'''Sir John Chichester, senior''' (died 31 March 1586), eldest son and heir,[11] of Raleigh, '''who married Ann Dennis, the eldest daughter''' of Sir Robert Dennis (died 1592), of Holcombe Burnell,[12] in Devon, by his wife Mary 
2 CONC Blount who was one of the two daughters and co-heiresses of William Blount, 4th Baron Mountjoy (c. 1478–1534), KG,[13] and a first cousin of Queen Lady Jane Grey.[14]
2 CONT *Arthur Chichester, 1st Baron Chichester of Belfast (1563-1624/5), 2nd son, who succeeded his brother '''Sir John as Governor of Carrickfergus.'''
2 CONT * etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Chichester_(died_1569)
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''The Visitations of the County of Devon: Comprising the Herald's Visitations ... By College of Arms (Great Britain)'''
2 CONT https://books.google.com/books?id=GmqlIibS95IC&pg=PA721&lpg=PA721&dq=John+Affeton+1467&source=bl&ots=whHzN1T59P&sig=JK0PGjYO2QMFN8xcLw9t13hbOcM&hl=en&ei=b2rwTY2cBJGisQPhg_ShDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=3&ved=0CCIQ6AEwAj
2 CONC hG#v=onepage&q=dennis&f=false
2 CONT Pg. 279
