1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anne Sutton (sometimes called Agnes) was only child of Edward Sutton, 4th baron Dudley (d. July 9, 1586) by his first wife, Catherine Brydges (c.1524-April 1566). Her marriage contract is dated June 3, 1571 but the
2 CONC  actual date of her marriage to Francis Throckmorton of Feckenham, Worcestershire and Throckmorton House at Paul's Wharf, London (1554-x.July 10, 1584) is unknown. They had one son, John (d.1604+). Francis Throckmorton was involve
2 CONC d with treasonous plots and was arrested at his London house on November 5, 1583. Anne is credited by some sources with taking a casket covered in green velvet from under his bed and spiriting it out of the house before it could b
2 CONC e confiscated. Later testimony indicated that her maid, Elizabeth Cooke, gave it to another servant, John Throckmorton, with instructions to take it to a safe place. He took it to a tailor named Russems, who lodged in Cheapside. T
2 CONC he following day, it was passed on to another tailor,  Haddocke, in St. Paul's Churchyard, and from there went to one of the Spanish ambassador's servants. John Bossy's account credits a housemaid and a priest named John Meredith 
2 CONC with the rescue of the casket. The casket contained letters recently received from Paris for Mary Queen of Scots. During Throckmorton's imprisonment in the Tower of London, he managed to smuggle out at least one letter to his wife
2 CONC . She received it on Friday, December 13, 1583. It opened with the words "my good sweetheart." On December 18, Anne and her sister were questioned about this letter, which they claimed was only a request for linen and bedding. Ann
2 CONC e was allowed to visit her husband in the Tower. Bossy says she and his mother persuaded him to confess in June 1584. Her second husband was Thomas Wilmer (d.1628), a barrister, by whom she had Thomas, John, Mary, and Ursula.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.kateemersonhistoricals.com/TudorWomenSo-Sy.htm
