2 CONT Walter Devereux, 7th Baron Ferrers of Chartley
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Walter Devereux, Lord Ferrers of Chartley)
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Devereux, jure uxoris 7th Baron Ferrers of Chartley (c. 1431 - 22 August 1485) was a minor member of the English peerage and a loyal supporter of the Yorkist cause during the Wars of the Roses. He was killed at the Battle o
2 CONC f Bosworth Field, fighting for Richard III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Career
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Death
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter was born in Weobley, Herefordshire. His parents were Walter Devereux, Lord Chancellor of Ireland from 1449 to about 1451 and his wife Elizabeth Merbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was the daughter and heiress of Sir John Merbury, Chief Justice of South Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of only thirteen, Walter married Anne Ferrers, 7th Baroness Ferrers of Chartley - who predeceased him by seventeen years - and became Baron Ferrers of Chartley in right of his wife in 1462. They had at least five childr
2 CONC en:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Devereux, 8th Baron Ferrers of Chartley (1463 - 3 May 1501).
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Devereux. Married first Sir Richard Corbet of Morton Corbet and secondly Sir Thomas Leighton of Watlesborough.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel "Sybil" Devereux. Married Sir James Baskerville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard Devereux.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Devereux.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Career
2 CONT
2 CONT As a Yorkist, he held many offices under Edward IV. In 1461 and 1469, Walter was commissioner of array for Herefordshire, Shropshire and Gloucestershire, as well as Worcestershire in 1469. He also served in 1470 and 1471, as joint
2 CONC  commissioner of array for Herefordshire, and then again in 1474 for Herefordshire and Shropshire. Walter's military career was also thorough. He fought at Towton in 1461, where he was made a Knight of the Garter. He also fought a
2 CONC t Barnet and Tewkesbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Devereux supported Richard III of England during his reign, and fought on his side at the Battle of Bosworth Field (22 August 1485). There, Lord Ferrers commanded in the vanguard under John Howard, Duke of Norfolk, alongsid
2 CONC e Sir Robert Brackenbury and Thomas Howard, Earl of Surrey. Devereux was slain during the initial fight with the opposing van under John de Vere, 13th Earl of Oxford, fighting next to the young John, Lord Zouche. An in-law, Sir Jo
2 CONC hn Ferrers, was also killed at Bosworth.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Walter Devereux, 5th Viscount Hereford
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Walter Devereux, 5th Viscount Hereford, 2nd Baronet of Castle Bromwich (c. 1575 - 1656), was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons at various times, between 1614 and 1624, before succeeding to the family Viscou
2 CONC ntcy in the peerage of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Sir Edward Devereux, of Castle Bromwich, Warwickshire, and his wife Catherine née Arden, he was grandson of Sir Walter Devereux, cr. Viscount Hereford in 1550. He succeeded his father in 1622 as 2nd baronet, later inher
2 CONC iting in 1646 the title of his first cousin twice removed as Viscount Hereford.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Devereux previously served as Member of Parliament (MP) for Stafford in 1614, before being elected in 1621 as MP for Marlborough, and in 1624 as MP for Worcestershire then in 1625 MP for Worcester.[2] He also served as High Sherif
2 CONC f of Worcestershire in 1625.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Robert Devereux, 3rd Earl of Essex died in 1646, Sir Walter succeeded as 5th Viscount Hereford, but not to his earldom to which he was not in remainder and which became extinct. Lord Hereford was appointed as Lord Lieutenant 
2 CONC of Monmouthshire in 1646 and became a member of the Radnorshire Militia Committee in 1648.
