1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''''''Jaine (Jane) Stanley (c.1572–1622): A Gentlewoman of the Senior Stanley Cadet Line
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Jaine Stanley was born into one of the most distinguished families in Lancashire, a gentlewoman whose ancestry reached directly into the heart of the medieval English nobility. Though she lived her life far from the great ho
2 CONC uses of her forebears, her bloodline carried the legacy of the Earls of Derby, the Barons Strange, and the powerful network of families that shaped the politics of late‑medieval England.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Her great‑grandfather was George Stanley, 9th Baron Strange, heir to the vast Stanley estates and eldest son of Thomas Stanley, 1st Earl of Derby. George’s early death in 1503 — one year before his father — altered the cours
2 CONC e of the family’s succession. Because he predeceased the 1st Earl, the earldom passed not to him but to his own son, the future 2nd Earl of Derby. This single event created a permanent division within the Stanley dynasty: the main
2 CONC  line, which inherited the title, and the senior cadet line, descended from George’s younger sons''''''.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Jaine belonged to that cadet branch.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Her grandfather, Sir James Stanley of Cross Hall and Bickerstaffe, was one of those younger sons — a man of status, land, and arms, but not the heir to the earldom. His descendants formed the leading gentry family of the Orm
2 CONC skirk–Bickerstaffe district, closely connected to other prominent Lancashire houses such as the Scarisbricks, Norrises, Molyneuxes, and Heskeths. It was into this world that Jaine was born: a world of modest estates, local influen
2 CONC ce, and deep ancestral prestige.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Around 1593, Jaine married Gabriel Hesketh, Gentleman, at St Peter and St Paul’s Church in Ormskirk. The match was entirely in keeping with the social expectations of the time: a union between two'''''' ''''''long‑establishe
2 CONC d'''''' '''gentry families whose lands and histories intertwined across south‑west Lancashire. The Heskeths were respected, armigerous, and well‑connected — a suitable household for a woman of Jaine’s lineage.''''''''''''''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Their first known child, Margaret Hesketh, was baptised in January 1596/7. Margaret would later become the administratrix of her father’s troubled estate, a role thrust upon her at the age of eighteen after Jaine formally re
2 CONC nounced the executorship of Gabriel’s will in December 1615. This renunciation — a common act when an estate was burdened with debts or legal complications — suggests that Jaine faced the difficult realities of widowhood in a hous
2 CONC ehold under financial strain. Yet her daughter’s appointment as administratrix also reflects the family’s continued standing: even in adversity, the Heskeths remained within the orbit of the Chester Consistory Court and the legal 
2 CONC structures of the gentry.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Jaine lived long enough to see her children reach adulthood, but she died in 1622, leaving behind a legacy that far exceeded the modest scale of her daily life. Through her, the blood of the Stanleys of Bickerstaffe — and th
2 CONC rough them, the Earls of Derby, the Goushills, the FitzAlans, the Mowbrays, and the Howards — passed into the Baldwin and Banks families of later generations.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''In genealogical terms, Jaine is the gateway ancestor: the point at which a fully documented Lancashire line intersects with the great noble houses of medieval England. Her life stands at the crossroads of two worlds — the lo
2 CONC cal and the legendary — and it is through her that the long arc of English aristocratic history enters your family story.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT When Jaine Scarisbrick Stanley was born in 1572, her father, Henry, was 57, and her mother, Margaret, was 36. She married Gabriel Bartholomew Hawarth Hesketh on 30 October 1593 in Ormskirk, Lancashire. They had '''six children''' 
2 CONC in 14 years. She died on 17 November 1622 in Ormskirk, Lancashire, at the age of 50, and was buried there in the Stanley Chancel at the Church of St Peter and St Paul.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jaine's parents were married on 26 Sep 1563 at the Church of St Peter and St Paul in Ormskirk, Lancashire, England, This was the marriage of Henrie Stanley, Esquire, and Mrs. Margreat Stanley.
2 CONT Register: Marriages 1557 - 1626, Page 188, Entry 3 Source: LDS Film 1849663; Marriage: 30 Oct 1593 &bull; Ormskirk, Lancashire, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 Oct 1593 at St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, the marriage of Gabriell Hesketh, Gent[ry] - Jaine Stanley, Gent[ry] occured. Register: Marriages 1557 - 1626, Page 228, Entry 3 Source: LDS Film 1849663; Death: 17 Nov
2 CONC  1622 &bull; Ormskirk, Lancashire,England; Burial: Ormskirk, West Lancashire District, Lancashire, England, Burial: 17 Nov 1622 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Hesketh, Widow -
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Notes: gen. [generosa - latin for of noble birth] - bur in Mr Stanley his chancell
2 CONT Register: Burials 1557 - 1625/6, Page 423, Entry 5 Source: LDS Film 1849663''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jaine Scarisbrick Stanley was born in 1572 to Margaret Mary Stanley, age 36, and Henry Stanley, age 57 at Aughton Hall Ormskirk, Lancashire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Edward was born on 14 December 1576 in Bickerstaffe, Lancashire, when Jaine Scarisbrick was 4 years old. Edward Stanley Baron of Bickerstaffe 1576–1640
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother James was born in 1580 in Ormskirk, Lancashire, when Jaine Scarisbrick was 8 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her sister Anne died in 1591 in Denbighshire when Jaine Scarisbrick was 19 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her half-sister Ursula died in 1591 in Denbighshire when Jaine Scarisbrick was 19 years old, at Llewenni Hall, Denbighshire, Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jaine Scarisbrick Stanley married Gabriel Bartholomew Hawarth Hesketh in Ormskirk, Lancashire, on 30 October 1593 when she was 21 years old at St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriell Hesketh, Gent[ry] and Jaine Stanley, Gent[ry] - Register: Marriages 1557 - 1626, Page 228, Entry 3 Source: LDS Film 1849663.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their daughter '''Margaret Ann''' was born on 27 January 1596 in Ormskirk, Lancashire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father Henry Stanley passed away on 23 July 1598 in Bickerstaffe, Lancashire, at the age of 83.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son '''Julian''' was born on 28 November 1600 in Ormskirk, Lancashire, and was baptized on 28 November 1600 at the Church of St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son '''Bartholomew''' was born in 1600 in Aughton Hall, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their daughter '''Alice''' was born in 1601 at Aughton (near Ormskirk), Lancashire, England,
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son '''William''' was born on 4 July 1608 in Ormskirk, Lancashire, and was baptized at the Church of St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son '''Alexander''' was born on 20 April 1610. (Alexander Hesketh 1610–1680)
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother Margaret Mary passed away on 3 November 1613 in Aughton, Lancashire, at the age of 77. (Margaret Mary Stanley 1536–1613).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband Gabriel Bartholomew Hawarth Hesketh  passed away on 22 November 1615 in Aughton, Lancashire, at the age of 37. They had been married 22 years. (Gabriel Bartholomew Hawarth Hesketh Sir
2 CONT 1578–1615)
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son William passed away in 1616 at the age of 8. (William Hesketh1608–1616).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son Julian passed away in 1620 at the age of 20. (Julian Hesketh 1600–1620).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jaine Scarisbrick Stanley died on 17 November 1622 in Ormskirk, Lancashire, when she was 50 years old. She was buried in the Stanley Chancel at the Church of St Peter and St Paul on the 17 Nov 1622 • Ormskirk, Lancashire,England. 
2 CONC (Burial: 17 Nov 1622 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancs. Jane Hesketh, Widow - Notes: gen. [generosa - latin for of noble birth] - bur in Mr Stanley his chancell Register: Burials 1557 - 1625/6, Page 423, Entry 5 Source: LDS Fi
2 CONC lm 1849663). Ormskirk, West Lancashire District, Lancashire, England
