2 CONC ret Howard1 F, #61429, b. 30 January 1543, d. 17 March 1591 Father Sir Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey1 b. bt 1516 - 1518, d. 19 Jan 1547 Mother Frances Vere1 b. c 1517, d. 30 Jun 1577 Margaret Howard was born on 30 January 1543 at L
2 CONC ambeth, England.1 She married Sir Henry Scrope, 9th Lord Scrope, son of John le Scrope, Baron Scrope of Bolton and Catherine Clifford, before 10 September 1565.1 Margaret Howard died on 17 March 1591 at Carlisle, Cumberlandshire, 
2 CONC England, at age 48.1 She was buried on 8 June 1591 at Wensley, Yorkshire, England.1 Family Sir Henry Scrope, 9th Lord Scrope b. c 1534, d. 13 Jun 1592 Citations 1.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great 
2 CONC Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. XI, p. 548-549. From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2044.htm#i61429 ________________ Margaret HOWARD (B. Scrope of Bolton) Born: Jan 1542/
2 CONC 3, Ashwell, Shropshire, England Baptised: 30 Jan 1542/3, Lambeth, London, England Died: 17 Mar 1591/2, Carlisle, Cumberland, England Buried: 8 Jun 1592, Wensley, Lancashire, England Notes: After her father’s execution for treason,
2 CONC  she and her sisters, Jane and Catherine, were brought up by their aunt, Mary Howard, Duchess of Richmond. The girls were educated by John Foxe, who taught them Greek and Latin and had them compose poetry. It may be that Margaret 
2 CONC Howard, unlike her older sisters, was permanently converted from the Catholicism of her father and grandfather to the Protestantism of her aunt. After Queen Mary succeeded to the throne in 1553, Margaret was briefly in the househo
2 CONC ld of her grandfather, Thomas Howard, 3rd Duke of Norfolk. Margaret married Henry Scrope, 9th baron Scrope of Bolton after his first wife died in Nov 1558. They had two sons, Thomas, 10th baron Scrope, and Henry. When Mary Stuart 
2 CONC first fled from Scotland into England her earliest prison was Carlisle Castle, where she was in the keeping of Lady Scrope. She was there by 18 May 1568 and was moved to Bolton Castle in Yorkshire by mid-Jul. On 3 Feb 1569, the Qu
2 CONC een of Scots arrived at Tutbury Castle, where she was turned over to the keeping of the Earl of Shrewsbury. Father: Henry HOWARD (E. Surrey) Mother: Frances De VERE (C. Surrey) Married: Henry SCROPE (9° B. Scrope of Bolton) BEF 10
2 CONC  Sep 1565, Bolton, Yorkshire, England Children: 1. Thomas SCROPE (10° B. Scrope of Bolton) 2. Henry SCROPE From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/HOWARD1.htm#Margaret HOWARD (B. Scrope of Bolton) _____________________ Henry Howard, Ea
2 CONC rl of Surrey, KG (1516/1517 – 19 January 1547), was an English aristocrat, and one of the founders of English Renaissance poetry. He was a first cousin of Catherine Howard, the fifth wife of King Henry VIII. He was the eldest son 
2 CONC of Thomas Howard, 3rd Duke of Norfolk, and his second wife, the former Lady Elizabeth Stafford (daughter of Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham), so he was descended from kings on both sides of his family tree: King Edward I o
2 CONC n his father's side and King Edward III on his mother's side. He was reared at Windsor with Henry VIII's illegitimate son Henry FitzRoy, 1st Duke of Richmond and Somerset, and they became close friends and, later, brothers-in-law 
2 CONC upon the marriage of Surrey's sister to Fitzroy.[1] Like his father and grandfather, he was a brave and able soldier, serving in Henry VIII's French wars as "Lieutenant General of the King on Sea and Land." He was repeatedly impri
2 CONC soned for rash behaviour, on one occasion for striking a courtier, on another for wandering through the streets of London breaking the windows of sleeping people.[2] He became Earl of Surrey in 1524 when his grandfather died and h
2 CONC is father became Duke of Norfolk. In 1532 he accompanied his first cousin Anne Boleyn, the King, and the Duke of Richmond to France, staying there for more than a year as a member of the entourage of Francis I of France. In 1536 h
2 CONC is first son, Thomas (later 4th Duke of Norfolk), was born, Anne Boleyn was executed on charges of adultery and treason, and the Duke of Richmond died at the age of 17 and was buried at one of the Howard homes, Thetford Abbey. In 
2 CONC 1536 Surrey also served with his father against the Pilgrimage of Grace rebellion protesting the Dissolution of the Monasteries. He and his friend Sir Thomas Wyatt were the first English poets to write in the sonnet form that Shak
2 CONC espeare later used, and Surrey was the first English poet to publish blank verse (unrhymed Iambic pentameter) in his translation of the second and fourth books of Virgil's Aeneid. Together, Wyatt and Surrey, due to their excellent
2 CONC  translations of Petrarch's sonnets, are known as "Fathers of the English Sonnet". While Wyatt introduced the sonnet into English, it was Surrey who gave them the rhyming meter and the division into quatrains that now characterize
2 CONC s the sonnets variously named English, Elizabethan or Shakespearean sonnets.[3][4] Henry VIII, consumed by paranoia and increasing illness, became convinced that Surrey had planned to usurp the crown from his son Edward. The King 
2 CONC had Surrey imprisoned—with his father—sentenced to death on 13 January 1547, and beheaded for treason on 19 January 1547 (his father survived impending execution only by it being set for the day after the king happened to die, tho
2 CONC ugh he remained imprisoned). Surrey's son Thomas became heir to the Dukedom of Norfolk instead, inheriting it on the 3rd Duke's death in 1554. He is buried in a spectacular painted alabaster tomb in the church of St Michael the Ar
2 CONC changel, Framlingham. He married Frances de Vere, the daughter of John de Vere, 15th Earl of Oxford, and Elizabeth Trussell. They had two sons and three daughters: Thomas Howard, 4th Duke of Norfolk (10 March 1536 – 2 June 1572), 
2 CONC married (1) Mary FitzAlan (2) Margaret Audley (3) Elizabeth Leyburne. Henry Howard, 1st Earl of Northampton, who died unmarried. Jane Howard, who married Charles Neville, 6th Earl of Westmorland. Margaret Howard, who married Henry
2 CONC  Scrope, 9th Baron Scrope of Bolton. Katherine Howard, who married Henry Berkeley, 7th Baron Berkeley. From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Howard,_Earl_of_Surrey ________________ Henry Scrope, 9th Baron Scrope of Bolton, KG (
2 CONC c. 1534 – 13 June 1592) was the son and heir of John Scrope, 8th Baron Scrope of Bolton and Catherine Clifford, daughter of Henry Clifford, Earl of Cumberland. Henry Scrope, a loyal supporter of Elizabeth I, was Marshal of the Arm
2 CONC y during the siege of Leith in 1559 which saw the end of the "Auld Alliance" between Scotland and France. He was appointed Warden of the West Marches and Captain of Carlisle from c.1560 until 1591. In April 1568 Mary, Queen of Sco
2 CONC ts fled across the Solway Firth to England after losing the Battle of Langside. She landed at Workington, travelling from there to Cockermouth and from thence to Carlisle. Scrope was at court at the time, but hurried north with Si
2 CONC r Francis Knollys to take charge of her. After a brief imprisonment in Carlisle Castle Mary was moved with a large retinue of servants to Lord Scrope's home in Yorkshire, Bolton Castle. Six months later she was moved on to Tutbury
2 CONC  Castle. In 1569, in the abortive Rising of the North, the Catholic Northern Earls rebelled with the objective of releasing Mary, Queen of Scots from her imprisonment at Tutbury and reinstating the old religion with Mary as queen.
2 CONC  As Captain of Carlisle Scrope was involved in preventing the dissident Earls from marching into Scotland.[1] He was made a Knight of the Garter in 1584. He died in Carlisle on June 13, 1592 and was succeeded by his eldest son Tho
2 CONC mas Scrope, 10th Baron Scrope of Bolton. After his death in 1592 queen Elizabeth wrote a letter praising his loyal service.[2] He married as his second wife Margaret Howard, daughter of Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey and Frances De 
2 CONC Vere. They had two sons, the eldest of which was his heir, Thomas. His first wife was Mary, daughter of Edward North, 1st Baron North, who died in 1558. By her he had a daughter Mary, who married William Bowes of Streatlam. From: 
2 CONC http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_le_Scrope,_9th_Baron_Scrope_of_Bolton _______________________________ Thomas le Scrope, 10th Baron Scrope of Bolton (1567 – 2 September 1609) was the son of Henry le Scrope, 9th Baron Scrope of B
2 CONC olton and Margaret Howard, daughter of Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey. He was knight of the shire (MP) for Cumberland from 1584 to 1586 and from 1588 to 1593.[1] He held the office of Warden of the English West March from 1593 until
2 CONC  the Union of the Crowns of England and Scotland in 1603. He was made a Knight of the Garter in 1599. While Scrope was Warden, the outlaw Kinmont Willie Armstrong was arrested (in violation of a truce day) and imprisoned at Carlis
2 CONC le Castle. Scrope had only recently been appointed to the post of Warden, and he was unsure of what to do with an outlaw who had been illegally arrested. On 13 April 1596, as a result of a raid orchestrated by Walter Scott, 1st Lo
2 CONC rd Scott of Buccleuch, Kinmont Willie was freed. In a failed attempt to recapture Kinmont Willie, Scrope "burnt the towns of Annan and Dumfries to the ground, capturing two hundred prisoners whom he marched home 'naked, chained to
2 CONC gether on leashes'. This caused a major diplomatic incident." [2] He died at the village of Langar in Nottinghamshire, England in 1609. In 1584, he had married Philadelphia Carey, daughter of Henry Carey, 1st Baron Hunsdon and Ann
2 CONC  Morgan. They had one child Emanuel Scrope, 1st Earl of Sunderland. In the village church at Langar is to be found a magnificent memorial to him. His tomb is adorned with an effigy of himself, his wife and their son Emmanuel. From
2 CONC : http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Scrope,_10th_Baron_Scrope_of_Bolton _____________________ read more
