1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Frances St. Leger1
2 CONT *F, #587347
2 CONT *Last Edited=12 Nov 2013
2 CONT *'''     Frances St. Leger is the daughter of John St. Leger and Lady Catherine Neville.2,3 She married John Stucley, son of Sir Lewis Stucley and Dorothy Hill.1
2 CONT *'''      Her married name became Stucley.
2 CONT *'''Child of Frances St. Leger and John Stucley
2 CONT ** 1.Sir Lewis Stucley+3 d. 1620
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S37] BP2003 volume 3, page 3800. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT * 2.[S4567] Bill Norton, "re: Pitman Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger LUNDY (101053), 6 April 2010 and 19 April 2011. Hereinafter cited as "re: Pitman Family."
2 CONT * 3.[S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p58735.htm#i587347 ___________________
2 CONT *'''Frances St. LEGER
2 CONT *'''Died: BEF 1597
2 CONT *Father: John St. LEGER of Annery (Sir Knight)
2 CONT *Mother: Catherine NEVILLE
2 CONT *'''Married 1: Son CULPEPPER
2 CONT *'''Married 2: John STUCLEY of Affeton (b. 1551 - d. 15 Jan 1610/1) (son of Lewis Stucley and Anne Hill) (m.2 Mary Redman) ABT 1573
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Catherine STUCLEY (m. Thomas Dowrish)
2 CONT **2. Lewis STUCLEY of Affeton (Sir Knight) (m. Frances Moncke)
2 CONT **3. Gertrude STUCLEY (m.1 Adam Williams - m.2 Humphrey Bury)
2 CONT **4. Anne STUCLEY (m.1 John Langford - m.2 William Coede - m.3 John Rowe)
2 CONT **5. Thomas STUCLEY
2 CONT **6. Mary STUCLEY (m. Simon Weekes)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/STLEGER.htm#Frances St. LEGER1 _______________________
2 CONT *Sir Lewis Stukley[2] (1552-1620) was an English gentleman and vice-admiral of Devonshire. He was guardian of Thomas Rolfe, and a main opponent of Sir Walter Raleigh in his last days. Stukley's reputation is equivocal; popular opi
2 CONC nion at the time idealised Ralegh, and to the public he was Sir "Judas" Stukley.
2 CONT *He was the '''eldest son of John Stucley of Affeton in Devon, by his wife Frances St Leger''', daughter of Sir John St Leger,[3] (d.1596) of Annery, Monkleigh, Devon, through whom he was related to leading families of the west of
2 CONC  England. His grandfather Lewis Stucley (c.1530–1581) of Affeton was the eldest brother of Thomas Stucley[4] (1520-1578) The Lusty Stucley, a mercenary leader who was killed fighting against the Moors at the Battle of Alcazar.[5]
2 CONT *.... etc.
2 CONT *Stukley married Frances, eldest daughter of Anthony Monck of Potheridge in Devon, and sister of Sir Thomas, the father of George Monck. By her he had issue.[4] From the point of view of Stukley's reputation, it mattered whether R
2 CONC aleigh was part of his extended family: this was widely accepted, but it has been pointed out that it may depend on Sir Richard Grenville's use of "cousin" to Raleigh, when they were not related.[3]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Stucley ________________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 55
2 CONT **Stucley, Lewis	by John Knox Laughton
2 CONT *STUCLEY or STUKELY, Sir LEWIS (d. 1620), vice-admiral of Devonshire, was '''eldest son of John Stucley of Affeton in Devonshire, and Frances St. Leger''', through whom he was related to all the leading families of the west of Eng
2 CONC land. His grandfather Lewis (1530?–1581) was younger brother of Thomas Stucley [q. v.] The younger Lewis was knighted by James I when on his way to London in 1603 (Metcalfe, Book of Knights), and in 1617 was appointed guardian of 
2 CONC Thomas Rolfe, the infant son of Pocahontas [see Rolfe, John]. .... etc.
2 CONT * .... The king possibly took this into consideration; possibly he thought that he owed Stucley something for his service against Ralegh. He pardoned him, and Stucley, an outcast from society in London, went down to Devonshire. Th
2 CONC e popular hatred pursued him even to Affeton, and he fled to hide his shame in the lonely island of Lundy, where he died in the course of 1620, raving mad it was said.
2 CONT *Stucley married Frances, eldest daughter of Anthony Monck of Potheridge in Devonshire, and sister of Sir Thomas, the father of George Monck, duke of Albemarle [q. v.] By her he had issue, and the family is still Stucley of Affeto
2 CONC n.
2 CONT *[Cal. State Papers, Dom.; The Humble Petition and Information of Sir Lewis Stucley, knt., Vice-admiral of Devonshire, in Harl. Misc. iii. 63–8; Vivian's Visitations of Devon, 1895, pp. 721–3; Gardiner's History of England; Speddi
2 CONC ng's Life of Bacon; Burke's Baronetage.]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Stucley,_Lewis_(DNB00)
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of national biography (1885) Vol. LV. STOW-TAYLOR
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofnati55stepuoft
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati55stepuoft#page/122/mode/1up __________________
2 CONT *Devon Perspectives
2 CONT *http://www.devonperspectives.co.uk/lewis_stukeley.html
2 CONT *an infamous betrayal
2 CONT *The life of Sir Lewis Stukeley (sometimes spelt Stukely, or Stucley) will be stigmatized forever by one dishonourable deed: the betrayal of his distant cousin and Devon worthy Sir Walter Raleigh on his return from Guiana in 1618,
2 CONC  a shameless act that lead directly to Raleigh's incarceration in the Tower and subsequent execution. Whence Sir Lewis has been branded 'the Judas of Devonshire'. 
2 CONT *The '''oldest son of John Stukeley of Affeton by Frances St Leger''', Sir Lewis was one of many who were granted a knighthood by James I on his way to London in 1603. As with others so honoured, this award was purely on account o
2 CONC f his breeding, rather than for his achievements. He first came to public notice in 1617 when, as the recently installed Vice-Admiral of Devonshire, he was appointed temporary guardian of the infant child Thomas Rolfe, son of John
2 CONC  Rolfe and his Indian Princess wife Pocahontas, after her sudden death just as preparations were being made for the three of them to return from England to Virginia.    . . . . . 
2 CONT *   Sixteen miles to the westward, like a blue cloud on the horizon, rises the ultima Thule of Devon, the little isle of Lundy. There one outlying peak of granite, carrying up a shelf of slate upon its southern flank, has defied t
2 CONC he waves, and formed an island some three miles long, desolate, flat-headed, fretted by every frost and storm, walled all round with four hundred feet of granite cliff, sacred only, then at least, to puffins and pirates. Over the 
2 CONC single landing-place frowns from the cliff the keep of an old ruin, 'Marisco Castle,' as they call it still, where some bold rover, Sir John de Marisco, in the times of the old Edwards, worked his works of darkness: a grey, weird,
2 CONC  uncanny pile of moorstone, through which all the winds of heaven howl day and night. 
2 CONT *   In a chamber of that ruin died Sir Lewis Stukeley, Lord of Affeton, cursing God and man.  ______________________
2 CONT *Sir John St. Leger (died 1596), of Annery in the parish of Monkleigh, Devon, .... etc.
2 CONT *He married Catherine Neville, daughter of George Neville, 5th Baron Bergavenny and according Virgoe (1982) left two sons[4] (only one according to Chope (1917)[5]) and four daughters:
2 CONT **John St Ledger, eldest son, a soldier in Ireland, died unmarried and in poverty
2 CONT **Dudley St Ledger, possibly illegitimate[6]
2 CONT **Mary St Ledger, the eldest, who married Sir Richard Grenville (1542-1591) of Stowe, Kilkhampton, Cornwall and Bideford, Devon, the famous captain of Revenge.
2 CONT **'''Frances St Ledger, who married John Stucley (1551-1611), of Affeton, Devon[9] and was the mother of Sir Lewis Stucley[10]
2 CONT **Margaret St Ledger, who married Richard Bellew of Ash, Braunton and Alverdiscott, Devon. A heraldic mural monument to Richard Bellew and his wife Margaret St Ledger survives in Braunton parish Church.
2 CONT **Eulalia St Ledger, who married firstly Edmund Tremayne of Collacombe, Devon, and secondly Tristram Arscott (1544-1621) of Tetcott, Devon, the latter who bought Annery from his father-in-law John St Leger.[11][12]
2 CONT *.... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_St_Leger _______________________
2 CONT *'''Collectanea topographica et genealogica (1834)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/collectaneatopog04londuoft
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/collectaneatopog04londuoft#page/321/mode/1up
2 CONT * . . .  Thomas Earl of Ormonde died 8th Aug. 1515, leaving issue Anne and Margaret his heirs. 
2 CONT *   To which of these ladies the island was apportioned I am unable to tell. Anne, the eldest coheir, married Sir James St. Leger of Annery, co. Devon, whose only son the issue of this marriage, Sir George St. Leger, had by Ann, d
2 CONC aughter of Edmund Knevyt, Esq. an only son Sir John, who, by Katharine daughter of George Lord Abergavenny, had issue : — John and Dudley, who both died s. p. and three daughters, of whom Mary, the eldest, had to her husband the g
2 CONC allant Sir Richard Grenville, of Bideford, co. Devon, Admiral of England, and by him was mother of the equally gallant Sir Bevill Grenville, who died in the cause of Charles I. at Lansdowne, and whose son and heir appears to have 
2 CONC held Lundy at his death. If the island descended through this Mary to her grandson, it passed for a time from the family, as will presently be seen. The two other daughters were, '''Frances, married to John Stucley, Esq. of Affeto
2 CONC n, Devon''', and Eulalia, the wife, first of Edmund Tremain, Esq. of Cullacomb, Devon ; secondly, of Tristram Arscott, Esq. of Annery.  _____________________
