1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Anthony St. Leger
2 CONT Born: ABT 1557, Kent, England
2 CONT Died: 19 Dec 1602, Leeds Castle, Kent, England
2 CONT Buried: Ulcombe Church, Ulcombe, Kent, England
2 CONT Father: Sir Warham St. Leger 
2 CONT Mother: Ursula Neville
2 CONT Married: Mary Scott  (b. 1557 - d. 1636) (dau. of Thomas Scott and Elizabeth Baker) ABT Mar 1578
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. Sir Warham St. Leger  
2 CONT 2. Thomas St. Leger (b. 1581 - d. 1631)
2 CONT 3. Anthony St. Leger (b. 1591 - d. 1636)
2 CONT 4. Francis St. Leger (d. 1598)
2 CONT 5. Ursula St. Leger (d. 1603)
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/125442357/anthony-saint_leger Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 18 2020, 17:49:39 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Per https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/St_Leger-22
2 CONT Anthony St Leger, eldest son of Sir Warham St Leger and his wife Ursula Neville,[3] daughter of George Neville, Baron Bergavenny,[4] and his 3rd wife Mary Stafford,[1] was born about 1551 in Ulcombe, Kent, England. (No source has 
2 CONC yet been found for Anthony's date of birth, the year 1551 is an estimate based on his parent's marriage in 1549.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Anthony married Mary Scott, daughter of Sir Thomas Scott of Scott's Hall, Sheriff of Kent (1576-7), and Elizabeth Baker, on 13 May 1578 in St Mary's Church at Smeeth, Kent.[5][1] According to Douglas Richardson they had four sons 
2 CONC and one daughter,[1] but only two of their children are mentioned by name:
2 CONT
2 CONT Warham St Leger, Knt., m. Mary Hayward[1][6][7], d. 1630[8]
2 CONT Anthony St Leger, b. 1591[9]; d. 1626[10]
2 CONT Anthony St Leger was made a knight 28 May 1593 by William FitzWilliam, lord deputy of Ireland.[11][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Anthony St. Leger died in 1602 and was buried on 17 June of that year in All Saints Church at Ulcombe, Kent.[13] His widow, Mary, married (2nd) Sir Alexander Culpeper of Wigsell, Sussex on 7 December 1603.[14][15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT Richardson dates Anthony's death as occurring in 1603, but this theory is superseded by the discovery of his burial record at Ulcombe.
2 CONT
