1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT =Fulke Greville 1st Baron Brooke, 5th Baron Willoughby de Broke KB PC=
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulke_Greville,_1st_Baron_Brooke From Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fulke Greville, 1st Baron Brooke, de jure 13th Baron Latimer and 5th Baron Willoughby de Broke KB PC''' (/fʊlk ˈɡrɛvɪl/; 3 October 1554 – 30 September 1628), known before 1621 as Sir Fulke Greville, was an Elizabethan poet, dra
2 CONC matist, and statesman who sat in the House of Commons at various times between 1581 and 1621, when he was raised to the peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greville was a capable administrator who served the English Crown under Elizabeth I and James I as, successively, treasurer of the navy, chancellor of the exchequer, and commissioner of the Treasury, and who for his services was i
2 CONC n 1621 made Baron Brooke, peer of the realm. Greville was granted Warwick Castle in 1604, making numerous improvements. Greville is best known today as the biographer of Sir Philip Sidney, and for his sober poetry, which presents 
2 CONC dark, thoughtful and distinctly Calvinist views on art, literature, beauty and other philosophical matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Fulke Greville, born 3 October 1554, at Beauchamp Court, near Alcester, Warwickshire, was the only son of Sir Fulke Greville (1536–1606) and Anne Neville (d.1583), the daughter of Ralph Neville, 4th Earl of Westmorland.[1] He was 
2 CONC the grandson of Sir Fulke Greville (d. 10 November 1559) and Elizabeth Willoughby (buried 15 November 1562), eldest daughter of Robert Willoughby, 2nd Baron Willoughby de Broke,[2] the only other child of the marriage was a daught
2 CONC er, Margaret Greville (1561–1631/2), who married Sir Richard Verney.[3] He 
2 CONT enrolled at Jesus College, Cambridge in 1568.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Henry Sidney, Philip's father, and president of the Council of Wales and the Marches, gave Greville in 1576 a post connected with the court of the Welsh Marches, but Greville resigned it in 1577 to go to attend court of Queen 
2 CONC Elizabeth along with Philip Sidney. There, Greville became a great favourite with the Queen, who valued his sober character and administrative skills. In 1581, he was elected in a by-election as Member of Parliament for Southampto
2 CONC n.[5] Queen Elizabeth made him secretary to the principality of Wales in 1583. However he was put out of favour more than once for leaving the country against her wishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greville, Philip Sidney and Sir Edward Dyer were members of the "Areopagus", the literary clique which, under the leadership of Gabriel Harvey, supported the introduction of classical metres into English verse. Sidney and Greville
2 CONC  arranged to sail with Sir Francis Drake in 1585 in his expedition against the Spanish West Indies, but Elizabeth forbade Drake to take them with him, and also refused Greville's request to be allowed to join Robert Dudley's army 
2 CONC in the Netherlands. Philip Sidney, who took part in the campaign, was killed on 17 October 1586. Greville memorialized his beloved friend in his Life of the Renowned Sir Philip Sidney.
2 CONT Greville participated in the Battle of Coutras in 1587.[6] About 1591 Greville served further for a short time in Normandy under Henry of Navarre in the French Wars of Religion. This was his last experience of war. Greville repres
2 CONC ented Warwickshire in parliament in 1592-1593, 1597, 1601 and 1621. In 1598 he was made Treasurer of the Navy, and he retained the office through the early years of the reign of James I. Greville was granted Warwick Castle—situate
2 CONC d on a bend of the River Avon in Warwickshire—by King James I in 1604.[7] Dilapidated when he took possession of the castle, he spent £20,000 to restore it to former glory.[5][8] In 1614 he became chancellor and under-treasurer of
2 CONC  the exchequer, and throughout the reign he was a valued supporter of James I, although in 1615 he advocated the summoning of a parliament. In 1618 he became commissioner of the treasury, and in 1621 he was raised to the peerage w
2 CONC ith the title of Baron Brooke, a title which had belonged to the family of his paternal grandmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT This section of a biographical article needs additional citations for verification. Please help by adding reliable sources. Contentious material about living persons that is unsourced or poorly sourced must be removed immediately,
2 CONC  especially if potentially libelous or harmful. (May 2014) In 1628 Greville was stabbed inside Warwick Castle by Ralph Heywood, a servant who believed that he had been cheated in his master's will. Heywood then turned the knife on
2 CONC  himself. Greville's physicians treated his wounds by filling them with pig fat rather than disinfecting them, the pig fat turned rancid and infected the wounds, and he died in agony four weeks after the attack. He was buried in t
2 CONC he Collegiate Church of St Mary, Warwick, and on his tomb was inscribed the epitaph he had composed:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Folk Grevill
2 CONT * Servant to Queene Elizabeth
2 CONT * Conceller to King James
2 CONT * and Frend to Sir Philip Sidney.
2 CONT * Trophaeum Peccati.[9]
2 CONT * Works[edit]
2 CONT * Greville is best known by his biography of Sidney, the full title of which expresses the scope of the work.[n 1] He includes some autobiographical matter in what amounts to a treatise on government.
2 CONT * Greville's poetry consists of closet tragedies, sonnets, and poems on political and moral subjects. His style is grave and sententious.
2 CONT * Greville's works include:
2 CONT * Biography
2 CONT * The Life of the Renowned Sir Philip Sidney (1625)
2 CONT * Closet drama
2 CONT * Alaham
2 CONT * Mustapha
2 CONT * Verse poems
2 CONT * Caelica in CX Sonnets
2 CONT * Of Monarchy
2 CONT * A Treatise of Religion
2 CONT * A Treatie of Humane Learning
2 CONT * An Inquisition upon Fame and Honour
2 CONT * A Treatie of Warres
2 CONT * Miscellaneous prose
2 CONT * a letter to an "Honourable Lady,"
2 CONT * a letter to Grevill Varney in France,
2 CONT * a short speech delivered on behalf of Francis Bacon
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Editions'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Greville's works were collected and reprinted by Alexander Balloch Grosart, in 1870, in four volumes. Poetry and Drama of Fulke Greville, edited by Geoffery Bullough, was published in 1938. The Prose Works of Fulke Greville, edite
2 CONC d by John Gouws, were published in 1986. "The Selected Poems of Fulke Greville," edited by Thom Gunn, with an afterword by Bradin Cormack, was published in 2009 (University of Chicago Press, ISBN 978-0-226-30846-3.) The Tragedy of
2 CONC  Mustapha (London: Printed by J. Windet for N. Butter, 1609). Certaine Learned and Elegant Workes (London: Printed by E. Purslowe for H. Seyle, 1633)--comprises A Treatise of Humane Learning, An Inquisition upon Fame and Honour, A
2 CONC  Treatise of Wars, Alaham, Mustapha, Caelica, A Letter to an Honorable Lady, and A Letter of Travel. The Remains of Sir Fvlk Grevill Lord Brooke: Being Poems of Monarchy and Religion: Never Before Printed (London: Printed by T. N.
2 CONC  for H. Herringman, 1670)--comprises A Treatise of Monarchy and A Treatise of Religion. Poems and Dramas of Fulke Greville, First Lord Brooke, 2 volumes, edited by Geoffrey Bullough (Edinburgh: Oliver & Boyd, 1939; New York: Oxfor
2 CONC d University Press, 1945)--comprises Caelica, A Treatise of Humane Learning, An Inquisition upon Fame and Honor, A Treatise of Wars, Mustapha, and Alaham. The Remains: Being Poems of Monarchy and Religion, edited by G. A. Wilkes (
2 CONC London: Oxford University Press, 1965)--comprises A Treatise of Monarchy and A Treatise of Religion. The Prose Works Fulke Greville, Lord Brooke, edited by John Guows (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1997)—published as part of th
2 CONC e Oxford English Texts series. A scholarly edition of his prose works, with an authoritative text, together with an introduction, commentary notes, and scholarly apparatus. The principal repository for Fulke Greville's papers is t
2 CONC he British Library (Add. Mss. 54566-71, the Warwick Manuscripts; letters in the as-yet uncatalogued Earl Cowper mss.). Individual manuscripts of the Dedication to Sir Philip Sidney are to be found in Headington, Oxford (the privat
2 CONC e collection of Dr. B. E. Juel-Jensen); Trinity College, Cambridge (Mss. R.7.32 and 33); and Shrewsbury Public Library (Ms. 295).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Critical reception'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Brooke Charles Lamb says . . "He is nine parts Machiavel and Tacitus, for one of Sophocles and Seneca.... Whether we look into his plays or his most passionate love-poems, we shall find all frozen and made rigid with intellect.
2 CONC " He goes on to speak of the obscurity of expression that runs through all Brooke's poetry. Andrea McCrea sees the influence of Justus Lipsius in the Letter to an Honourable Lady, but elsewhere detects a scepticism more akin to Mi
2 CONC chel de Montaigne.[10] A rhyming elegy on Brooke, published in Henry Huth's Inedited Poetical Miscellanies, brings charges of miserliness against him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Pinsky has asserted that this work is comparable in force of imagination to John Donne.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Brooke left no natural heirs, and his senior (Brooke) barony passed to his cousin and adopted son, Robert Greville (1608–1643), who took the side of Parliament part in the English Civil War, and defeated the Royalists in a sk
2 CONC irmish at Kineton in August 1642. Robert was killed during the siege of Lichfield on 2 March 1643, having survived the elder Greville by only fifteen years. His other barony (Willoughby de Broke) was inherited by his sister Margar
2 CONC et who married Sir Richard Verney.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump up ^ the complete title: The Life of the Renowned Sr. Philip Sidney. With the true Interest of England as it then stood in relation to all Forrain Princes: And particularly for suppressing the power of Spain Stated by Him: Hi
2 CONC s principall Actions, Counsels, Designes, and Death. Together with a short account of the Maximes and Policies used by Queen Elizabeth in her Government.
2 CONT
