2 CONT This unusual double portrait of the teenage sisters Dorothy (left) and Penelope Devereux (right) hangs at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longleat_House Longleat House], home of Lord Bath. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothy_Per
2 CONC cy,_Countess_of_Northumberland Dorothy] was to become Countess of Northumberland upon her marriage, and Penelope was to become Lady Penelope Rich on marrying Lord Robert Rich. For more information on this portrait and Penelope Ric
2 CONC h, refer to Chapter Fifteen and the Author's Postscript to Shakespeare's ''Dark Lady''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000205576187847&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Penelope Rich, Lady Rich''', later styled '''Penelope Blount''' (née '''Devereux'''; January 1563[1] – 7 July 1607) was an English court office holder. She served as lady-in-waiting to the English queen Anne of Denmark. She was
2 CONC  the sister of Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex, and is traditionally thought to be the inspiration for "Stella" of Sir Philip Sidney's Astrophel and Stella sonnet sequence (published posthumously in 1591).[2][3] She was married
2 CONC  to Robert Rich, 3rd Baron Rich (later 1st Earl of Warwick) and had a public liaison with Charles Blount, Baron Mountjoy, whom she married in an unlicensed ceremony following her divorce from Rich. She died in 1607.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had a strict Puritan upbringing and quite a simple life until she was presented at Court in early 1581. In January, she arrived at court accompanied by her guardian's wife, Catherine, Countess of Huntingdon, who was Leicester'
2 CONC s sister and Sidney's aunt.[4] In March 1581 Huntingdon as her guardian secured the queen's assent through Lord Burghley, Master of the Court of Wards, for her marriage with Robert Rich, 3rd Baron Rich (later 1st Earl of Warwick).
2 CONC  Penelope is said to have protested in vain against the alliance with Rich.[3][8] The marriage was unhappy from the start.
2 CONT
2 CONT Penelope Rich was considered one of the beauties of Elizabeth's court. She was golden-haired with dark eyes, a gifted singer and dancer, fluent in French, Italian, and Spanish.[3][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether Sidney fell passionately in love with Penelope in the years between her arrival at court in 1581 and his own marriage in 1583, or whether the "Stella" sonnets were courtly amusements reflecting fashionable poetic conceits 
2 CONC may never be known. In her essay "Sidney, Stella, and Lady Rich", Katherine Duncan-Jones writes:
2 CONT
2 CONT >No one since 1935 has seriously doubted that Sidney intended the first readers of Astropil and Stella, whoever they may have been, to link "Stella" with Lady Rich. The exact nature of Sidney's relationship with the famous beauty 
2 CONC is another and much more ticklish matter ..."[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Penelope's arranged marriage to Rich had been unhappy, and by 1595 she began a secret affair with Charles Blount, Baron Mountjoy. Lord Rich took no action during the lifetime of Penelope's brother, the powerful Earl of Essex, who 
2 CONC became the ageing Queen's favourite in the years after the death of Leicester in 1588.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT However Penelope became tainted by association with her brother's plotting. Essex shocked many people, after the failure of his rebellion, by denouncing her as a traitor, and after his execution for treason in 1601, Lord Rich had 
2 CONC Penelope and her children by Mountjoy cast out. Mountjoy, like Penelope, had been implicated in the Essex rebellion, but the Queen, who wished to show as much clemency as possible to the rebels, took no action against either of th
2 CONC em. Lady Rich moved in with her lover, and the couple began a very public relationship. Mountjoy was created Earl of Devonshire on the accession of James I, and Lady Rich was in high favour at court.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children  
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penelope_Blount,_Countess_of_Devonshire
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Penelope's children by Robert Rich were:
2 CONT # Lettice Rich (d. 1619), named after her maternal grandmother Lettice Knollys and known as Lucy. Married firstly Sir George Carey and secondly Sir Arthur Lake[9]
2 CONT # Essex Rich, married Sir Thomas Cheek and had three sons and five daughters
2 CONT # Robert Rich (1587–1658), later 2nd Earl of Warwick
2 CONT # Henry Rich (1590–1649), later 1st Earl of Holland
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Penelope's illegitimate children acknowledged by Charles Blount were:[22][23]
2 CONT # Penelope Rich, who, despite her surname, was a daughter of Penelope by Blount
2 CONT # Mountjoy Blount (c.1597–1666),[24] later 1st Earl of Newport
2 CONT # Charles Blount
2 CONT # St. John Blount
2 CONT # Isabella Blount
2 CONT # another child died in infancy
2 CONT
2 CONT 22. Sylvia Freedman Poor Penelope: Lady Penelope Rich, An Elizabethan Woman (London, Kensal Press, 1983), 188.
2 CONT
2 CONT 23. Frederick M. Jones, Mountjoy, 1563–1606: The Last Elizabeth Deputy (Dublin, Clonmore & Reynolds, 1958), 226.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children listed in the biography for Blount, Charles, eighth Baron Mountjoy and earl of Devonshire.  < [https://www.oxforddnb.com/display/10.1093/ref:odnb/9780198614128.001.0001/odnb-9780198614128-e-2683 ODNB] >
2 CONT
2 CONT # Mountjoy Blount, received a substantial inheritance. His other children,
2 CONT # St John,
2 CONT # Charles,
2 CONT # Penelope, and
2 CONT # Isabella also received an annual sum out of his vast estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/discussions/101249?msg=1436252
2 CONT
2 CONT From Sally Varlow, The Lady Penelope: Passion and Intrigue at the Heart of the Elizabethan Court (Andre Deutch: London, England, 2014)
2 CONT
2 CONT page 238: "Once the peace was signed [Treaty of London, 1604] Devonshire was at last able to get away to Wanstead for a summer interlude. Here in the calm and quiet of Wanstead he could relax with Penelope and his children. Their 
2 CONC eldest child, Penelope, was now twelve years old; Isabella had turned nine in January; and though none of their three sons, Mountjoy, St. John and Charles, had yet reached eight, they were fine boys who would one day join him in r
2 CONC iding, hunting and hawking in the parks."
2 CONT
2 CONT page 259: "The deed of trust and Devonshire's will, signed the day before he died, meant his properties should pass straight to his beneficiaries . . . . In the will, Penelope and their five children were carefully identified . . 
2 CONC . . To make it watertight only their Christian names were used: Mountjoy St. John, Charles, Penelope and Isabella, and 'such issue as the said lady now goeth withall'."
2 CONT
2 CONT page 312. The endnotes cite Devonshire's will thusly: "PROB 11/108, re-examined 11/109/322; the inquistionem post mortem: PRO C 142/306/146 to establish his estate; and the Star Chamber court case is STAC 8/108/10."
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 February 1600, Elizabeth sent Charles Blount, then 8th Baron Mountjoy, to Ireland to replace Essex. A year later (during the time Essex was committed to the Tower) he petitioned Elizabeth to return to London, but she refused.
2 CONC  He returned from Ireland in June of 1603 after her death. (His success in quashing the Irish rebellion earned him the title 1st Earl of Devonshire from James I.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
