1 OCCU Countess of Northumberland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Dorothy Percy, Countess of Northumberland''' (formerly Perrot, née Devereux; c. 1564 – 3 August 1619) was the younger daughter of Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex by Lettice Knollys, and the wife of Henry Perc
2 CONC y, 9th Earl of Northumberland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait identified as Dorothy Devereux (on the left) and her elder sister Penelope Devereux, c.1581 This unusual double portrait of the teenage sisters Dorothy (left) and Penelope Devereux (right) hangs at Longleat House, home of
2 CONC  Lord Bath. Dorothy was to become Countess of Northumberland upon her marriage, and Penelope was to become Lady Penelope Rich on marrying Lord Robert Rich. For more information on this portrait and Penelope Rich, refer to Chapter 
2 CONC Fifteen and the Author's Postscript to Shakespeare's Dark Lady. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Perrot married Dorothy Devereux (d.1619), daughter of Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex, by whom he is said to have had a son who predeceased him without issue, and a daughter, Penelope.[1] Perrot's daughter, Penelope, mar
2 CONC ried firstly the astronomer Sir William Lower, by whom she had three sons and a daughter.[10][11][12] After Lower's death Penelope (née Perrot) married secondly Sir Robert Naunton, by whom she had a daughter Penelope (born 1620), 
2 CONC who married firstly Paul Bayning, 2nd Viscount Bayning, and secondly Philip Herbert, 5th Earl of Pembroke.[13][14] Sir Robert Naunton's Fragmenta Regalia is the source of the claim that Thomas Perrot's father, Sir John Perrot, was
2 CONC  an illegitimate son of Henry VIII.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Perrot's death his widow, Dorothy, married Henry Percy, 9th Earl of Northumberland.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy was born in about 1564, the daughter of Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex and Lettice Knollys, a lady-in-waiting of Queen Elizabeth I of England. Her paternal grandparents were Sir Richard Devereux and Dorothy Hastings, a
2 CONC fter whom she was named. Her maternal grandparents were Sir Francis Knollys and Lady Catherine Carey, the daughter of Mary Boleyn, herself the sister of Queen consort Anne Boleyn. Dorothy had an elder sister Penelope Devereux, who
2 CONC  was said to have been the inspiration for Sir Philip Sidney's sonnet sequence Astrophel and Stella. She had three younger brothers, Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex, Walter Devereux and Francis Devereux.
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1576, Dorothy's father died in Dublin, Ireland of dysentery. Two years later, her mother married secondly and in secret, Queen Elizabeth's favourite Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester, thus earning the wrath of the quee
2 CONC n, who promptly banished her from court. The marriage produced one son Robert, Baron Denbigh who was born in 1581. The boy died at the age of three. In 1589, eleven months after Leicester's death, Dorothy acquired another stepfath
2 CONC er, Sir Christopher Blount, who was thirteen years younger than her mother. On 25 February 1601, her brother Robert, 2nd Earl of Essex was beheaded at the Tower of London for treason.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1583 at Broxbourne Hertfordshire she married Sir Thomas Perrott, 1st Baronet of Haroldston. There were rumours at the time that Dorothy's father-in-law, John Perrot, was an illegitimate son of Henry VIII by his mistress Ma
2 CONC ry Berkeley. They had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Penelope Perrott, married 1. William Lower, had issue; 2. Sir Robert Naunton, by whom she had a daughter Penelope. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Perrott, married James Perrott of Wallingford, by whom she had two children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Perrot 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy married secondly in 1594, Henry Percy, Earl of Northumberland, known as "The Wizard Earl", but the marriage was not a success, and they later separated. In 1605, the earl was sent to the Tower of London on suspicion of inv
2 CONC olvement in the Gunpowder Plot, and he was not freed until after his wife's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy (c.1598- 20 August 1659), married Robert Sidney, 2nd Earl of Leicester, by whom she had six children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy (1599/1600- 5 November 1660), married as his second wife James Hay, 1st Earl of Carlisle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Algernon (29 September 1602- 13 October 1668), married firstly, Anne Cecil, by whom he had five daughters, including Lady Elizabeth Percy, Countess of Essex; he married secondly, Lady Elizabeth Howard, by whom he had his heir, Joc
2 CONC eline Percy, 11th Earl of Northumberland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Percy, Lord of Alnwick (1604- April 1659), died unmarried. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her eldest daughter Dorothy, Countess of Leicester, she was an ancestress of Diana, Princess of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Betchemann, Lita-Rose (2005). Court Lady and Country Wife: Two Noble Sisters in Seventeenth-Century England. New York: Collins. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brenan, Gerald (1902). A History of the House of Percy. London: Freemantle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brennan, Michael and Noel Kinnamon (2003). A Sidney Chronology. London: Palgrave Macmillan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT thePeerage.com 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dorothy Percy, Countess of Northumberland
