1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sir Nicholas Clifford, Kt., MP
2 CONT Family and Education s. of Henry Clifford by Anne Devereux, sis. of ‘Visct. Hereford’ (presumably Walter, 2nd Visct. cr. Earl of Essex 1572). m. Frances, da. of Sir William Drury, 1da. Kntd. 1591.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Like his first cousin Sir Conyers, Clifford was in the service of his cousin Robert, 2nd Earl of Essex, who knighted him for gallantry at Rouen. Henri IV conferred on him the order of St. Michael, but the Queen, in a ‘pa
2 CONC ssion’ and ‘rage’ at his wearing at court foreign insignia accepted without her leave, imprisoned him first in the ‘noisome’ Fleet, then in the Tower ‘where I look rather to die than live’, and forced him to return the order, thou
2 CONC gh to do so was to make ‘a scorn of that nation, which is so jealous of their honour’. It was about this incident that Elizabeth is supposed to have said ‘My dogs wear my collars’. He was granted an honorary degree at Cambridge in
2 CONC  1594, and in the following year served on Drake’s last voyage to the Indies, where he was killed at Puerto Rico when a shore battery fired a lucky shot into the cabin of the Defiance as Drake and other officers sat at table. He w
2 CONC as buried at sea.2
2 CONT
2 CONT The Devereux connexion explains Clifford’s nomination for Haverfordwest. In 1596 the town belatedly paid 7s.6d. to the clerk of the Crown ‘for entering Sir Nicholas Clifford’s name when he went into the Parliament House’.3
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Clifford-3607
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas was born about 1565, son and heir of Henry Clifford of Bobbing, Kent, and Anne Devereux.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married about 1593, Frances Drury, daughter of William Drury and Elizabeth Stafford.[2] They had
2 CONT
2 CONT Frances Clifford, heiress, born before 1594, married Sir Edward Ayscough of South Kelsey, Lincolnshire.[3][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Note
2 CONT
2 CONT The authors of "Alumni Cantabrigienses," mistakenly makes him brother of Sir Conyers Clifford, when they were first cousins.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References
