1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *''''''Frances Newton1
2 CONT *''''''F, #11818, d. 17 October 1592
2 CONT *Last Edited=28 Apr 2010
2 CONT *''''''Frances Newton married Sir William Brooke, 10th Lord Cobham (of Kent), son of Sir George Brooke, 9th Lord Cobham (of Kent) and Anne Bray, on 25 February 1559/60 at Westminster Palace, Westminster, London, England.1,3 She di
2 CONC ed on 17 October 1592 at Cobham, Kent, England.3 She was buried at Cobham, Kent, England.3
2 CONT *''''''     She was the daughter of Sir John Newton and Margaret Poyntz.1,3 From 25 February 1559/60, her married name became Brooke.
2 CONT *''''''Children of Frances Newton and Sir William Brooke, 10th Lord Cobham (of Kent)
2 CONT **1.Hon. Frances Brooke
2 CONT **2.Margaret Brooke+1
2 CONT **3.Hon. Elizabeth Brooke+4 b. 1 Jan 1562/63, d. 24 Jan 1596/97
2 CONT **4.Sir Henry Brooke, 11th Lord Cobham (of Kent)5 b. 22 Nov 1564, d. 24 Jan 1618/19
2 CONT **5.Reverend George Brooke+1 b. 17 Apr 1568, d. 5 Dec 1603
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S21] L. G. Pine, The New Extinct Peerage 1884-1971: Containing Extinct, Abeyant, Dormant and Suspended Peerages With Genealogies and Arms (London, U.K.: Heraldry Today, 1972), page 78. Hereinafter cited as The New Extinct Peer
2 CONC age.
2 CONT *2.[S3409] Caroline Maubois, "re: Penancoet Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 2 December 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Penancoet Family."
2 CONT *3.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
2 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume III, page 348. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *4.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume III, page 349.
2 CONT *5.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume II, page 288.
2 CONT *http://thepeerage.com/p1182.htm#i11818
2 CONT *________
2 CONT *''''''William Brooke, 10th Baron Cobham (1527–1597) was Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports, and a Member of Parliament for Hythe. Although he was viewed by some as a religious radical during the Somerset protectorate, he entertained
2 CONC  Elizabeth at Cobham Hall in 1559, signalling his acceptance of the moderate regime.
2 CONT *His father died in 1558 when William was just over thirty. Brooke married Dorothy Neville, daughter of George Neville, 5th Baron Bergavenny in 1545, but the marriage was unhappy, and they separated after 1553. Brooke seems to hav
2 CONC e attended The King's School, Canterbury and Queens' College, Cambridge before 1544.[1] He spent much of his younger life in Europe. In the early 1540s he visited Padua. At the end of the decade he served in northern France, where
2 CONC  his father was in charge of Calais and in 1549 accompanied Paget's embassy to Brussels.
2 CONT *Like his father, Brooke sympathized with the anti-Marian nobles; he sided with the rebels during Wyatt's rebellion, and the intervention of his brother-in-law, Henry Nevill was needed to keep him from prison. For the last years o
2 CONC f Mary's reign he served as MP for Rochester.
2 CONT *''''''In the late 1550s, Brooke's opportunities expanded in a number of areas. His father died, making him Baron Cobham; his first wife died, leaving him free to marry Frances Newton (at Whitehall in 1560). He became Warden of th
2 CONC e Cinque Ports, a position in which he wielded great power over a large number of seats in Parliament. Most important, the accession of Elizabeth, and his close friendship with William Cecil made him a powerful noble. Elizabeth de
2 CONC puted him to inform Philip II of Mary's death. This embassy was only the first in a long series of missions and intrigues. Along with Cecil, he numbered among his friends some nobles, such as Thomas Howard and the Earl of Arundel,
2 CONC  whose loyalty to Elizabeth was far from certain. He suffered some months' house imprisonment as a result of a very tangential role in the Ridolfi plot. In 1578, he joined Francis Walsingham's failed mission to the Low Countries; 
2 CONC on this mission he presumably served as Cecil's agent. In the late 1580s, he helped John Whitgift search for the author of the Martin Marprelate tracts.
2 CONT *''''''Brooke became a member of the Privy Council in 1586, the year after he joined the Order of the Garter. He was involved in a minor capacity in the events that ended with the death of Mary, Queen of Scots. During the Armada c
2 CONC risis, he was on a diplomatic mission to Alexander Farnese, Duke of Parma. By the early 1590s he had assumed a less active role in government. His daughter married Robert Cecil in 1589. His second wife died in 1592. In 1596, he wa
2 CONC s named Lord Chamberlain on the death of Baron Hunsdon; he died in March 1597.
2 CONT *References
2 CONT *1.^ Cobham, William in Venn, J. & J. A., Alumni Cantabrigienses, Cambridge University Press, 10 vols, 1922–1958.
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Brooke,_10th_Baron_Cobham
2 CONT *____________
2 CONT *''''''William BROOKE (5° B. Cobham)
2 CONT *Born: ABT 1526 / 1 Nov 1527, Cobham, Kent, England
2 CONT *Died: 6 Mar 1596/7
2 CONT *Buried: 5 Apr 1597, Cobham, Kent, England
2 CONT *Notes: See his Biography.
2 CONT *Father: George BROOKE (4° B. Cobham)
2 CONT *Mother: Anne BRAY (B. Cobham)
2 CONT *Married 1: Dorothy NEVILLE 4 Jun 1535 / 1550, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, Wales
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Dorothy BROOKE (Maid of Honour)
2 CONT **2. Frances BROOKE
2 CONT *''''''Married 2: Frances NEWTON (B. Cobham) (b. 1539 - d. 17 Oct 1592) (dau. of Sir John Newton of Hawtrey and Margaret Poyntz) 25 Feb 1559/60, Westminster Palace, Westminster, Middlesex, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **3. Maximilian BROOKE
2 CONT **4. Frances BROOKE (B. Stourton of Stourton)
2 CONT **5. Elizabeth BROOKE (C. Salisbury)
2 CONT **6. Henry BROOKE (6° B. Cobham)
2 CONT **7. William BROOKE (b. 11 Dec 1565)
2 CONT **8. Margaret BROOKE
2 CONT **9. George BROOKE
2 CONT *http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BROOKE1.htm#William BROOKE (5° B. Cobham)
2 CONT *________
2 CONT *''''''Frances Newton1
2 CONT *''''''F, b. circa 1539, d. 17 October 1592
2 CONT *Father Sir John Newton2 b. c 1510, d. 1568 
2 CONT *Mother Margaret Poyntz2 b. c 1511 
2 CONT *''''''     Frances Newton was born circa 1539 at of East Harptree, Somersetshire, England.1 She married Sir William Brooke, 10th Lord Cobham, son of Sir George Brooke, 9th Lord Cobham and Anne Bray, on 25 February 1560 at Westmin
2 CONC ster Palace, London, Middlesex, England.1 Frances Newton died on 17 October 1592 at Cobham, Kent, England.1
2 CONT *''''''Family Sir William Brooke, 10th Lord Cobham b. 1 Nov 1527, d. 6 Mar 1597 
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. III, p. 348-349.
2 CONT *2.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. III, p. 348.
2 CONT *http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2837.htm#i85249
2 CONT *_____________
