1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_de_Bourbon,_comte_de_Soissons '''Wikipedia....''' - English], [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_de_Bourbon_%281566-1612%29 '''Wikipedia....''' - Français]
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fichier:Blandy_les_tours_chateau_pano.jpg birth place - '''Castel de Blandy-les Tours''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles, Count of Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Charles de Bourbon, comte de Soissons)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait of Charles de Bourbon, copper engraving by Dominicus Custos, c. 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles de Bourbon (3 November 1566 – 1 November 1612) was a French prince du sang and military commander during the struggles over religion and the throne in late 16th century France. A first cousin of King Henry IV of France, he
2 CONC  was the son of the Huguenot leader Louis I de Bourbon, prince de Condé and his second wife, Françoise d'Orléans-Longueville (5 April 1549-1601).[1][2] He gave his name to the Hotel de Soissons after his title Count of Soissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Nogent-le-Rotrou, Soissons joined the Catholic League during the French Wars of Religion despite his older half-brothers' Protestant affiliations. He left the royal court disenchanted soon thereafter however, and was won o
2 CONC ver to the cause of the anti-Guise malcontents by Henry, who would himself convert soon enough to Catholicism for the sake of the French crown.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles fought at the battle of Coutras in 1587, attended the Estates General at Blois in 1588, fought back the League's forces at the battle of Saint Symphorien in 1589, was taken prisoner at Château-Giron and, escaping from Nant
2 CONC es, joined forces with Henry at Dieppe. After the battle of Ivry he led the king's cavalry in besieging Paris in 1590, and proved his worth at the sieges of Chartres in 1591 and of Rouen in 1592. Although he briefly joined in the 
2 CONC scheme of his brother Charles, Cardinal de Bourbon, to form a third party in the kingdom, he attended Henry's coronation in 1594. He fought loyally at the successful siege of Laon. Peace having been concluded with Spain, he comman
2 CONC ded troops in the war in Savoy in 1600.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had been inducted into the Order of the Holy Spirit in 1585 by Henry III. Henry IV made him Grand mâitre of the royal household and governor of the province of Brittany in 1589. In 1602 he was made governor of the Dauphiné, and
2 CONC  of Normandy in 1610, in which year he was also present at the coronation of Louis XIII.[3] After Henry's death later that year, Soissons opposed the policies of his widow, the queen regent Marie de Médicis. In 1612 Samuel de Cham
2 CONC plain convinced Charles to obtain the office of Lieutenant-General from King Louis XIII, which he did.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Bourbons obtained the French crown and the Princes de Condé and their heirs apparent (by right of their rank as premier princes du sang[citation needed]) became known, respectively, as Monsieur le prince and Monsieur le 
2 CONC duc, Charles came to be styled Monsieur le comte at court.[4] That honorific was borne also by his son Louis and, subsequently, by the Savoy counts of Soissons who inherited the countship from Charles's daughter, Marie, princesse 
2 CONC de Carignan, even though they ranked as princes étrangers in France rather than as princes du sang.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT As the youngest son of a cadet branch of the royal dynasty, Louis could not expect a large patrimony, but was allotted the countship of Soissons from among the Bourbon estates inherited from his paternal great-grandmother, Marie d
2 CONC e Luxembourg.[citation needed] He also obtained the countship of Dreux and the seigneuries of Châtel-Chinon, Noyers, Baugé, and Blandy. In 1601 Charles wed Anne de Montafié (1577–1644) who, although not of royal blood, brought to 
2 CONC the Bourbon-Soissons her father's countship of Montafié in Piedmont, as well as her mother's seigneuries of Bonnétable and Lucé.[3] Of their five children, three survived childhood:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de Bourbon, comte de Soissons 1604-1641
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louise de Bourbon 1603-1637 duchesse de Longueville
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marie de Bourbon, Countess of Soissons 1607-1692 princesse de Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles's illegitimate daughters by Anne-Marie Bohier, daughter of Antoine, seigneur de la Rochebourdet, took the veil: Charlotte, bâtarde de Soissons (d.1626), became abbess of Fontevrault and Catherine, bâtarde de Soissons (d.16
2 CONC 51), became abbess of Perrigne in Maine.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles de Soissons died at Blandy 1 November 1612, of puerperal fever according to Père Anselme, and was buried in the Soissons' family tomb in the charterhouse of Gaillon, where his wife and son would also be buried[6] (The Char
2 CONC treuse de Bourbon-lèz-Gaillon, built in 1562 one km from the Chateau de Gaillon by Charles, Cardinal de Bourbon, who was buried there, was sold during the French Revolution and demolished in 1834).[7]
2 CONT
