0 @N6000000002909004167@ NOTE Jacquetta of Luxembourg (1415/1416 – May 30, 1472) was the elder daughter of Peter I, Count of St Pol, Conversano and Brienne and his wife Margaret de Baux (Margherita del Balzo of Andria).
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and ancestry
1 CONT Her father Peter I, comte de St-Pol was a hereditary Count of Brienne from 1397 to his death in 1433.
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter had succeeded his father John, Lord of Beauvoir and mother Marguerite of Enghien. They had co-reigned as Count and Countess of Brienne from 1394 to her death in 1397.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was a fourth-generation descendant of Waleran I of Luxembourg, Lord of Ligny, second son of Henry V of Luxembourg and Margaret of Bar. This cadet line of the House of Luxembourg reigned in Ligny-en-Barrois.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her mother Margaret de Baux was a daughter of Francois de Baux, Duke of Andria and Sueva Orsini. Sueva was a daughter of Nicola Orsini, Count of Nola (August 27, 1331 - February 14, 1399) and Jeanne de Sabrano.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nicola Orsini was a son of Roberto Orsini, Count of Nola (1295-1345) and Sibilla del Balzo. Sibilla was a daughter of Hugh de Baux, Great Seneschal of the Kingdom of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roberto Orsini was a son of Romano Orsini, Royal Vicar of Rome and Anastasia de Montfort. Anastasia was the oldest daughter and heiress of Guy de Montfort, Count of Nola and Margherita Aldobrandeschi.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy de Montfort was a son of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester and Eleanor of England. Eleanor was the youngest child of John of England and his Queen consort Isabella of Angoulême.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jacquetta herself was an eighth-generation descendant of John and thus distantly related to the Kings of England descending from him.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] First marriage
1 CONT On April 22, 1433 at 17 years of age, Jacquetta married John of Lancaster, 1st Duke of Bedford at Therouenne. The Duke was a son of King Henry IV of England and Mary de Bohun.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jacquetta was a cousin of Sigismund of Luxembourg, the reigning Holy Roman Emperor, and King of Bohemia and Hungary. The marriage was meant to strengthen the ties of the Kingdom of England with the Holy Roman Empire and to increas
1 CONC e English influence in the affairs of Continental Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage was childless and the Duke died on the night of September 14/15, 1435 at Rouen. There are famous events associated with this first marriage. Jacquetta's uncle, John II of Luxembourg, Count of Ligny, betrayed Joan of A
1 CONC rc and arrested her.[citation needed] He brought Joan in chains to Rouen castle, seat of the Duke of Bedford. During Joan's two year long trial there, she was attacked by a drunken English lord who, according to rumour, had taken 
1 CONC her maidenhood. Joan had to be examined in order to prove this rumour false.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Second marriage
1 CONT Sir Richard Woodville, son of Sir Richard Wydevill who had served as the late Duke's chamberlain, was commissioned by Henry VI of England to bring the young widow to England. During the rough journey, the couple fell in love and m
1 CONC arried in secret (before March 23, 1436/1437), without seeking the king's permission. Enraged, Henry VI refused to see them but was mollified by the payment of a fine.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the mid-1440s, the Woodvilles were in ascendancy. Queen Margaret influenced her husband Henry VI to create Richard Woodville 1st Earl Rivers in 1448. Jacquetta was related to both the Queen and the King. Her sister, Isabelle de
1 CONC  Saint Pol, married the brother of Queen Margaret, while Jacquetta was herself the erstwhile widow of the uncle of Henry VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT As royalty, she outranked all ladies at Court with the exception of the Queen herself. As a personal favourite and close relative of the Queen, she also enjoyed special privileges and influence at court. Happily married to the lov
1 CONC e of her life, Jacquetta bore Richard sixteen children, among them Elizabeth Woodville who was to become the wife of King Edward IV of England, and mother of Elizabeth of York (in her turn mother of King Henry VIII, thus making Ja
1 CONC cquetta his great-grandmother).
1 CONT
1 CONT Jacquetta weathered two accusations of witchcraft during her second marriage,[citation needed] once by the mob that illegally beheaded her second husband and once when a little leaden figure of a man of arms "about the size of a t
1 CONC humb" bound up in wire was discovered among her personal effects.[citation needed] She was acquitted by her son-in-law, King Edward IV. However, these instances were recalled and cited after her death when Richard III ordered Parl
1 CONC iament in 1483 to attaint her daughter, the widowed Queen Elizabeth Woodville, for witchcraft.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Wars of the Roses
1 CONT The death of her son-in-law Sir John Grey (Elizabeth's husband) in the Second Battle of St Albans (February 22, 1461) against King Edward IV brought out the strong calculating and manipulative mind in Jacquetta. Following her moth
1 CONC er's directives, in 1464, Elizabeth (with her two minor sons) accosted Edward (out on a hunt) at Whittlebury Forest near the family home and pleaded with the King to return the confiscated estates of her husband to her sons. Thoro
1 CONC ughly bewitched by her beauty, Edward offered to make her his mistress, but she held out for marriage. A desperate Edward married Elizabeth in secret, but the marriage was not disclosed as it would mean difficulty for the House of
1 CONC  York. Once it became common knowledge, however, the alliance displeased Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick, the King's most trusted ally, and his friends.
1 CONT
1 CONT With Elizabeth as Queen of England, Jacquetta managed to find rich and influential spouses for all her children and helped her grandchildren achieve high posts. She arranged for her 20-year-old son, John Woodville, to marry the wi
1 CONC dowed and very rich dowager Duchess of Norfolk, Catherine Neville. The bride was at least forty years older than the groom at the time of the wedding. The marriage caused a furore and earned the Woodvilles considerable unpopularit
1 CONC y. Catherine Neville's son, John de Mowbray, 3rd Duke of Norfolk, especially, turned against the Queen and her family and vowed vengeance against the Yorkist allies for the slur on his family honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sadly, the Woodvilles' luck soon ran out. The Lancastrian side (on which the Woodvilles found themselves) was the losing side in the War of the Roses. In 1466, Richard Woodville was captured by Warwick and executed subsequently in
1 CONC  1469. A broken hearted Jacquetta survived her beloved husband by six years and died in 1472, at about 56 years of age.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jacquetta was as influential in death as she was in life. She is credited with being the ancestress of most present day European monarchs.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children of Jacquetta de Luxembourg by Richard Woodville[1]
1 CONT Elizabeth Woodville (b. c. 1437, d. 1492), Queen consort of Edward IV of England. 
1 CONT Anne Woodville (b. c. 1438, d. 1489). Married firstly William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier and secondly George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent. 
1 CONT Anthony Woodville, 2nd Earl Rivers (b. 1442, d. 1483). 
1 CONT Mary Woodville (b. 1443, d. 1481). Married William Herbert, Earl of Pembroke. 
1 CONT Jacquetta Woodville (b. 1444, d. 1509). Married John IX Lestrange, 8th Lord Strange. 
1 CONT John Woodville (b. 1445, executed 2 August 1469). Married Catherine Neville, Dowager Duchess of Norfolk. 
1 CONT Lionel Woodville (b. 1447, d. 1485), Bishop of Salisbury. 
1 CONT Catherine Woodville (b. 1448, d. c. 1513). Married firstly Henry Stafford, Duke of Buckingham and secondly Jasper Tudor. 
1 CONT Martha Woodville (b. 1450, d. 1500). Married Sir John Bromley. 
1 CONT Eleanor Woodville (b. 1452, d. 1512). Married Sir Anthony Grey. 
1 CONT Margaret Woodville (b. 1454, d. c. 1491) married Sir Thomas FitzAlan, 17th Earl of Arundel. 
1 CONT Richard Woodville, 3rd Earl Rivers (d. 1491). 
1 CONT Edward Woodville (d. 1488). 
1 CONT Lewis Woodville (d. young). 
1 CONT Thomas Woodville. Married Anne Holland. 
1 CONT Agnes Woodville (d. 1506). Married William Dormer. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ per Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy by Alison Weir 
