1 CONT The Prince of Wales
1 CONT The Duke of York
1 CONT Prince Henry
1 CONT Royal house House of Tudor
1 CONT Father Henry VII
1 CONT Mother Elizabeth of York
1 CONT Born 28 June 1491(1491-06-28)
1 CONT Greenwich Palace, Greenwich
1 CONT Died 28 January 1547 (aged 55)
1 CONT Palace of Whitehall, London
1 CONT Burial St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VIII (28 June 1491 – 28 January 1547) was King of England and Lord of Ireland, later King of Ireland and claimant to the Kingdom of France, from 21 April 1509 until his death. Henry was the second monarch of the House of Tud
1 CONC or, succeeding his father, Henry VII.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VIII was a significant figure in the history of the English monarchy. Although in the first parts of his reign he energetically suppressed the Reformation of the Anglican Church which had been building steam since John Wycli
1 CONC ffe of the fourteenth century, he is more often known for his ecclesiastical struggles with Rome. These struggles ultimately led to him separating the Anglican Church from Roman authority, the Dissolution of the Monasteries, and e
1 CONC stablishing the English monarch as the Supreme Head of the Church of England. Although some claim he became a Protestant on his death-bed, he advocated Catholic ceremony and doctrine throughout his life; royal backing of the Engli
1 CONC sh Reformation was left to his heirs, Edward VI and Elizabeth I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry also oversaw the legal union of England and Wales (see Laws in Wales Acts 1535–1542). He is noted in popular culture for being married six times.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early years (1491-1509)
1 CONT           o 1.1 Death of Arthur
1 CONT     * 2 France and the Habsburgs (1509-1525)
1 CONT           o 2.1 Mistresses
1 CONT     * 3 Henry's "great matter" (1525-1533)
1 CONT           o 3.1 Second marriage
1 CONT     * 4 English Reformation (1533-1540)
1 CONT           o 4.1 Personal troubles
1 CONT           o 4.2 Execution of Anne Boleyn
1 CONT           o 4.3 Birth of a prince
1 CONT     * 5 Final years (1540-1547)
1 CONT           o 5.1 Death and succession
1 CONT     * 6 Legacy
1 CONT           o 6.1 Church of England
1 CONT           o 6.2 English navy
1 CONT     * 7 Style and arms
1 CONT     * 8 Ancestry
1 CONT     * 9 Marriage and issue
1 CONT     * 10 See also
1 CONT     * 11 Notes
1 CONT     * 12 References
1 CONT     * 13 Further reading
1 CONT     * 14 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT Early years (1491-1509)
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Greenwich Palace, Henry VIII was the third child of Henry VII and Elizabeth of York.[1] Of the young Henry's six siblings, only three — Arthur (the Prince of Wales), Margaret, and Mary — survived infancy. In 1493, Henry wa
1 CONC s appointed Constable of Dover Castle and Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports. In 1494, he was created Duke of York. He was subsequently appointed Earl Marshal of England and Lord Lieutenant of Ireland. Henry was given a first-rate ed
1 CONC ucation from leading tutors, becoming fluent in Latin, French, and Spanish.[2] As it was expected that the throne would pass to Prince Arthur, Henry's older brother, Henry was prepared for a career in the Church.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT Death of Arthur
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1502, however, Arthur suddenly died of an unknown disease, perhaps tuberculosis, and Henry was thrust into all the duties of his late brother, becoming Prince of Wales. Henry's father renewed his efforts to seal an alliance bet
1 CONC ween England and Spain via marriage. In place of the dead Arthur, Spain was offered Henry in marriage to Prince Arthur's widow, Catherine of Aragon, the youngest surviving child of King Ferdinand II of Aragon and Queen Isabella I 
1 CONC of Castile.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT In order for the new Prince of Wales to marry his brother's widow, a dispensation from the Pope was normally required to overrule the impediment of affinity. Catherine swore that her marriage to Prince Arthur had been unconsummate
1 CONC d. Still, both the English and Spanish parties agreed that an additional papal dispensation of affinity would be prudent to remove all doubt regarding the legitimacy of the marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to the impatience of Catherine's mother, Queen Isabella, the Pope granted his dispensation in the form of a Papal bull. Thus, fourteen months after her young husband's death, Catherine found herself betrothed to his brother, t
1 CONC he new Prince of Wales. By 1505, however, Henry VII lost interest in an alliance with Spain, and the younger Henry declared that his betrothal had been arranged without his consent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Continued diplomatic maneuvering over the fate of the proposed marriage lingered until the death of Henry VII in 1509. Only 17 years old, Henry married his brother's widow, Catherine, on 11 June 1509, and on 24 June 1509, the two 
1 CONC were crowned at Westminster Abbey. Two days later, he arrested his father's two most unpopular ministers, Sir Richard Empson and Edmund Dudley. They were groundlessly charged with high treason and in 1510, the king had them execut
1 CONC ed. This was to become Henry's primary tactic for dealing with those who stood in his way.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT France and the Habsburgs (1509-1525)
1 CONT Eighteen year-old Henry VIII after his coronation in 1509.
1 CONT Eighteen year-old Henry VIII after his coronation in 1509.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was a Renaissance Man and his court was a centre of scholarly and artistic innovation and glamourous excess. He was an accomplished musician, author, and poet. His best known musical composition is Pastime with Good Company 
1 CONC or The Kynges Ballade. He was also known to have been an avid gambler and dice player. He excelled at sports, especially jousting, hunting, and tennis. The king was also known for his strong dedication to the Christian faith.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1511, Pope Julius II proclaimed a Holy League against France. This new alliance rapidly grew to include not only Spain and the Holy Roman Empire, but also England. Henry decided to use the occasion as an excuse to expand his ho
1 CONC ldings in northern France. He concluded the Treaty of Westminster, a pledge of mutual aid against the French with Spain, in November 1511 and prepared for involvement in the War of the League of Cambrai. In 1513, Henry invaded Fra
1 CONC nce and his troops defeated a French army at the Battle of the Spurs. James IV of Scotland invaded England at the behest of Louis,[7] but he failed to draw Henry's attention from France. The Scots' disastrous defeat at the Battle 
1 CONC of Flodden Field took place on 9 September 1513. Among the dead were the Scottish King and the battle ended Scotland's brief involvement in the war.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mistresses
1 CONT
1 CONT Historians are sure of the names of only two of Henry's mistresses: Elizabeth Blount (usually referred to as Bessie) and Mary Boleyn (Anne's sister). Elizabeth Blount gave birth to Henry's illegitimate son, Henry FitzRoy. The youn
1 CONC g boy was made Duke of Richmond in June 1525 in what some thought was one step on the path to legitimatizing him. In 1533, he married Mary Howard, Anne Boleyn's first cousin, but died only three years later without any successors.
1 CONC  At the time of FitzRoy's death, the king was trying to get a law passed that would allow his otherwise illegitimate son to become king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary Boleyn is believed to be the elder sister of Anne Boleyn. She was requested to the Queen's court by the king in hopes to seduce her, and was his mistress at some point between 1519 and 1526. Contemporary rumors stated that Ma
1 CONC ry Boleyn's two children, Catherine and Henry were fathered by Henry, but this has never been proven.
1 CONT The Six Wives of
1 CONT King Henry VIII
1 CONT Catherine of Aragon
1 CONT Anne Boleyn
1 CONT Jane Seymour
1 CONT Anne of Cleves
1 CONT Catherine Howard
1 CONT Catherine Parr
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's "great matter" (1525-1533)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1525, Henry's impatience with what he perceived to be Catherine's inability to produce the desired heir increased when he became attracted to a charismatic young woman in the Queen's entourage, Anne Boleyn. Henry became enamour
1 CONC ed with her and began his pursuit.[8] Boleyn resisted his attempts to seduce her and she refused to become his mistress, as her sister Mary Boleyn had. Henry was all the more attracted to her because of this refusal and he pursued
1 CONC  her relentlessly. Boleyn continued to reject the King’s initial advances by saying, “I beseech your highness most earnestly to desist, and to this my answer in good part. I would rather lose my life than my honesty.”[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT It is possible that the idea of annulment had suggested itself to the King much earlier than this, and it is highly probable that it was motivated by his desire for a male heir. Before Henry's father Henry VII ascended the throne,
1 CONC  England had been beset by civil warfare over rival claims to the English crown and Henry wanted to avoid a similar uncertainty over the succession. The King had no sons due to the death in infancy of all Catherine of Aragon's chi
1 CONC ldren except his daughter Mary.[10] Anne saw her opportunity in Henry's infatuation and determined that she would only yield as his acknowledged queen.[11]
1 CONT Catherine of Aragon, first wife of Henry VIII.
1 CONT Catherine of Aragon, first wife of Henry VIII.
1 CONT
1 CONT It soon became the one absorbing object of the King's desires to secure an annulment from Catherine.[12] Henry set his hopes upon a direct appeal to the Holy See, acting in this independently of Thomas Cardinal Wolsey, to whom he 
1 CONC at first communicated nothing of his plans so far as they related to Anne. William Knight, the king's secretary, was sent to Pope Clement VII to sue for the annulment of his marriage with Catherine. The grounds were that the dispe
1 CONC nsing bull of Pope Julius II was obtained by false pretenses, because Catherine's brief marriage to the sickly Arthur had in fact been consummated. Henry also petitioned, in the event of his becoming free, a dispensation to contra
1 CONC ct a new marriage with any woman even in the first degree of affinity, whether the affinity was contracted by lawful or unlawful connection. This clearly had reference to Anne.[11]
1 CONT
1 CONT As the pope was at that time the prisoner of Emperor Charles V, Knight had some difficulty in obtaining access to him. In the end the king's envoy had to return without accomplishing much, though the conditional dispensation for a
1 CONC  new marriage was granted. Henry had now no choice but to put his great matter into the hands of Thomas Wolsey, and Wolsey did all he could to secure a decision in the King's favour.[11] How far the pope was influenced by Charles 
1 CONC V in his resistance, it is difficult to say, but it is clear Henry saw that the Pope was unlikely to give him an annulment from the Emperor's aunt.[13] The pope forbade Henry to proceed to a new marriage before a decision was give
1 CONC n in Rome. Convinced that he was treacherous, Anne Boleyn maintained pressure until Wolsey was dismissed from public office in 1529. After being dismissed, the cardinal begged her to help him return to power, but she refused. He t
1 CONC hen began a secret plot to have Anne forced into exile and began communication with Queen Catherine and the Pope, to that end. When this was discovered, Henry ordered Wolsey's arrest and had it not been for his death from a termin
1 CONC al illness in 1530, he might have been executed for treason.[14] His replacement, Sir Thomas More, initially cooperated with the king's new policy, denouncing Wolsey in Parliament and proclaiming the opinion of the theologians at 
1 CONC Oxford and Cambridge that the marriage of Henry to Catherine had been unlawful. As Henry began to deny the authority of the Pope, More's qualms grew.
1 CONT
1 CONT A year later, Queen Catherine was banished from court and her old rooms were given to Anne. With Wolsey gone, Anne now had considerable power over government appointments and political matters. When Archbishop of Canterbury Willia
1 CONC m Warham died, Anne had the Boleyn family's chaplain, Thomas Cranmer, appointed to the vacant position. Through the intervention of the King of France, this was conceded by Rome, the pallium being granted to him by Clement.[15]
1 CONT
1 CONT The breaking of the power of Rome in England proceeded little by little. In 1532, a lawyer who was a supporter of Anne, Thomas Cromwell, brought before Parliament a number of acts including the Supplication against the Ordinaries 
1 CONC and the Submission of the Clergy, which recognised Royal Supremacy over the church. Following these acts, Thomas More resigned as Chancellor, leaving Cromwell as Henry's chief minister.[16]
1 CONT English Royalty
1 CONT House of Tudor
