0 @N6000000002322281987@ NOTE Edward was Henry's only "legitimate" son. He ruled from 1547-1553 and was the
1 CONT last in the male line of the house of Tudor. Edward became seriously ill with
1 CONT tuberculosis in 1553, dying at Greenwich mid-year.
0 @N6000000001562928141@ NOTE Edward removed his half-sisters May and Elizabeth from succession.  The crown passed to Lady Jane Grey 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward VI, son of Henry VIII and Jane Seymour, was born in 1537. He ascended the throne at age nine, upon the death of his father. He was betrothed to his cousin, Mary Queen of Scots, but deteriorating English-Scot relations prohi
1 CONC bited their marriage. The frail, Protestant boy died of consumption at age sixteen having never married. 
1 CONT Edward's reign was beset by problems from the onset. Ascending the throne while stillin his minority presented a backdrop for factional in fighting and power plays. Henry VIII, in his last days, sought to eliminate this potential 
1 CONC problem by decreeing that a Council of Regency would govern until the child came of age, but Edward Seymour (Edward VI's uncle) gained the upper hand. The Council offered Seymour the Protectorship of the realm and the Dukedom of S
1 CONC omerset; he genuinely cared for both the boy and the realm, but used the Protectorship, as well as Edward's religious radicalism, to further his Protestant interests. The Book of Common Prayer, the eloquent work of Archbishop Thom
1 CONC as Cranmer, was instituted in 1549 as a handbook to the new style of worship that skated controversial issues in an effort to pacify Catholics. Henrician treason and heresy laws were repealed, transforming England into a haven for
1 CONC  continental heretics. Catholics were pleased with the softer version of Protestantism, but radical Protestants clamored for further reforms, adding to the ever-present factional discord. 
1 CONT Economic hardship plagued England during Edward's rule and foreign relations were in a state of disarray. The new faith and the dissolution of the monasteries left a considerable amount of ecclesiastical officials out of work, at 
1 CONC a time when unemployment soared; enclosure of monastic lands deprived many peasants of their means of subsistence. The coinage lost value as new coins were minted from inferior metals, as specie from the New World flooded English 
1 CONC markets. A French/Scottish alliance threatened England, prompting Somerset to invade Scotland, where Scottish forces were trounced at Pinkie. Then general unrest and factional maneuvering proved Somerset's undoing; he was executed
1 CONC  in September 1552. Thus began one of the most corrupt eras of English political history. 
1 CONT The author of this corruption was the Earl of Warwick, John Dudley. Dudley was an ambitious political survivor driven by the desire to become the largest landowner in England. Dudley coerced Edward by claiming that the boy had rea
1 CONC ched manhood on his 12th birthday and was now ready to rule; Dudley also held Edward's purse strings. Dudley was created Duke of Northumberland and virtually ruled England, although he had no official title. The Council, under his
1 CONC  leadership, systematically confiscated church territories, as the recent wave of radical Protestantism seemed a logical, and justifiable, continuation of Henrician reform. Northumberland's ambitions grew in proportion to his gain
1 CONC s of power: he desperately sought to connect himself to the royal family. 
1 CONT Northumberland was given the opportunity to indulge in king making - the practice by which an influential noble named the next successor, such as Richard Neville during the Wars of the Roses - when Edward was diagnosed with consum
1 CONC ption in January 1553. Henry VIII named the line of succession in his will;next in line after Edward were his sisters Mary and Elizabeth, followed by the descendants of Henry's sister, Mary: Frances Grey and her children. Northumb
1 CONC erland convinced Edward that his Catholic sister, Mary, would ruin the Protestant reforms enacted throughout the reign; in actuality, he knew Mary would restore Catholicism and return the confiscated Church territories which were 
1 CONC making the Council very rich. Northumberland's appeal to Edward's radicalism worked as intended: the dying lad declared his sisters to be bastards and passed the succession to Frances Grey's daughter, Lady Jane Grey, one of the bo
1 CONC y's only true friends. Northumberland impelled the Greys to consent to a marriage between his son, Guildford and Lady Jane. Edward died on July 6, 1553, leaving a disputed succession. Jane, against her wishes, was declared queen b
1 CONC y the Council. Mary retreated to Framlingham in Suffolk and claimed the throne. Northumberland took an army to capture Mary, but bungled the escapade. The Council abandoned Northumberland as Mary collected popular support and rode
1 CONC  triumphantly into London. Jane after a reign of only nine days, was imprisoned in the Tower of London until her 1554 execution at the hands of her cousin Mary. 
1 CONT Edward was a highly intellectual and pious lad who fell prey to the machinations of his powerful Council of Regency. His frailty led to an early death. Had he lived into manhood, he potentially could have become one of England's g
1 CONC reatest kings. Jane Austen wrote, "This Man was on the whole of a very amiable character...", to which Beckett added, " as docile as a lamb, if indeed his gentleness did not amount to absolute sheepishness."
0 @N6000000002176641546@ NOTE Edward was Henry's only "legitimate" son. He ruled from 1547-1553 and was the
1 CONT last in the male line of the house of Tudor. Edward became seriously ill with
1 CONT tuberculosis in 1553, dying at Greenwich mid-year.
0 @N6000000001579274842@ NOTE Edward removed his half-sisters May and Elizabeth from succession.  The crown passed to Lady Jane Grey
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward VI, son of Henry VIII and Jane Seymour, was born in 1537. He ascended the throne at age nine, upon the death of his father. He was betrothed to his cousin, Mary Queen of Scots, but deteriorating English-Scot relations prohi
1 CONC bited their marriage. The frail, Protestant boy died of consumption at age sixteen having never married.
1 CONT Edward's reign was beset by problems from the onset. Ascending the throne while stillin his minority presented a backdrop for factional in fighting and power plays. Henry VIII, in his last days, sought to eliminate this potential 
1 CONC problem by decreeing that a Council of Regency would govern until the child came of age, but Edward Seymour (Edward VI's uncle) gained the upper hand. The Council offered Seymour the Protectorship of the realm and the Dukedom of S
1 CONC omerset; he genuinely cared for both the boy and the realm, but used the Protectorship, as well as Edward's religious radicalism, to further his Protestant interests. The Book of Common Prayer, the eloquent work of Archbishop Thom
1 CONC as Cranmer, was instituted in 1549 as a handbook to the new style of worship that skated controversial issues in an effort to pacify Catholics. Henrician treason and heresy laws were repealed, transforming England into a haven for
1 CONC  continental heretics. Catholics were pleased with the softer version of Protestantism, but radical Protestants clamored for further reforms, adding to the ever-present factional discord.
1 CONT Economic hardship plagued England during Edward's rule and foreign relations were in a state of disarray. The new faith and the dissolution of the monasteries left a considerable amount of ecclesiastical officials out of work, at 
1 CONC a time when unemployment soared; enclosure of monastic lands deprived many peasants of their means of subsistence. The coinage lost value as new coins were minted from inferior metals, as specie from the New World flooded English 
1 CONC markets. A French/Scottish alliance threatened England, prompting Somerset to invade Scotland, where Scottish forces were trounced at Pinkie. Then general unrest and factional maneuvering proved Somerset's undoing; he was executed
1 CONC  in September 1552. Thus began one of the most corrupt eras of English political history.
1 CONT The author of this corruption was the Earl of Warwick, John Dudley. Dudley was an ambitious political survivor driven by the desire to become the largest landowner in England. Dudley coerced Edward by claiming that the boy had rea
1 CONC ched manhood on his 12th birthday and was now ready to rule; Dudley also held Edward's purse strings. Dudley was created Duke of Northumberland and virtually ruled England, although he had no official title. The Council, under his
1 CONC  leadership, systematically confiscated church territories, as the recent wave of radical Protestantism seemed a logical, and justifiable, continuation of Henrician reform. Northumberland's ambitions grew in proportion to his gain
1 CONC s of power: he desperately sought to connect himself to the royal family.
1 CONT Northumberland was given the opportunity to indulge in king making - the practice by which an influential noble named the next successor, such as Richard Neville during the Wars of the Roses - when Edward was diagnosed with consum
1 CONC ption in January 1553. Henry VIII named the line of succession in his will;next in line after Edward were his sisters Mary and Elizabeth, followed by the descendants of Henry's sister, Mary: Frances Grey and her children. Northumb
1 CONC erland convinced Edward that his Catholic sister, Mary, would ruin the Protestant reforms enacted throughout the reign; in actuality, he knew Mary would restore Catholicism and return the confiscated Church territories which were 
1 CONC making the Council very rich. Northumberland's appeal to Edward's radicalism worked as intended: the dying lad declared his sisters to be bastards and passed the succession to Frances Grey's daughter, Lady Jane Grey, one of the bo
1 CONC y's only true friends. Northumberland impelled the Greys to consent to a marriage between his son, Guildford and Lady Jane. Edward died on July 6, 1553, leaving a disputed succession. Jane, against her wishes, was declared queen b
1 CONC y the Council. Mary retreated to Framlingham in Suffolk and claimed the throne. Northumberland took an army to capture Mary, but bungled the escapade. The Council abandoned Northumberland as Mary collected popular support and rode
1 CONC  triumphantly into London. Jane after a reign of only nine days, was imprisoned in the Tower of London until her 1554 execution at the hands of her cousin Mary.
1 CONT Edward was a highly intellectual and pious lad who fell prey to the machinations of his powerful Council of Regency. His frailty led to an early death. Had he lived into manhood, he potentially could have become one of England's g
1 CONC reatest kings. Jane Austen wrote, "This Man was on the whole of a very amiable character...", to which Beckett added, " as docile as a lamb, if indeed his gentleness did not amount to absolute sheepishness."
0 @N6000000002188342462@ NOTE Edward VI of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT "Edward Tudor" redirects here. For the impostor who masqueraded as Edward Plantagenet, 17th Earl of Warwick, and as such claimed the throne as "Edward VI" in 1487, see Lambert Simnel.
1 CONT This article needs additional citations for verification.
1 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (March 2008)
1 CONT Edward VI
1 CONT King of England and Ireland (more...)
1 CONT Edward as Prince of Wales, Flemish School
1 CONT Edward as Prince of Wales, Flemish School[1]
1 CONT Reign 28 January 1547 – 6 July 1553
1 CONT Coronation 20 February 1547
1 CONT Predecessor Henry VIII
1 CONT Regent Lord Northumberland (1549–1553)
1 CONT Lord Somerset (1547–1549)
1 CONT and Council
1 CONT Successor Jane de facto;
1 CONT Mary I de jure
1 CONT Detail
1 CONT Titles and styles
1 CONT HM The King
1 CONT The Prince of Wales
1 CONT The Duke of Cornwall
1 CONT Royal house House of Tudor
1 CONT Father Henry VIII
1 CONT Mother Jane Seymour
1 CONT Born 12 October 1537(1537-10-12)
1 CONT Hampton Court Palace, Richmond upon Thames
1 CONT Died 6 July 1553 (aged 15)
1 CONT Palace of Placentia, Greenwich
1 CONT Burial 9 August 1553
1 CONT Henry VII Lady Chapel, Westminster Abbey
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward VI (12 October 1537 – 6 July 1553) became King of England and Ireland, on 28 January 1547, and was crowned on 20 February, at ten years of age. He also carried the English claim to the French throne, but he did not rule Fra
1 CONC nce. Edward, the son of Henry VIII and Jane Seymour, was the third monarch of the Tudor dynasty and England's first born Protestant ruler. Edward's entire rule was mediated through a council of regency, as he never reached maturit
1 CONC y. The council was first led by his uncle, Edward Seymour, 1st Duke of Somerset (1547–1549), and then by John Dudley, 1st Duke of Northumberland (1549–1553).
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Henry VIII had severed the link between the English church and Rome, it was during Edward's reign that Protestantism was established for the first time in England, with Thomas Cranmer, the Archbishop of Canterbury, implem
1 CONC enting the Book of Common Prayer. Edward's reign was marked by increasingly harsh Protestant reforms, the loss of control of Scotland, and an economic downturn. A period of social unrest begun earlier intensified during his rule, 
1 CONC and conflicts with the French increased.
1 CONT
1 CONT When it became clear that Edward's life was to be a short one, the Device to Alter the Succession was drafted. This made Lady Jane Grey, Edward's solidly Protestant cousin, the first in line of succession to the throne by excludin
1 CONC g his two half sisters, the devout Catholic Mary and moderate Protestant Elizabeth. Following Edward's death at the age of fifteen, a disputed succession reopened the religious conflicts. Lady Jane was Queen for only nine days, du
1 CONC ring that time reigning in name only, before she was replaced by Mary. Queen Mary then sought to undo many of Edward's Protestant reforms with the Marian Repeal Acts in her first two Parliaments.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
